al is quite specific and narrow in scope (just the verification identity recording), which makes it easier to prototype quickly.\n- The risk is scope creep: \"dedicated digital authentication system\" could balloon into building a full IAM solution when the regulatory requirement is really just \"we need to know who verified what and when.\"\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. The proposal positions this as a \"dedicated digital authentication system\" which over-engineers what Article 12(3)(d) actually requires. The regulation says \"identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results\" - you need to know WHO did it, not necessarily build a new auth system.\n2. However, if we already have user accounts/sessions (which any production AI system should), this is just an audit event: \"User X verified output Y at time Z.\" That's trivially implementable.\n3. The proposal correctly identifies its regulatory anchor (Annex III(1a), Article 12(3)(d)), which shows it's targeted.\n4. From a prototyping speed perspective, this is low-complexity if scoped correctly. It's essentially an audit log entry tied to a verification action.\n\n**Decision factors:**\n- Technically viable: Yes, absolutely. It's standard software engineering (audit logging + session tracking).\n- Speed of prototyping: High - this could be a 1-2 week prototype if we have existing auth infrastructure. Could be longer if \"dedicated digital authentication system\" means building new auth from scratch, but that framing seems like over-scoping.\n- Quality concern: The \"mandatory sign-off layer\" in the UI needs careful UX design to not create friction that users bypass or resent. But that's a manageable design challenge.\n\nI think this is a solid, well-scoped proposal. It targets a specific regulatory requirement with a clear technical solution. From my vantage point, it's technically straightforward, fast to prototype, and addresses a real gap. The main risk is the \