umentation requirements, and (b) building an ambitious \"living\" documentation platform. The regulatory requirement is that documentation exists, is comprehensive, and is kept current. It doesn't require a real-time synced knowledge base. Building this as described would consume significant engineering resources that could go toward actual model development. However, the core idea — tying documentation to version control so it updates with each model commit — is practical and low-cost if we already have CI/CD pipelines.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: The mention of \"linked to our version control system\" is the most resource-efficient element here. If the lab already has Git, CI/CD, and some form of documentation pipeline, extending that to auto-generate or update technical documentation on each model commit is a modest incremental effort. However, the broader vision of \"real-time sync\" across \"technical specifications, training data logs, and risk assessments\" into a unified knowledge base is a much larger undertaking. Risk assessments in particular are typically human-authored documents with periodic review cycles — automating their update would be complex and arguably inappropriate (they require expert judgment).\n\n**Assessment**: The core regulatory need (keeping documentation current per Recital 71) is real and must be addressed. The proposal identifies the right problem but over-scopes the solution. A pragmatic version — tying documentation updates to version control commits, with periodic human review for risk assessments — would satisfy the regulation at a fraction of the cost. The \"real-time\" and \"dynamic\" framing suggests building infrastructure that exceeds what's needed. From a resource-allocation standpoint, I'd want to approve the *minimum viable* version (version-control-linked doc updates) but reject the full \"living documentation platform\" as over-engineered for compliance purposes.\n\nHowever, the proposal as w