ather than requiring new infrastructure builds.\n\nCons/Concerns:\n1. **Upfront engineering cost**: Building the unified library requires dedicated engineering resources up front. In a funding-constrained environment, this is a non-trivial commitment before any single project benefits. However, if there are multiple high-risk projects, it amortizes well.\n2. **\"Mandatory\" integration could slow down research teams**: Requiring researchers to integrate with a specific middleware could add friction to their workflows, potentially delaying scientific progress. But the proposal says it \"minimizes manual overhead,\" suggesting it's designed to be lightweight.\n3. **Scope risk**: Building a \"unified\" framework that works across all high-risk AI projects (which may use different architectures, training pipelines, etc.) is technically challenging and could balloon in scope/cost.\n4. **Maintenance burden**: Once built, someone must maintain this as models evolve. In a resource-constrained lab, ongoing maintenance is a real cost.\n\nOverall assessment: From the resource-allocation efficiency perspective, this is strongly attractive. The one-time build cost is offset by avoiding N separate builds across N projects. It directly addresses the regulatory requirement (Article 12) in a way that leverages existing infrastructure and frees researcher time for actual science. The main risk is upfront engineering investment and scope creep, but the modular API design mitigates this.\n\nI'd rate this quite highly from my vantage point. It's a textbook example of an efficiency play — consolidate compliance work into one reusable asset rather than fragmenting it across teams. In a lab with limited funding, avoiding redundant engineering work is exactly what I should be prioritizing. The \"mandatory middleware\" aspect ensures no project can skip compliance (which would create regulatory risk and potential rework costs later).\n\nPromise score: I'd put this around 82-85. It's very wel