s heavily on Article 12(1) (automatic recording capability) but doesn't explicitly address:\n  - Article 12(2): logging must enable traceability appropriate to intended purpose, specifically for identifying risk situations (79(1)), facilitating post-market monitoring (Article 72), and monitoring operations (Article 26(5)). The proposal mentions \"required telemetry\" but doesn't detail how it maps to these specific traceability purposes.\n  - Article 12(3): For Annex III point 1(a) systems, there are very specific minimum logging requirements: start/end dates of each use, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification (Article 14(5)). The proposal mentions \"input references\" and \"user IDs\" which partially covers this, but doesn't explicitly call out the reference database or match data.\n- However, as a compliance officer, I should note that the proposal is an architectural framework — it says \"automatically records event timestamps, input references, and user IDs across all high-risk projects.\" This seems to be a foundational layer that could be extended. The fact that it's a \"standardized software library\" suggests it can be configured per system type.\n- From market reputation: having a unified, auditable logging architecture demonstrates regulatory seriousness to customers, partners, and regulators. It's a positive signal.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a core compliance requirement. Article 12(1) is mandatory (\"shall technically allow\"). Without this infrastructure, we cannot deploy high-risk AI systems in the EU. The proposal directly addresses the most fundamental obligation. A unified approach is better than ad-hoc per-project solutions from a compliance auditability standpoint — regulators will want to see consistent logging across all our high-risk systems.\n\nThe main gap I'd note is that the proposal as stated doesn't fully articulate how it meets 12(2) traceability purposes or 12(3) specific minim