t to traceability (timestamps, input data). However:\n  - It's generic (\"all AI prototypes\") rather than tailored to specific high-risk system categories with their specific logging needs (e.g., Annex III point 1(a) systems need reference database tracking, match results, natural person identification — none of which are mentioned).\n  - It doesn't address the \"appropriate to intended purpose\" requirement in Art. 12(2).\n  - It's a one-size-fits-all approach that may not meet the minimum requirements for specific categories.\n\n**From my vantage point specifically:**\n- As a researcher focused on knowledge frontier and intellectual merit, this is essentially an engineering convenience tool. It doesn't advance research. It's necessary operational infrastructure but it's not something I would champion from a research perspective.\n- It doesn't enable new science. It doesn't deepen understanding of AI systems. It's plumbing.\n- That said, if researchers are freed from writing logging boilerplate, they have marginally more time for actual research. But that's a very thin connection to my domain.\n\n**My assessment:** This is a competent engineering proposal that addresses a real compliance need, but from the perspective of knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and broader scientific impact, it contributes essentially nothing novel. It's infrastructure, not research. I would reject it from my vantage point — not because it's bad (it seems like fine engineering), but because it doesn't advance any research agenda or open new intellectual territory.\n\nPromise score: From my perspective as a researcher focused on knowledge frontier and intellectual merit, this is very low value. It's not that I'd oppose it, but I wouldn't allocate scarce implementation resources to it when I could be working on things that actually push the science forward. Maybe a 10-15. It has zero research novelty, minimal indirect benefit to research productivity, and doesn't open any new scienti