adapting to it.\n- \"Middleware layer that intercepts inputs, outputs, and state changes\" - building this well is non-trivial engineering work. The resource commitment here is significant. But again, if we're in the high-risk AI space, we need this.\n- The proposal doesn't mention leveraging existing logging infrastructure or open-source solutions (e.g., OpenTelemetry). Building a bespoke \"immutable audit trail\" middleware from scratch might be less efficient than adapting existing tools. This is a resource-allocation concern.\n- However, the core idea - one standardized module rather than N ad-hoc implementations - is clearly the right architectural decision from an efficiency standpoint.\n\nOverall assessment: The direction is correct and necessary (Article 12 compliance is mandatory for high-risk systems). The centralized/standardized approach aligns with resource efficiency. My main concern is whether \"mandatory for ALL project deployments\" is proportionate - some research projects may not be heading toward high-risk deployment, and forcing them through a heavy middleware could slow scientific progress. Also, the proposal doesn't address whether we're building from scratch or adapting existing infrastructure (OpenTelemetry, etc.), which matters for resource efficiency.\n\nBut on balance: this is a necessary investment, the standardization approach is efficient, and without it we'd have each team reinventing logging. The risk of \"over-mandating\" can be managed in implementation scope. I think this clears my bar as something the lab needs to pursue, with the caveat that implementation should leverage existing tools where possible and perhaps phase in rather than being immediately universal for all projects regardless of stage.\n\nDecision: approve - it's a necessary compliance investment done at scale (efficient), and the standardized approach protects scientific ambition by removing per-project compliance overhead from researchers' plates.\n\nPromise score