r verifying systems \"before they are marked as deployment-ready\" — so it's not in the experimental/prototyping loop per se, but at the transition point to deployment. This is actually reasonable and doesn't impede prototyping speed directly.\n- The quality benefit is real: catching logging gaps before production deployment prevents costly rework. If a system goes live with incomplete Article 12 compliance, retrofitting logging infrastructure post-deployment is significantly more expensive and disruptive than catching it in staging.\n- The \"edge case simulation\" aspect is valuable for prototyping quality — it forces us to think about what events we need to log before they happen in production, which improves the design of our logging architecture from the start.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- Resource commitment: Building and maintaining a mirrored cluster is non-trivial infrastructure work. In an R&D lab with scarce resources, this could be a significant overhead.\n- The \"mirrored production cluster\" language suggests full parity, which may be overkill for logging validation. A lightweight simulation environment that replicates just the relevant event streams might suffice and be faster to build/maintain.\n- However, the core idea — having a pre-deployment gate specifically for Article 12 compliance — is technically sound and aligns with good engineering practice (shift-left compliance testing).\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThe proposal is technically viable and addresses a real gap in our development pipeline. The concept of shift-left compliance validation for logging is sound engineering. The main concern from my vantage point is whether the \"mirrored production cluster\" framing over-commits on infrastructure when a lighter-weight approach might achieve the same validation goals faster. But the core idea is strong and I can see how it would improve both quality (catching logging gaps early) and ultimately speed (avoiding rework post-deployment).\n\nI think this clear