ent cost upfront is significant: building a robust, secure, immutable logging infrastructure with a good API takes real engineering resources.\n- Risk of over-engineering: the framework might become rigid as new types of AI systems emerge, requiring constant maintenance.\n- The \"forces consistent logging\" language suggests it's somewhat prescriptive - this could conflict with scientific ambition if researchers need to log different types of events for different system architectures.\n- Maintenance burden: who maintains the library? It becomes a critical dependency that requires ongoing investment.\n\n**From resource-allocation efficiency perspective:**\nThis is actually a very good use of scarce resources IF the lab has multiple high-risk AI projects (or plans to). The fixed cost of building the framework is amortized across all projects. Without it, each project bears its own compliance burden. This is classic \"build once, use many\" infrastructure investment that a resource-constrained institution would benefit from.\n\nHowever, if the lab only has 1-2 high-risk projects, the overhead might not be justified yet - simpler per-project solutions might suffice initially. But given this is an R&D institution with multiple projects (implied by \"all high-risk AI projects\"), the unified approach makes sense.\n\nThe key question: does this commit scarce implementation resources wisely? I think yes - it's a focused, bounded engineering effort that produces reusable infrastructure. It doesn't require ongoing massive investment beyond maintenance. The alternative (each team building their own) is strictly more expensive in aggregate.\n\nI'd approve this. From my resource-allocation perspective, this is one of the most efficient ways to meet Article 12 requirements across multiple projects. The promise score should be high but not perfect - there are execution risks (developer burden on researchers, maintenance overhead, potential rigidity), and I want to make sure it's s