        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2869\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A unified telemetry SDK is technically feasible and addresses the core mandate of Art. 12(1)–(2) by automating event capture at the execution layer, which does reduce per-project boilerplate in steady state. However, from a prototyping-velocity standpoint, a mandatory centralized wrapper introduces an upfront infrastructure dependency that will slow initial builds on heterogeneous model stacks, and the proposal is underspecified on how it handles the highly domain-specific fields required by Art. 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) systems (e.g., reference-database identity, matched-input recording, verification-person identification), which likely demand project-level configuration that a one-size-fits-all SDK does not naturally provide.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 64\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2869 describes a \"Unified Telemetry SDK for High-Risk Systems\" — a standardized SDK providing pre-defined hooks for automatic event and lifecycle logging across all lab projects, with a wrapper around the model execution environment that captures timestamps, input hashes, and system states to a centralized database.\n\nLet me think about this from my professional vantage point:\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- A unified SDK approach is technically sound and well-established in software engineering. Wrapping the model execution environment to capture telemetry is a standard pattern (similar to what you see with OpenTelemetry, distributed tracing libraries, etc.).\n- The concept of \"pre-defined hooks\" for automatic logging is feasible — you can intercept at key points in the inference/training pipeline (input reception, preprocessing, model invocation, post-processing, output).\n- Centralized database storage is standard and viable.\n- However, there are technical challenges: different model architectures have very different execution paths. A \"wrapper around our model execution environment\" assumes a relatively uniform execution environment. If the lab works with heterogeneous stacks (different frameworks, different serving infrastructures), the SDK may need significant adaptation per project.\n- The scope is ambitious: covering \"all lab projects\" means the SDK needs to be flexible enough for diverse architectures while still providing consistent logging.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping impact:**\n- This is where I need to think carefully. My primary concern is speed and quality of prototyping.\n- PROS: If implemented well, a standardized SDK reduces per-project boilerplate code. Developers don't have to design their own logging from scratch each time. \"Compliant by default\" means less review overhead before deployment. The hooks are pre-defined, so developers just integrate the SDK rather than building custom telemetry.\n- CONS: \n  - Building the SDK itself is a significant upfront investment. During that development period, prototyping speed may actually decrease as teams either wait for the SDK or build ad-hoc solutions in parallel.\n  - A centralized database introduces a dependency — if it goes down, does logging fail? Does the system go into degraded mode? This adds operational complexity to every prototype.\n  - \"Across all lab projects\" is a very broad scope. The SDK needs to be general enough for diverse use cases but specific enough to capture what Article 12 requires (which varies by risk category). For Annex III point 1(a) systems, you need specific fields (period of each use, reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons in verification) — these are quite domain-specific and a generic SDK may not naturally cover them without project-specific configuration.\n  - The \"wrapper\" approach can add latency to inference. For high-risk systems where response time matters, every additional layer in the execution path is a concern.\n  - Centralized database: what about data volume? If you're logging every input hash and system state for every inference call at scale, storage and query performance become real engineering challenges.\n\n**Article 12 compliance fit:**\n- Article 12(1) requires \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs)\" — this directly addresses that with automatic hooks.\n- Article 12(2) requires logging of events relevant to identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations. The SDK's \"system states\" capture could support this, but it's vague in the proposal — what specific events are logged? The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input hashes, and system states\" which is a reasonable starting set but may not fully cover 12(2)(a)-(c) without more detail.\n- Article 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) systems requires very specific fields (start/end of each use, reference database, matched input data, identification of natural persons in verification). The proposal doesn't explicitly address these domain-specific requirements. A generic SDK would need significant project-specific configuration to meet 12(3).\n\n**My overall assessment:**\nThe idea is directionally correct and technically feasible. A standardized logging/telemetry layer reduces per-project effort for the common case. However:\n- The scope (\"all lab projects\") makes it a large upfront investment with uncertain ROI during prototyping phase.\n- It may slow down initial prototyping rather than speed it up, because teams will be waiting for or adapting to a centralized infrastructure dependency.\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level; the actual technical challenges (heterogeneous model stacks, variable logging requirements by risk category, performance overhead of wrapping execution) are not addressed.\n- For my specific vantage point (speed/quality of prototyping), a heavyweight unified SDK that must be integrated into every project before you can even start building is potentially a net negative for prototyping velocity in the short-to-medium term. A lighter-weight approach (e.g., well-documented logging patterns + library that projects opt into) might serve prototyping better than a mandated centralized wrapper.\n\nThat said, if the lab already has a relatively uniform execution environment and the SDK can be built incrementally (start with basic timestamp/hash capture, add domain-specific fields later), it could work. The \"compliant by default\" angle is attractive for reducing rework.\n\nI'd say this is a reasonable proposal that clears my bar but isn't a slam-dunk from the prototyping-speed perspective. It's technically viable, addresses the regulation appropriately at a high level, and does reduce per-project friction in the steady state. But the upfront cost to prototyping velocity and the breadth of scope give me pause. I'll approve it with a moderate promise score reflecting that it's good but not my top priority for prototyping speed — there might be lighter approaches that serve prototyping better while still meeting Article 12.\n\nLet me settle on: Approve, but with a promise around 62-68. It's technically sound and directionally right, reduces long-term friction, but the upfront investment and centralized dependency concern me from a prototyping-velocity standpoint. The proposal is somewhat underspecified for the domain-specific requirements of Article 12(3).\n\nI'll go with approve at 64.\n",
