        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2705 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Regulatory Compliance Audit Dashboard\" - a read-only web application for external auditors to view system logs and human verification signatures, querying an immutable log database with scoped API keys.\n\nLet me think about this from my professional vantage point (technical viability, speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A read-only dashboard querying a database is technically straightforward. This is well-trodden software engineering territory - web app + API + database query. No novel technology required.\n- The concept of \"immutable log database\" suggests an append-only data store (like an event-sourced system or a database with restricted update/delete permissions). This is feasible but adds architectural complexity to the logging infrastructure itself.\n- Scoped API keys for access control - standard practice, technically sound.\n- Displaying \"specific matches and identities of verifying personnel\" - this maps to Article 12(3)(c) and (d) for Annex III point 1(a) systems. The data needs to be captured at the system level first; the dashboard is just a presentation layer.\n- One concern: \"immutable log database\" - making logs truly immutable while still being queryable by auditors requires careful architectural decisions (WORM storage, cryptographic chaining, etc.). This isn't trivial but is well-understood.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A read-only web dashboard is one of the fastest things to prototype. No complex ML pipeline, no novel algorithm. Standard CRUD (read only), standard authentication patterns.\n- However, there's a dependency: you can't build this until the underlying logging infrastructure actually captures all the required data fields (start/end times, reference databases, input data for matches, verifier identities). The dashboard is useless without the data pipeline feeding it.\n- The proposal as stated assumes the log database already exists with the right schema. If we're building from scratch, the bigger engineering effort is in the logging infrastructure itself, not the dashboard.\n- From a prototyping speed perspective: the dashboard itself could be prototyped quickly (days to weeks), but it's gated on upstream data availability.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- Read-only access is good security practice - limits attack surface.\n- \"Secure\" and \"scoped API keys\" are mentioned but not elaborated. For actual deployment, you'd need proper authentication (OAuth2/OIDC perhaps), audit trails of who viewed what, rate limiting, etc. But for a prototype, scoped API keys work fine.\n- The proposal is somewhat narrow in scope - it's just the presentation layer. It doesn't address how logs are generated, stored immutably, or retained over lifetime. But that's fine; it's one piece of the puzzle.\n\n**Does it clear my bar?**\n- Technically viable: Yes, absolutely. This is standard web development.\n- Speed of prototyping: The dashboard itself prototypes fast. But its value is entirely dependent on upstream logging infrastructure being in place. If we're starting from zero, this alone doesn't move the needle much.\n- Quality: A read-only view with proper access control is a clean architectural choice. Separating auditor-facing interface from internal dev environments is good practice.\n\nI think this is technically sound and prototypably quick as a component. However, it's somewhat trivial in isolation - it's a query interface on a database that must already exist. The real engineering challenge (and the part Article 12 actually mandates) is the automatic recording of events over the system lifetime. The dashboard is nice-to-have for compliance verification but doesn't by itself satisfy Article 12(1).\n\nThat said, from my vantage point on technical viability and prototyping 