\"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\" and \"risk-management system\" - some of these are not purely derivable from git commits and hyperparameter logs. You need human-authored context (e.g., rationale for architectural choices, risk assessments).\n- However, as a *prototype* that handles the mechanical 60-70% of documentation (model cards, data provenance, training configs, version history), it's very achievable. The remaining narrative sections can be templated with human-in-the-loop completion.\n- Speed of prototyping: This is actually quite buildable. A first pass could be a Python script that reads git log + config YAMLs + MLflow/WTB exports and renders Markdown/LaTeX docs using Jinja2 templates. That's a 1-2 week prototype for an experienced engineer.\n- Quality: The generated docs will be accurate on the mechanical facts (what version, what hyperparameters, when trained, data sources) but may need curation for the qualitative sections. This is acceptable as it still saves significant time vs. manual writing.\n\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):\n- Technically viable: Yes, clearly within reach with standard tools.\n- Speed to prototype: High - this is a well-bounded problem with clear inputs and outputs.\n- Quality potential: Good for the structured portions; the \"living\" aspect means it stays current automatically, which is a significant quality advantage over static docs that go stale.\n- Risk: Scope creep into trying to auto-generate ALL compliance documentation (including risk management narratives) would be problematic, but the proposal as stated focuses on synthesizing from technical artifacts, which is reasonable.\n\nThe framing \"prevents scientific ambition from being throttled by administrative labor\" speaks directly to my concern as a dev engineer - manual doc maintenance pulls engineers away from building/iterating on systems. An automated pipeline that keeps docs current without requiring dedicated writer time is genuinely valuable for prototyping velocity.\n\nI think this clears the bar. It's technically sound, quick to prototype, addresses a real bottleneck in our workflow, and the inputs are all machine-readable. I'll approve it with a high promise score reflecting its technical tractability and direct impact on prototyping speed.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 82-85. It's very buildable, directly relevant, but there's some risk that \"dynamic\" becomes an over-engineered monolith rather than a lightweight pipeline. Also, the boundary between what can be auto-generated vs. what needs human narrative (risk management sections) needs to be clearly scoped in prototyping. But as a prototype concept, it's strong.\n",
