can be properly documented and traced. The integration with existing CI/CD tools is pragmatic and reduces implementation risk.\n\nFrom a compliance officer's perspective:\n- This reduces the risk of documentation gaps (a major audit finding)\n- Version control provides an audit trail\n- Real-time data pull ensures accuracy\n- However, it doesn't fully solve the logging capability problem - it assumes logs exist and formats them\n- The \"clear and comprehensive form\" requirement is partially addressed\n\nI would approve this. It's a meaningful step toward compliance with the documentation aspects of Article 12 and Recital 71. It addresses institutional survival (avoiding non-compliance penalties) and market reputation (demonstrating robust compliance infrastructure). \n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's valuable but not a complete solution to Article 12 requirements - it's more about documentation than logging per se, and it doesn't address the specific event recording requirements of Articles 12(2) and 12(3). But as a component of a broader compliance program, it's solid and addresses a real gap. I'll go with 74.\n",
