        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is \"P1731: Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline\" - an automated tool that generates Recital 71 compliance documentation by parsing code metadata, docstrings, MLflow experiment tracking logs, etc., triggered on major version releases.\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The core idea is sound: use existing engineering artifacts (code metadata, experiment tracking via MLflow, docstrings) to auto-generate structured regulatory documentation. This is a well-understood software engineering pattern - CI/CD pipelines already generate reports from build artifacts.\n- However, there's a significant gap: Recital 71 requires \"clear and comprehensive\" documentation of algorithms, data, training/testing/validation processes, risk management systems, AND capabilities/limitations. Can docstring parsing + MLflow logs truly capture all of this? \n  - MLflow tracks model versions, parameters, metrics, datasets - good for some parts\n  - Docstrings can capture system characteristics at a basic level\n  - But \"risk-management system\" documentation, \"capabilities and limitations\" in regulatory language, \"training processes\" in the nuanced sense required by regulators - these are harder to auto-generate from code metadata alone. The risk management section especially requires human judgment about what risks exist and how they're mitigated.\n- The \"trigger on every major version release\" is a reasonable automation point, but Recital 71 says documentation should be kept up-to-date \"throughout the lifetime\" - this suggests continuous updates, not just at release points.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This directly addresses a real pain point: manual regulatory report writing does 