oyment gate but doesn't make the R&D iteration cycle faster. The core technical building blocks (MLflow integration, code metadata parsing) are well-understood and not risky to implement. I'd say this is moderately promising but not a breakthrough. It's a practical engineering improvement rather than an enabler of new capabilities.\n\nI'll approve it with a moderate promise score - it's technically feasible, addresses a real pain point at the deployment boundary, uses tools we likely already have or can adopt quickly, and the implementation risk is low. But it won't transform prototyping speed per se, and the \"eliminates manual writing\" framing is overpromising.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, with a promise score around 62-68. It's solidly useful engineering work that reduces friction at deployment time, technically viable, but not transformative for core R&D velocity. I'll go with 65.\n",
