        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer at an R&D institution, focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2932 is about building a \"Unified High-Risk Logging Infrastructure\" — a standardized middleware layer that intercepts model calls to log events into an immutable database, integrated into CI/CD pipelines.\n\nLet me assess this from a development engineering perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The core idea of middleware APIs intercepting model calls is technically sound and well-understood (similar to APM tools like Datadog, New Relic, or OpenTelemetry approaches). This is a proven pattern.\n- \"Immutable, version-controlled database\" — this is feasible (append-only stores, hash-chained logs, etc.) but adds complexity. Need to think about what \"version-controlled\" means in practice for a logging system vs. a code repository.\n- Integration into existing CI/CD pipelines: This is reasonable but conflates two different concerns. CI/CD integration makes sense for deployment-time configuration of logging (e.g., enabling/disabling loggers, setting retention policies), but the actual runtime logging happens at inference time, not build/deploy time. The proposal seems to blur these.\n- \"Across all high-risk AI pipelines\" — this is a scope risk. Different model architectures (LLMs, vision models, tabular classifiers) have very different event semantics. A truly unified layer requires significant abstraction work and may become a bottleneck or become so generic it's useless for specific compliance needs.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Impact:**\n- This is the key concern from my vantage point. Building a unified middleware layer that intercepts ALL model calls across ALL pipelines is a massive engineering undertaking. It would:\n  - Add latency to every inference call (even if small, at scale it matters)\n  - Create a single point of failure for all 