posal addresses a real compliance need.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address Article 12(1) (automatic recording of events/logs over lifetime), nor does it address 12(2)(a)-(c) specifically about logging capabilities for risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring. It's more focused on the development/documentation side.\n- However, as a Compliance Officer, I should note that Recital 71 explicitly requires documentation of \"data, training, testing and validation processes\" - so this IS relevant to compliance even if it's not the logging mechanism per se.\n- The DVC tool mention is practical and implementable - not just hand-waving.\n- The framing as \"intellectual merit and reproducibility\" while meeting regulatory mandates is a good dual-benefit positioning that would help get internal buy-in without seeming like pure compliance overhead.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:** Having proper data provenance documentation protects the institution in case of regulatory audits, enforcement actions, or litigation. Without it, we could face significant penalties under the AI Act (up to 7% of global turnover for certain violations).\n\n**From market reputation perspective:** Demonstrating robust data governance and reproducibility is a selling point, especially when dealing with public sector clients who are subject to their own compliance obligations.\n\n**From regulatory compliance perspective:** This addresses a real gap. The regulation requires technical documentation that includes information on \"data, training, testing and validation processes.\" Having an automated pipeline for this rather than relying on manual documentation is significantly more reliable and audit-ready.\n\nHowever, I should note that this proposal doesn't fully cover Article 12's specific logging requirements (the automatic event recording, the specific data points in 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems). It's complementary but not sufficient on its