eous. Different AI systems (NLP models, computer vision, reinforcement learning agents) may have very different logging needs. A \"one-size-fits-all\" API might force unnecessary abstraction or fail to capture system-specific events that Article 12 requires.\n- The proposal says \"all high-risk AI systems\" — but in an R&D lab, not all projects are necessarily high-risk under the Act. Building infrastructure for a subset of projects and then having it be mandatory could be over-engineering for some teams.\n- Centralized vault: this is a security consideration (good) but also an operational bottleneck. If the middleware or vault goes down, logging stops across all systems — which would itself be a compliance violation.\n- The proposal doesn't address who maintains this infrastructure long-term, what happens when new requirements emerge (the Act is evolving), or how it handles the specific minimum requirements in Article 12(3) for biometric systems vs. general high-risk systems.\n\n**Net assessment from my vantage point:**\nThe core idea — sharing compliance infrastructure to avoid duplicated engineering effort — is sound and directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency. This is exactly the kind of \"build once, use many\" approach that a lab director should champion when multiple teams face the same regulatory obligation. However, the proposal as stated is somewhat high-level and doesn't address the significant upfront investment, the heterogeneity problem in research environments, or maintenance/ownership questions. \n\nBut I'm screening whether it clears my bar, not whether it's perfectly specified. The fundamental logic — that a shared logging layer reduces per-project compliance cost at scale — is strong from a resource-allocation perspective. In a lab with multiple high-risk AI projects (which is presumably the case since they're developing AI systems subject to the Act), the economies of scale argument is compelling. The alternative (each team builds their