e database, (c) input data for matches, (d) identification of natural persons in verification. The centralized framework might not capture all these specific fields unless explicitly designed to. However, the proposal says it's a \"framework\" — it could be extended. But as stated, it's somewhat generic and may not fully cover 12(3).\n- \"Throughout a system's lifetime\" — the proposal mentions this, which is good. Retention period needs to align with system lifecycle.\n- The proposal says \"centralized event-bus captures timestamps and input/output metadata across different model versions during runtime.\" This is somewhat narrow — Article 12(2) requires logs that facilitate identifying risk situations, post-market monitoring, and operational monitoring. Input/output metadata may not be sufficient; you might need to log model decisions, confidence scores, edge cases triggered, etc.\n- \"Mandatory for any project labeled high-risk by our internal review board\" — this is good governance but creates a dependency on the classification process being robust. If misclassification occurs, logging gaps arise.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\n- This is squarely a compliance infrastructure proposal. It addresses the most fundamental technical requirement of Article 12(1). Without it, we cannot demonstrate compliance to regulators, and in an enforcement action, absence of proper logs would be catastrophic for institutional survival.\n- Market reputation: being able to demonstrably show robust logging infrastructure is a competitive advantage when selling to EU enterprises who will demand proof of AI Act compliance from their suppliers.\n- The \"immutable\" aspect protects against allegations of evidence tampering.\n- Standardization reduces the risk that individual projects implement inadequate or inconsistent logging.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is a strong, well-aligned proposal for my function. It directl