t captures internal representations is technically ambitious but the connection between regulatory metadata and genuine causal understanding is tenuous at best.\n\n**Intellectual Merit:**\n- The intellectual premise—that building observability infrastructure mandated by regulation could advance interpretability science—has some merit. Production systems running on real data with diverse inputs could generate interesting behavioral patterns.\n- However, the proposal conflates several different things: (1) logging for compliance, (2) capturing internal activations for research, and (3) deriving causal explanations from those captures. These are quite different in difficulty and methodology.\n- \"Neuron activation patterns\" suggests dense network interpretability, but many high-risk AI systems under the EU AI Act may be ensemble-based, use multiple models, or operate at a higher abstraction level than individual neurons. The proposal doesn't adequately address this heterogeneity.\n- The phrase \"turns mandatory auditing into a mechanism for scientific discovery\" is somewhat glib. Regulatory logs are designed to answer specific compliance questions (who used the system when, what input matched what reference). Bolting on full activation tracing creates enormous data management challenges and may not yield the kind of controlled experiments that interpretability research actually requires.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- If successful, a standardized way to capture internal state during production operation could be valuable for the interpretability community. Having access to how systems behave in-the-wild (not just in lab conditions) would be genuinely novel data.\n- But the scientific impact depends heavily on whether the captured data can actually be used for causal analysis. Raw activation traces without proper experimental design, controlled perturbations, and ground truth labels have limited interpretive value.\n- There's also a privacy/ethics dimension: ca