e 12(1) regarding automatic recording, and partially addresses Article 12(2) on traceability for post-market monitoring. However:\n- It's framed in research/reproducibility language rather than compliance language\n- The open-source aspect introduces governance and liability questions\n- It doesn't explicitly address the specific minimum requirements of Article 12(3) for Annex III systems\n- It doesn't clearly address how it handles human verification logging (Art 14(5), Art 12(3)(d))\n- The \"autonomous\" aspect needs careful compliance oversight to ensure logs can't be tampered with\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point, this is a useful building block but not sufficient as a standalone compliance solution. It addresses the technical infrastructure for logging (which is necessary) but doesn't fully map to the regulatory requirements. I would approve it as it directly supports Article 12(1) and partially supports Article 12(2), and the provenance/lineage tracking is genuinely useful for demonstrating compliance during audits. But my promise score should reflect that it's a strong partial solution with notable gaps from a pure compliance standpoint.\n\nI'll approve it because:\n- It directly implements the automatic logging requirement of Art 12(1)\n- It supports traceability which is the core purpose of Article 12 per Recital 71\n- It covers distributed infrastructure (practical necessity)\n- The lineage tracking helps with post-market monitoring (Art 12(2)(b))\n\nBut I'll give it a moderate-high promise score because:\n- The framing is research-oriented, not compliance-oriented\n- Open-source introduces governance risks\n- Specific Art 12(3) requirements aren't explicitly addressed\n- Human verification logging isn't mentioned\n- \"Autonomous\" raises tamper-resistance questions\n\nI'll set promise at around 65. It's a useful and relevant infrastructure investment that supports compliance, but it's not a complete compliance solution and the framing/governance concer