tal 71 does provide interpretive context for what \"comprehensible information\" means, so referencing it is reasonable.\n- It's a process/procedure proposal rather than a technical implementation — as Compliance Officer, I'd want to ensure this actually drives technical changes in the logging architecture, not just paper reviews.\n- \"Appointing compliance officers\" — we likely already have them; the question is whether this creates new roles or formalizes existing ones. This could be resource-intensive if it means dedicated headcount per project.\n- The proposal doesn't address Article 12(3) specifics (the minimum logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems), which are more prescriptive.\n\n**From my vantage point:**\nThis is a solid, standard compliance control mechanism. It's not groundbreaking but it addresses real risks: late-stage discovery of missing documentation/log evidence could delay certification by months or years, harming our ability to bring products to market and potentially triggering regulatory scrutiny. As Compliance Officer, I would want such a gate in place. However, as described, it's somewhat high-level and would need operational detail (specific checklists tied to Article 12(2)(a)-(c), defined pass/fail criteria for log completeness, escalation paths). \n\nIt's not a technical proposal that implements the logging itself — it's an oversight/verification layer. That's still valuable from my perspective because compliance requires independent verification that technical teams have met their obligations.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a reasonable, standard compliance control that directly serves my function's mandate of ensuring regulatory compliance and protecting institutional survival through timely market entry. I'd approve it but with moderate confidence since the description is somewhat generic and would need fleshing out in implementation.\n\nPromise score: This is useful but not transformative. It's a standard QA/compli