ion vs funding constraints**: This proposal has very little scientific ambition. It's essentially compliance engineering - building a UI that logs who clicked \"verify\" and what database was queried. There's no novel research here. From a resource-allocation perspective, this is the kind of thing you'd assign to existing engineering teams as a compliance task rather than a new R&D initiative. The question is whether it justifies being a standalone \"proposal\" consuming lab leadership attention and dedicated resources, or whether it's better absorbed into an existing compliance/logging workstream.\n\n4. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: A logging/sign-off interface could potentially leverage existing authentication systems, database query logging, and audit trail infrastructure that the lab may already have for other purposes. If built as a \"specialized\" tool from scratch, it risks creating siloed infrastructure rather than integrating with what exists. However, if the lab doesn't currently develop Annex III 1(a) systems, this is entirely unnecessary resource expenditure.\n\n5. **The \"mitigate extreme legal risks\" framing**: This is accurate - Article 12(3) has specific minimum requirements for biometric verification systems, and non-compliance carries significant penalties. So there IS a real need if we have such systems. But the proposal positions this as a standalone tool rather than an integrated component of our system architecture.\n\n6. **Key concern from my vantage**: The proposal is narrow (one sub-category), relatively straightforward engineering (not R&D per se), and potentially duplicative of existing infrastructure. It's the kind of thing that should be handled as a compliance work item within existing teams rather than as a standalone \"proposal\" that commits scarce implementation resources. However, if we DO have biometric verification systems in development, this is a necessary and relatively low-cost addition.\n\n7. **Another a