nts the wheel and gets it slightly wrong, creating technical debt.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n1. Building a unified SDK itself requires upfront investment of engineering resources before any benefit is realized. Is there enough scale (number of projects) to justify this? If the lab has many high-risk AI systems in development, yes. If only 2-3, maybe not.\n2. The scope might creep — \"standardized hooks for automatic event recording and metadata capture across all AI modules\" could become a large engineering project if not scoped tightly.\n3. However, wrapping OpenTelemetry rather than building from scratch significantly mitigates the upfront cost concern.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a very well-aligned proposal with my domain of concern. It directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency (shared component reduces per-project costs), leverages existing infrastructure (OpenTelemetry wrapper), and protects funding by preventing compliance work from consuming R&D time at scale. The \"build once, use everywhere\" model is the canonical answer to how you handle a cross-cutting regulatory requirement without letting it eat your research budget.\n\nThe risk of scope creep is manageable if the SDK team is kept small and focused on the wrapper layer rather than trying to solve all logging problems. But the core idea is sound.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high — maybe 85-90. It's a strong, well-motivated proposal that directly serves my function's concerns. I'll say 87.\n",
