tion over logs, secure multi-party verification, TEE-based attestation chains, etc. Without a specific technical thesis, it reads more like a broad exploration mandate than a focused research project.\n- The mention of \"distributed ledgers or secure enclaves\" suggests the team hasn't yet committed to a specific technical approach. This is fine for an early-stage proposal but reduces intellectual specificity.\n- Evaluating \"against various attack vectors and performance constraints\" is standard systems security work, not particularly novel in itself.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- If successful, verifiable AI logging could have genuine impact beyond compliance — it would contribute to the broader field of verifiable computation, trustworthy ML, and auditability of automated systems.\n- The \"global leader in the science of trustworthy AI\" framing is aspirational but somewhat generic.\n- The intersection of AI-specific compliance (EU AI Act) with cryptographic verification is a relatively new research niche that could generate novel contributions to both AI safety/alignment literature and applied cryptography.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- The proposal conflates regulatory compliance engineering with fundamental research. \"Provable traceability\" for EU AI Act compliance is more of an engineering systems problem than a knowledge-frontier problem, unless the cryptographic techniques themselves are novel contributions.\n- The \"distributed ledger\" component feels like buzzword-driven framing rather than a carefully chosen technical approach. For most high-risk AI deployment scenarios (industrial safety, medical devices), a distributed ledger introduces significant complexity without clear benefit over well-designed centralized verifiable log architectures with TEE-based signing.\n- The intellectual contribution is unclear: what new theorem, protocol, or computational result would this produce? Or is it primarily applied systems engineering dressed up 