out automatic event recording; technical documentation is a broader artifact that includes risk management system docs, capabilities, limitations, etc. A \"generator\" that converts logging data into \"comprehensive technical documentation\" might oversimplify - not all required documentation content flows from logs. Risk assessments, conformity assessment documents, and other elements are more static or semi-static.\n- The phrase \"automate the generation of compliance reports for regulators\" is slightly concerning. Regulators receive specific artifacts (technical documentation per Annex IV, declaration of conformity, etc.), not just \"compliance reports.\" The tool needs to be scoped correctly.\n- \"From our version control systems\" - this assumes all relevant data lives in VCS. Training data, test results, risk assessments may live elsewhere. The pipeline might have gaps.\n- However, the core idea is sound: if you structure your metadata well during R&D, you can keep documentation current automatically rather than having a stale PDF that's updated once a year before an audit.\n\n**From my vantage point (compliance, institutional survival, market reputation):**\nThis is genuinely valuable. The EU AI Act requires ongoing compliance, not just point-in-time. Having a system that keeps technical documentation dynamically aligned with the actual state of the system is exactly what regulators will want to see. It reduces the risk of documentation drift - where your system changes but your docs don't reflect it. This is a real compliance gap that many organizations struggle with.\n\nThe proposal is not perfect in its scoping (it slightly overclaims by suggesting logging data alone produces all required technical documentation), but the core direction is correct and high-value. It's an implementation tool that directly supports Article 12 obligations and Recital 71 requirements.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a legitimate compliance infrastructure investment. The risk of NOT having it (documentation drift, slow response to regulator requests, inability to demonstrate ongoing compliance) is real and could threaten institutional survival if we can't produce compliant documentation on demand.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's a strong direction but has scoping issues that need refinement before implementation. The core value is clear - dynamic documentation aligned with live system state is exactly what the Act envisions (\"kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system\"). But it won't be a silver bullet for all compliance documentation needs.\n\nLet me settle on 80.\n",
