ance\" is a practical concern — if the middleware slows systems down significantly, engineers might work around it, creating compliance gaps.\n\n**Weaknesses / Concerns from Compliance vantage:**\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level and doesn't specify retention periods (the Act references \"lifetime of the system\"), specific log formats for regulatory submission, or how logs would be made available to notified bodies during conformity assessment.\n- It mentions \"all high-risk AI assets\" but Article 12(3) has specific minimum requirements only for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics). The proposal doesn't differentiate between the baseline logging requirement (Art 12(1)-(2)) and the enhanced logging for biometric systems (Art 12(3)). A one-size-fits-all approach might over-engineer for some systems or under-specify for others.\n- \"Tamper-evident format\" is good but the Act also requires that logs be retained for a specific period — the proposal doesn't address retention policy explicitly.\n- The proposal says \"without degrading performance\" which is an engineering concern more than compliance, but from my vantage, if it does degrade performance and engineers work around it, that's a compliance risk. So flagging this is appropriate.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom a compliance standpoint, this is a strong structural proposal. It directly maps to Article 12's core requirement (automatic logging), provides institutional enforcement mechanism (mandatory dependency), addresses traceability (tamper-evident), and covers the key data points needed for regulatory audit. The unified approach reduces the risk of inconsistent or missing logs across different AI systems, which is a major compliance failure mode.\n\nThe main gap is that it doesn't fully address the differentiated requirements in Article 12(3) for biometric systems, and retention periods aren't specified. But as a foundational infrastructure piece, this is exactly what a compliance officer would want: a single, enforceable mechanism ensuring no high-risk system goes to production without adequate logging capability.\n\nThis clears my bar. It directly serves institutional survival (avoids fines), regulatory compliance (implements Art 12 technically), and market reputation (demonstrates robust governance). I'd approve it with moderate-high promise score. The lack of specificity on Annex III 1(a) differentiated requirements and retention policy keeps it from being a perfect 90+, but the core architecture is sound and essential.\n\nI'll rate it around 82-85. It's a clear \"yes\" for compliance infrastructure, with some gaps that would need to be addressed in implementation (the proposal is at framework level).\n",
