oposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources to pursue it\").\n- The actual Article 12 obligation is engineering work: building logging infrastructure, ensuring automatic recording, designing what events to log. A research project on causal inference over logs doesn't build that capability.\n- It risks distracting from the core compliance work.\n\n**Market reputation angle:**\n- On the surface, \"turning a regulatory burden into an intellectual contribution\" sounds good for PR. But regulators and market partners care about whether we're actually compliant, not whether we have interesting research papers derived from our logs.\n- There's a reputational risk: if we use compliance-mandated logs (which include personal data per 12(3)(d)) for research without clear legal basis, that could be seen as an overreach and damage trust with regulators.\n- Conversely, it doesn't really enhance market reputation in the eyes of regulatory authorities or enterprise clients who care about reliability and compliance.\n\n**Critical issues:**\n1. It's a research project, not an implementation. My job is to ensure we meet Article 12, which requires engineering solutions (automatic logging systems). This proposal doesn't build that.\n2. Data integrity risk: using compliance logs for research muddies the audit trail. If regulators ask for logs and some have been altered or accessed for research purposes, that's a problem.\n3. The \"transforming legal requirement into intellectual contribution\" framing is cosmetic spin. It doesn't make our logging more compliant; it just repurposes data we're already required to collect.\n4. No clear deliverable that maps back to Article 12 compliance obligations.\n5. Resource misallocation: scarce engineering resources go toward speculative research rather than building the actual logging infrastructure Article 12 requires.\n\nThis doesn't meet my bar. It's an interesting academic idea but from a compliance officer's perspective, it