        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1571\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This is a process-layer protocol that leverages the logging infrastructure we are already obligated to build under Article 12(1) rather than requiring new capital investment, and its quarterly cadence with a small task force keeps the recurring opportunity cost on R&D throughput bounded. The main efficiency risk is coordination overhead of pulling cross-functional staff off their research tracks each quarter, but the 'without disrupting daily workflows' constraint and the diagnostic (not remedial) scope keep that manageable; catching traceability gaps internally before an external auditor does protects the lab's ability to actually deploy systems on which downstream scientific programmes depend.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 70\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director at an R&D institution focused on resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal P1571 is about establishing a quarterly internal regulatory stress test protocol - essentially periodic audits where a small cross-functional task force simulates post-market monitoring requests to verify logging and traceability meet Article 12 standards.\n\nLet me assess this from my domain:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- A \"small cross-functional task force\" conducting quarterly simulations sounds relatively lightweight in terms of dedicated headcount.\n- However, \"cross-functional\" means pulling people from various teams (engineering, data science, compliance) away from their primary R&D work every quarter. That's a recurring opportunity cost on research output.\n- Quarterly frequency is reasonable - not excessive, not too infrequent to be useful.\n- The protocol itself is essentially a process, not infrastructure investment. It leverages existing staff rather than requiring new hires or tools (though it may require some minor tooling).\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- This is purely compliance work - it doesn't advance any scientific goal directly. But it's defensive: without proper logging/traceability, the lab can't deploy high-risk AI systems at all, which would block scientific programs that depend on those deployments.\n- The \"pre-emptive check before external audits\" framing means it could save significant costs if an external auditor finds gaps - remediation under audit pressure is far more expensive than catching issues internally.\n- It's a modest investment relative to the risk it mitigates (non-compliance with EU AI Act can mean fines up to 7% of global turnover or €35M).\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The protocol leverages existing systems - it doesn't build new logging infrastructure, it tests whether what's already there is sufficient. This is efficient: it uses the lab's current investment in logging (required by Article 12(1)) and verifies it works as intended.\n- \"Without disrupting daily research workflows\" is a positive constraint that shows awareness of protecting R&D throughput.\n- The simulation approach (reconstructing events from logs) is clever - it's a functional test of the existing infrastructure rather than building parallel systems.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- Who defines the scope of \"simulations\"? Without clear boundaries, this could balloon into an open-ended exercise consuming more time than budgeted.\n- Cross-functional task forces can be administratively heavy to coordinate. Scheduling people from different teams quarterly is a non-trivial coordination overhead for a lab that's already stretched.\n- It assumes the logging infrastructure already exists and functions. If it doesn't, this protocol will just repeatedly identify gaps without solving them - it's a diagnostic, not a remedy. But as a screening step before committing to build out full compliance, it's efficient.\n- The \"quarterly\" cadence is reasonable for a lab that isn't in active deployment of high-risk systems but is in R&D mode. If the lab moves toward deployment, annual might suffice initially; if they're actively deploying and under market surveillance, quarterly makes sense.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a reasonably efficient use of existing resources. It's process-oriented rather than capital-intensive. The main cost is time (quarterly pull-away of a small team), which is modest. The benefit is risk mitigation that protects the lab's ability to actually deploy systems - without it, scientific programs that require deployed high-risk AI are at risk of being blocked by compliance failures discovered externally. It leverages existing infrastructure rather than requiring new builds. The \"small cross-functional task force\" framing keeps resource commitment bounded.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar. It's not glamorous R&D, but it's efficient, bounded, and protects the lab's ability to actually use its systems. It uses what we have (existing logs, existing staff) rather than requiring new investment. The quarterly cadence is proportional. I'll approve with a moderate-to-good promise score - it's solid operational work but not transformative, and there are minor coordination costs that could erode efficiency if poorly managed.\n\nI'd put the promise around 68-72. It's clearly worthwhile and efficient, but from my vantage point it's \"good operations\" rather than \"excellent allocation.\" The main uncertainty is execution quality of a cross-functional task force in a busy R&D environment.\n",
