on faces enforcement risk. Automation reduces this exposure.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n1. **Scope creep beyond Article 12**: The proposal conflates \"record-keeping\" (Article 12) with broader \"technical documentation\" obligations (which are primarily in Articles 11 and Annex IV). Technical documentation under the AI Act is a specific, structured document with defined content requirements. A \"dashboard\" may not satisfy the requirement for a formal technical file that can be produced to regulators on demand.\n2. **\"Replaces static PDF reports\"** — this framing is risky. Regulators expect a producible, versioned, signed-off documentation artifact. A dashboard is ephemeral and screen-dependent. The institution still needs a formal, exportable, archivable document. This could create a compliance gap if the \"real-time dashboard\" isn't also an auditable record.\n3. **Implementation risk**: Building this pipeline requires integration with MLflow or similar tools, which presupposes those tools are already in place and well-maintained. If the lab's MLOps infrastructure is immature, this becomes a large project with uncertain ROI.\n4. **\"Automated\" doesn't eliminate human accountability**: The regulation still requires a natural person to be responsible for documentation accuracy. Automation reduces error but doesn't remove the need for sign-off and review processes.\n5. **Resource commitment concern**: This is a significant engineering investment. As Compliance, I need to weigh whether this is a \"must-have\" compliance infrastructure or an optimization that could be addressed more simply (e.g., scheduled manual updates with version control).\n\n**From my vantage point (Compliance - institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\n\nThe core insight of the proposal — tying documentation generation directly to the development pipeline so it can't drift — is genuinely valuable for compliance. The \"up to date throughout lifetime\" requirement is o