ms, and as an R&D institution developing such systems, we likely will have at least a few.\n- The \"immutable ledger\" adds cost — what kind of immutable storage? Blockchain? Append-only database? This could be over-engineered if the actual requirement is just structured logging that meets Article 12's minimum requirements.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Integrating into CI/CD pipeline leverages existing infrastructure rather than building parallel systems. Good.\n- \"Shared library for all developers\" means we're not duplicating effort across teams — this is efficient use of the lab's human capital.\n- The middleware approach in our \"primary development language\" suggests it fits within what we already have, reducing friction.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- Scope: \"across ALL high-risk prototypes\" — do we know which systems will be high-risk? Over-building for the long tail is inefficient. But a shared library that's opt-in per system would mitigate this.\n- The \"immutable ledger\" might be overkill — Article 12 requires logging capabilities but doesn't mandate immutability in a specific technical sense. This could inflate cost without proportional regulatory benefit.\n- Building a middleware layer has upfront cost and maintenance burden. Who maintains it? If the lab is resource-constrained, this ongoing maintenance is a recurring cost that competes with research priorities.\n- However, the counterargument is powerful: if we DON'T build this once, every project team will independently struggle with logging compliance, creating N× redundancy in effort and inconsistency in implementation.\n\n**Net assessment:**\nThis is a well-conceived efficiency play from my vantage point. It converts per-project compliance cost into an amortized infrastructure cost, which scales better as the number of high-risk prototypes grows. The CI/CD integration is smart because it makes compliance non-optional (you can't deploy without passing through log