gineering plumbing. The positioning is overstated.\n- Resource allocation: diverting engineers to build, maintain, document, and support an open-source project for external users is a significant ongoing commitment. Open-source means you inherit maintenance burden, bug reports from strangers, version compatibility issues across the ecosystem, security patching obligations, etc. This is NOT free — it's a perpetual drain on resources.\n- \"Minimal code changes\" in the proposal description doesn't mean minimal effort to build correctly for all edge cases. Article 12(3) has specific requirements (period of use, reference database, input data, identification of natural persons). Getting this right across diverse institutional setups is non-trivial.\n- The academic community is fragmented — different institutions have vastly different infrastructure, security postures, and compliance needs. A \"one-size-fits-all\" library may not be useful to many, reducing adoption and thus the ROI.\n- We already need to implement logging for our own systems (that's the legal requirement). Building it as a standalone open-source project adds scope beyond what we need. We could build internal tools that happen to be modular without taking on the full open-source support burden.\n- The \"scientific leader\" framing is misleading from a resource perspective. It sounds grand but the actual deliverable (decorators for logging) doesn't advance scientific frontiers. It's compliance engineering.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a pure resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this proposal asks us to take on perpetual open-source maintenance obligations for an audience we don't serve commercially or regulatorily. The core value — implementing Article 12 logging in our own systems — is necessary but doesn't require the open-source packaging. The \"scientific leadership\" justification is weak because compliance tooling isn't a scientific contribution. The opportunity cost of diverting engineers to b