        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1202\",\n      \"rationale\": \"MLflow with an immutable append-only backend is a technically mature and feasible pattern for capturing model/dataset/hyperparameter lineage, and it removes a manual documentation bottleneck from the prototyping loop. However, Article 12(3) specifically demands per-inference runtime events (session start/end timestamps, reference-database identity, matched input data, verifying-person IDs), which MLflow's native tracking does not capture; those require bespoke per-system instrumentation on top of this layer, and a hard centralized dependency can introduce latency and coupling that slows early prototyping cycles.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Immutable Provenance Tracking System\" - a centralized infrastructure layer using MLflow with an immutable backend to track dataset, model version, and hyperparameters for every high-risk inference, providing automatic lineage archiving.\n\nLet me think about this from my development engineering perspective:\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- MLflow is a well-established, mature tool for experiment tracking. It's not experimental. The core functionality (tracking model versions, parameters, datasets) is production-ready and widely used.\n- \"Immutable backend\" - this is the key technical challenge. MLflow's default backends (Postgres, S3) are mutable. Making it truly immutable requires either:\n  - Hash-chained logging (append-only with cryptographic hashes)\n  - Writing to WORM storage\n  - Using something like an event-sourcing pattern with append-only logs\n  - This adds complexity but is technically feasible\n- \"Centralized infrastructure layer\" - this is a significant architectural commitment. It means every high-risk system must route through or integrate with this central service. For prototyping speed, this could be a bottleneck if the infrastructure isn't already built.\n- Mapping to Article 12 requirements: The article specifically asks for automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime, including start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, and person identification for Annex III point 1(a) systems. MLflow's native tracking is more about experiment/model lifecycle than runtime event logging. It captures model version and parameters well, but the \"automatic recording of events\" during inference (start/end time of each use, input data, reference database used) would need custom instrumentation beyond what MLflow provides out of the box.\n- The proposal says it \"satisfies traceability requirements\" - but Article 12 is specifically about runtime logging capabilities (what happens during inference), not just provenance (which model version was deployed). Provenance tracking answers \"which model ran?\" but doesn't inherently answer \"what inputs came in, what reference database was queried, when did the session start and end.\"\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- MLflow integration is relatively quick to prototype. The basic setup (tracking experiments, logging parameters) can be done in days.\n- However, making it truly immutable adds complexity. You need to design the immutability mechanism, test for tamper-resistance, and ensure it doesn't slow down inference.\n- \"Centralized\" means every team must integrate with this before they can ship a high-risk system. This creates a dependency that could slow prototyping if the central service has bugs or is under maintenance.\n- The claim that it eliminates \"manual documentation from researchers\" is partially true - model lineage and hyperparameters are auto-captured by MLflow. But runtime event logging (Article 12(3) specifics for Annex III 1(a)) still requires per-system instrumentation code.\n- For prototyping speed: if you're building a new high-risk system, you now need to integrate with this central layer from day one. That's added upfront work but reduces later compliance burden.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- Centralization is good for consistency - one way to do provenance tracking across the org.\n- But it introduces a single point of failure and a bottleneck. If the MLflow server goes down, does inference stop? (It shouldn't, but if logging is synchronous, it could.)\n- The \"immutable backend\" requirement adds operational complexity - storage grows indefinitely, you need retention policies that still satisfy \"lifetime of the system.\"\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound and technically feasible. MLflow is a reasonable choice for model/dataset provenance. However:\n1. It doesn't fully address Article 12's requirement for runtime event logging (inference-level events like start/end time, input data, reference database). It addresses the \"which model version\" part but not the \"what happened during this inference\" part.\n2. The immutability layer adds engineering complexity that needs to be scoped carefully.\n3. As a centralized infrastructure dependency, it could slow prototyping if not built incrementally.\n\nThat said, it's a solid foundation for part of the compliance picture. It's not a complete solution for Article 12 but it addresses a meaningful subset (model/dataset/hyperparameter lineage) and uses well-known tooling. The technical viability is high - MLflow works, immutable backends exist. The speed impact on prototyping is moderate - you add one integration point but gain automatic tracking of model versions.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar for approval as a technically viable direction that addresses part of the requirement and uses established tools. It's not a silver bullet (doesn't cover runtime event logging fully), but it's a reasonable engineering investment. The promise score should reflect that it's solid but incomplete relative to the full Article 12 scope, and the centralization could create prototyping friction if not handled well.\n\nI'll approve with a moderate-to-high promise score - maybe 72-78. It's technically sound, uses known tools, addresses a real part of the requirement, but has gaps (runtime events) and centralization risks for prototyping speed.\n",
