reating awkward abstractions.\n- However, if designed as an OPTIONAL layer that can be enabled per-project rather than a mandatory gate for all pipelines, it's much more palatable from a prototyping speed perspective.\n- The proposal says \"researchers call to log\" — this implies explicit instrumentation points, which means researchers need to know where to insert hooks. That adds cognitive overhead during rapid prototyping phases.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- A centralized approach does ensure consistency, which is good for compliance verification and reduces the chance of missed logging requirements.\n- Immutable storage is a reasonable choice for audit trails — prevents tampering, supports traceability as required by Article 12(1) \"over the lifetime of the system.\"\n- The framework approach means bugs in the library affect all projects simultaneously (single point of failure), but also means fixes propagate everywhere.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage:**\nThe technical concept is sound and well-understood. The main concern from a prototyping speed perspective is whether this becomes a mandatory prerequisite that blocks experimental work, or whether it's designed as a lightweight opt-in layer. The \"universal\" framing is slightly concerning because real AI systems vary enormously in architecture. However, the core idea — provide a logging library so researchers don't each reinvent compliance logging — is genuinely useful and reduces duplicated effort.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's technically viable (mature patterns), it actually helps prototyping speed by removing the \"figure out compliance logging\" task from every individual project, and it produces consistent quality. The risk of over-engineering or pipeline-mandate blocking is manageable with good scoping. I'd approve it but note that the \"universal\" scope needs careful phasing so it doesn't become a bottleneck before the first high-risk system ships.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 