eed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A narrow prototype (e.g., auto-generating a \"model change log\" section from git + registry) could be built in days to a couple weeks. That's prototypably feasible.\n- But the proposal as stated is quite broad - \"comprehensive technical documentation required by Recital 71.\" This is a multi-quarter engineering effort at minimum, spanning multiple teams (ML engineering, QA, legal/compliance for content validation).\n- The value of a prototype here: you could quickly demo that git-to-doc linkage works, validate the architecture with stakeholders. That's useful for scoping the full build.\n- Risk: \"automatically generate\" is doing a lot of heavy lifting. The quality of auto-generated documentation vs. what regulators expect (\"clear and comprehensive form\") may not match. Regulators want comprehensibility - a machine-generated doc that's technically accurate but lacks narrative context might fail the \"comprehensible\" bar in Recital 71.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage:**\n- The technical architecture is sound and well-understood (event-driven, template-based generation). Not a novel or risky engineering bet.\n- But the scope as stated (\"comprehensive technical documentation\") is very large for what seems like it's being pitched as a single pipeline. The prototype could be narrow, but the proposal implies a full solution.\n- The \"real-time\" aspect adds complexity (event streaming, consistency guarantees) that may not be necessary - batch updates on each release/commit might suffice and be much simpler to build.\n- There's a mismatch between what's technically auto-generable from code/model metadata vs. what Recital 71 actually requires (risk management documentation, system limitations discussion, etc.).\n\n**Decision:**\nThe core idea has technical merit - linking version control and model registry to documentation is a well-solvable engineering problem. A prototype of the narrow case (auto-updating a \"model changelog\" or