cing scientific ambition against regulatory overhead.\n4. **Scalability**: As we take on more high-risk projects (which the EU AI Act will likely force), this middleware scales without linear cost increases per new project.\n\nPotential concerns:\n- Updating base container images across the lab is a non-trivial operational task — there's disruption risk, potential compatibility issues with existing workloads, and a maintenance burden going forward (the middleware itself becomes critical infrastructure that needs its own updates).\n- \"Immutable storage\" implies specific storage architecture decisions that lock in choices; if our compliance needs evolve (new Annex III categories, etc.), the immutability pattern might become a constraint.\n- A centralized API creates a single point of failure and a bottleneck for engineering resources — all teams depend on one middleware team's capacity to extend it.\n- The proposal doesn't address who maintains this over time or how the lab funds ongoing maintenance vs. new feature development.\n\nHowever, these are manageable risks in a well-run lab. The core value proposition is strong: build compliance infrastructure once, leverage existing compute, free up researchers from administrative burden. This is precisely the kind of resource-efficient use of R&D infrastructure that my domain cares about.\n\nThe proposal is well-scoped — it's not \"build an entire new data platform\" but rather a middleware layer on top of what exists. It's proportionate to the regulatory requirement (Article 12 demands automatic logging; this delivers that).\n\n**Decision**: Approve. This is a high-value, resource-efficient use of our existing infrastructure that directly serves my domain concerns. The efficiency gains from not duplicating compliance work across projects are substantial, and it preserves researcher time for scientific work.\n\n**Promise score**: I'd give this around 82-85. It's clearly the right architectural approach from a resource-ef