is ambitious: novel compression techniques, merging regulatory necessity with interpretability research. That's a long-term R&D bet.\n- However, the lab has scarce resources. We need to actually implement compliance (which is mandatory), not explore whether \"novel compression techniques\" might work someday.\n- The language \"We will develop novel compression techniques\" and \"Our researchers will publish findings\" signals this is a multi-year research program, not an implementation project.\n- From my vantage point: we can meet Article 12's requirements with existing logging infrastructure (structured event logs, timestamps, database references). We don't need to reinvent how we log things at the activation level.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The lab likely already has logging capabilities or can implement standard structured logging for the events Article 12 requires.\n- This proposal would divert resources away from actually implementing compliance (which is a hard deadline) toward exploratory research that may or may not yield useful results.\n- \"High-dimensional activations\" logging is computationally expensive — this directly conflicts with the \"low-overhead\" framing but in practice, even compressed activation logs add significant computational and storage overhead to production systems.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis proposal, while intellectually interesting, misidentifies what Article 12 actually requires. The regulation calls for event-level logging (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, verifier identification) — not internal model state tracking or activation-level provenance. The proposal over-engineers the compliance response by framing it as a deep interpretability research problem rather than an engineering implementation task.\n\nFrom my resource-allocation perspective:\n- We have a hard regulatory requirement to implement (Article 12 logging). That should be done with standard, well-understood engineering approaches using existing infrastructure.\n- This proposal diverts scarce resources into novel R&D that isn't necessary for compliance and whose results are uncertain.\n- The \"scientific ambition\" here is not aligned with the actual regulatory ask. We'd be spending months/years on research when we could implement compliant logging in weeks/months with existing tools.\n\nHowever, I should note: there IS a kernel of legitimate concern — if our high-risk AI systems are complex enough that simple input-output logs don't capture \"events relevant for identifying situations that may result in risk\" (Article 12(2)(a)), then we might need more than basic logging. But the proposal's solution (high-dimensional activation compression) is far overkill and not what the regulation envisions.\n\nI'll reject this. It's scientifically interesting but misaligned with actual compliance needs, overly resource-intensive for uncertain returns, and doesn't effectively leverage existing infrastructure. From my vantage point, it's a research distraction from the practical implementation task at hand.\n\nPromise score: I'd give it maybe 25-30. There's intellectual value in understanding logging granularity trade-offs, but it's not what we need right now, it's expensive to pursue, and it solves a problem the regulation doesn't actually pose in that form. It's not worthless (the interpretability angle has long-term value), but I wouldn't commit implementation resources to it when compliance deadlines are approaching.\n\nLet me settle on 25.\n",
