face rather than N different implementations. This reduces compliance burden during audits.\n- The \"lifetime\" requirement in Article 12(1) is critical — the logging must persist for the entire lifetime of the system. A centralized library makes it easier to enforce retention policies and ensure logs aren't deleted prematurely.\n\n**Weaknesses / Concerns:**\n- A \"small task force\" might underestimate the complexity. Different high-risk AI systems have very different architectures. A truly \"plug-and-play\" module that works across all project types may be aspirational rather than practical. However, from a compliance perspective, even a framework that covers 80% of cases with documented deviations is better than each team rolling their own ad-hoc solution.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention the specific minimum fields in Article 12(3) for biometric systems or the three categories in Article 12(2). However, it says \"consistent adherence to Article 12\" which implies these would be built in.\n- There's a risk that R&D teams see this as a compliance tax and resist integration, leading to shadow implementations outside the framework. But from my vantage point, having a mandate for a single approved logging mechanism is actually what I'd want — it creates a clear compliance boundary.\n- The proposal doesn't mention log integrity, immutability, or access controls. Article 12 implies logs should be reliable records. However, this is an implementation detail that the task force would handle.\n- From institutional survival: if we get hit with a regulatory investigation and our logging is fragmented across N different implementations, each with its own quirks, that's a nightmare. A unified framework makes it defensible to say \"here is how we ensure Article 12 compliance institutionally.\"\n- Market reputation: being able to demonstrate to customers (especially those in regulated industries) that we have a systematic approach to AI logging strengthens trust and ma