sses Art. 12(1) directly.\n- System-wide approach ensures no gaps in coverage.\n\nConcerns/Weaknesses:\n- The proposal focuses heavily on *training* provenance (hyperparameters, dataset versions). Article 12 is primarily about *operational* logging — the \"automatic recording of events over the lifetime of the system.\" While training data is important for technical documentation (Art. 11), Art. 12(2) specifically requires logging that enables:\n  - Identifying risk situations (Art. 79(1)) or substantial modifications\n  - Post-market monitoring (Art. 72)\n  - Operation monitoring (Art. 26(5))\n  \n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention the specific minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems: start/end times of each use, reference databases, input data matches, and identification of natural persons in verification. This is a significant gap if any of our systems fall under that category.\n\n- \"Immutable ledger\" — while good for integrity, I need to consider data protection implications (GDPR). Logging runtime events could capture personal data. As compliance officer, I'd want to see how this interacts with data minimization principles.\n\n- The proposal says it's the \"primary technical mechanism for Article 12 compliance.\" This is ambitious but also risky — if the framework has gaps, we have no fallback. However, having a single primary mechanism is actually good from a compliance governance perspective (single source of truth).\n\n- \"Embedded in our core API\" — this is operationally sound; it means logging can't be bypassed by developers who might skip manual steps. This reduces the risk of non-compliance through developer error.\n\n- The proposal doesn't mention retention periods, access controls for regulators, or how logs will be made available to notified bodies during conformity assessments. These are practical compliance concerns but perhaps implementation details rather than conceptual gaps.\n\n- \"Across all high-risk experiments\" — goo