        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Unified Traceability Logging Framework\" - a standardized software wrapper/middleware layer that automatically captures system events, timestamps, and input metadata across all high-risk prototypes, implemented as a shared library in the primary development language, integrated into CI/CD pipeline, forcing logging to an immutable ledger.\n\nLet me assess this from my perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A middleware/wrapper approach is technically sound and well-understood in software engineering. Logging frameworks are mature technology (structured logging, event capture, etc.).\n- \"Immutable ledger\" - this adds complexity. An append-only log or WORM storage would be simpler and sufficient for Article 12 compliance. Calling it a \"ledger\" might imply blockchain-like infrastructure which would be overkill and slow down prototyping. However, \"immutable\" could simply mean append-only database logs with cryptographic chaining, which is feasible.\n- Forcing logging via middleware in the primary development language: technically viable but has implications. If we force it at the framework level, developers can't bypass it (good for compliance) but it adds coupling to a specific implementation (bad for prototyping speed if the framework itself becomes a bottleneck).\n- CI/CD integration is standard practice and technically straightforward.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The claim that it \"preserves prototyping speed by removing repetitive boilerplate code\" is partially true but potentially misleading. While you remove per-project logging boilerplate, you introduce:\n  - A dependency on the shared library (version management)\n  - Constraint: your system must fit within what the wrapper can capture\n  - Debugging complexity when the wrapper itself