cript pulling from MLflow's REST API or local storage, feeding into Jinja2 (or similar) templates that output PDF/HTML. This is maybe 1-3 weeks for a working v1.\n- The risk is scope creep: trying to make it cover ALL of Recital 71 vs. the subset that's actually structured in MLflow. A focused prototype covering the data-centric parts would be fast and useful.\n- Quality concern: regulatory documents need precision. An automated pipeline could introduce subtle errors if the mapping from experiment metadata to document fields isn't carefully validated. But this is manageable with proper unit tests on template rendering.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a technically sound, feasible engineering project. It leverages existing infrastructure (MLflow) which reduces implementation risk significantly. The prototyping path is clear and fast. The main caveat is that it won't cover 100% of Recital 71's requirements (narrative descriptions of capabilities/limitations, risk-management documentation), but the data-centric subset it covers is substantial and well-suited to automation.\n\nFrom my vantage point: this is a viable, buildable project with clear technical path and fast prototyping potential. I'd approve it. The promise score should reflect that it's solid engineering work that directly addresses part of Article 12/Recital 71, but not a magic bullet for the entire documentation requirement.\n\nI'll give it around 78-82. It's clearly viable and fast to prototype, leverages existing infrastructure, but has the inherent limitation that \"automated\" won't cover all narrative/documentation elements.\n",
