rns/Weaknesses:**\n- \"Adversarial compliance scenarios\" - this language suggests researchers creating synthetic failure modes. As a compliance officer, I need to be careful: are we creating artificial failures just for testing? That's actually fine and standard practice (penetration testing, red-teaming). It's not about deploying faulty systems; it's about verifying the logging infrastructure works under stress conditions.\n- The proposal is somewhat vague on scope - \"a large set of candidate proposals\" context suggests this might be one of many. But I judge each independently.\n- Resource commitment: building a simulation suite takes time and engineering resources. However, as compliance officer, my concern is institutional survival and regulatory compliance. If we don't verify our logging works, and a regulator finds gaps during audit, that's a much bigger cost (fines up to 7% of global turnover or €35M for prohibited practices; up to €15M/3% for other violations).\n\n**From my vantage point (compliance, institutional survival, market reputation):**\n- This is directly relevant to Article 12 compliance verification\n- It demonstrates proactive compliance culture to regulators\n- It protects against the scenario where our logging system has gaps we don't know about until an audit or incident occurs\n- The connection to Art 72 (post-market monitoring) and Art 79(1) (risk identification) is explicit in Article 12 itself\n- This is not a \"nice to have\" - it's verification that a mandatory technical requirement actually functions\n\nI think this clears the bar. It's a focused, directly compliance-relevant testing activity. The scope is well-defined (stress-test logging against Art 12 requirements). It doesn't require building new AI systems or exploring novel technology - it's quality assurance on an existing compliance obligation.\n\nDecision: Approve\nPromise: This is highly relevant to my domain. I'd give it a high score. Maybe 82-85. It's not a perfect propos