ately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\" The automatic snapshots on updates support this.\n\n**Strengths from compliance perspective:**\n- Directly supports Article 12(1) automatic logging requirement\n- The graph database approach linking datasets, code commits, and execution logs creates a comprehensive traceability chain\n- Automatic snapshots on updates address the \"kept up to date throughout lifetime\" requirement in Recital 71\n- Maps model iterations to training data — this is critical for compliance documentation under Article 12\n- Supports substantial modification detection (Article 12(2)(a)) by tracking what changed between versions\n- The \"seamless digital thread from raw data to final deployment\" addresses the traceability requirement\n\n**Concerns/limitations:**\n- The proposal focuses heavily on development/training provenance but doesn't explicitly address Article 12(3) requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification) — recording period of each use, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification. However, Article 12(3) is specific to biometric systems, and this proposal seems more about general high-risk system logging infrastructure.\n- The \"execution logs\" component addresses the operational logging (Article 12(1)), but it's not entirely clear how comprehensive these logs will be regarding the specific event types in Article 12(2)(a)-(c).\n- \"Real-time\" graph database linking is ambitious and could introduce its own compliance risks (data integrity, access control for logged data).\n- The proposal frames it as \"scientific reproducibility\" which is good but doesn't explicitly reference the regulatory obligations. However, from a compliance standpoint, if it achieves what Article 12 requires, the framing doesn't matter much.\n\n**Institutional survival / market reputation angle:**\n- Having a robust provenance pipeline would be a strong signal to regulators and market partners that the institution takes compliance seriously\n- It demonstrates proactive compliance rather than reactive\n- The \"seamless digital thread\" could be a competitive differentiator in EU markets where clients increasingly demand AI governance transparency\n\n**Resource commitment assessment:**\n- A graph database metadata layer with real-time linking of datasets, code commits, and execution logs is a significant engineering investment\n- However, if the institution is developing high-risk AI systems, this infrastructure is essentially mandatory for compliance — without it, they cannot demonstrate Article 12 compliance\n- The \"automatic snapshots\" feature adds operational overhead but is critical for the lifetime documentation requirement\n\n**Decision:** This proposal directly addresses core requirements of Article 12. It supports traceability, automatic logging, and up-to-date technical documentation throughout the system's lifetime. From a compliance standpoint, this is not optional — it's infrastructure that enables regulatory compliance. The graph database approach is sound for linking disparate elements (data, code, logs). I would approve this.\n\n**Promise score:** This is highly relevant to my function. It addresses Article 12(1), supports 12(2)(a) through version tracking, and the lifetime documentation requirement from Recital 71. However, it doesn't fully address all of Article 12 (particularly the specific operational logging events in 12(2)(b)-(c) or the biometric-specific requirements in 12(3)), but as a foundational infrastructure piece, it's strong. I'd give it around 85-90. It's very promising from a compliance angle but not a complete solution to all of Article 12 — it's more about development provenance than operational event logging. Let me settle on 87.\n",
