tners that the institution takes compliance seriously and has systemic controls in place. This is a positive signal for market trust.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a strong, practical proposal that addresses a core compliance obligation (technical documentation under Recital 71 / Art 11) with an appropriate technical approach. It doesn't fully cover all of Article 12's runtime logging requirements, but the regulatory text I was given includes both the recital and the article, and this proposal squarely addresses the documentation/traceability aspect. The integration with version control is smart for keeping docs current. The focus on reducing scientist burden is pragmatic — compliance that falls on researchers as manual paperwork will be inconsistently executed; automation makes it systemic.\n\nI would approve this. It's directly relevant to the regulatory text provided, addresses a real compliance gap, and has clear institutional value. My promise score should reflect high confidence from my vantage point but acknowledge it doesn't cover everything (it's documentation, not runtime logging). I'd put it around 82-85.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 83.\n",
