 comes from other parts of the Act (Annex I and Annex III). So the rubric would need to reference those provisions too. A slight misattribution that could lead to incorrect classifications if taken at face value.\n4. From resource-allocation efficiency: this is actually a net positive. Without such a rubric, you either over-apply compliance (wasting resources) or under-apply it (risking non-compliance). A structured triage mechanism is efficient use of limited engineering and storage budgets.\n5. The \"cross-functional committee\" aspect: from my perspective as Lab Director, I'm concerned about the overhead. But the alternative — no classification at all — would mean either every project gets full Article 12 treatment (expensive) or nobody knows which do (risky). A lightweight version of this could work well.\n\nOverall assessment: The core concept is valuable for resource efficiency and aligns with my mandate to balance ambition against funding constraints. However, the implementation as described (formal cross-functional committee at project onset) adds process overhead that may slow scientific work, and the regulatory framing has a minor inaccuracy (Article 12 doesn't define what's high-risk; Annex III does). The idea is good but needs refinement to be lean enough for an R&D context.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar — it addresses a real resource-allocation question and would prevent significant waste. But the promise score should reflect that it needs streamlining to avoid becoming bureaucratic overhead in a research institution. I'll approve with a moderate-high promise score, maybe 72-78 range. The core idea is strong for my domain but the implementation details as stated (committee at onset) add friction that could be reduced.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 74. It's clearly useful from a resource-allocation standpoint, the governance mechanism is reasonable in principle, but I'd want it to be lighter-weight than a formal committee review at every project 