s is a reasonable direction but the implementation as described (centralized immutable ledger capturing full database state per inference) seems like it could be resource-prohibitive for an R&D lab with funding constraints, especially if it means building net-new infrastructure rather than extending existing systems. However, the underlying need is real and the dual-use value (compliance + debugging) gives it merit.\n\nI think I'll approve it but at a moderate promise score — maybe 58-65. It addresses a real regulatory need with some engineering upside, but as scoped (immutable, per-call snapshots of full database state), it risks becoming a resource black hole that could be addressed more efficiently by extending existing infrastructure or implementing tiered logging.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"reference databases and input data matches\" — not the entire database every time. It's capturing which reference DB version was used and what the matching input data was. That's actually more bounded than I initially read it. Still, \"every specific inference call\" with immutable storage is a growing cost.\n\nI'll go with approve at around 60-63. It's necessary work (regulatory), has dual-use value, but the implementation approach as described risks over-investment in new infrastructure when existing R&D systems could be leveraged.\n",
