high-risk\" systems yet, or the scope might be overblown relative to actual regulatory obligations.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- This is pure compliance infrastructure with zero scientific output. It doesn't advance any research goal directly.\n- However, it's a prerequisite for deploying high-risk AI systems at all. Without it, you can't go to market with those systems. So it's not \"nice to have\" — it's table stakes.\n- The question is: does the lab actually have high-risk AI systems that need this NOW? If the lab's portfolio includes high-risk systems (Annex III category 1(a) - biometrics, critical infrastructure, etc.), then yes. If not, this might be premature.\n- From a funding constraint perspective, building a \"tamper-proof database\" and \"universal API wrappers\" is expensive. The proposal doesn't mention cost or phasing.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This could leverage existing MLOps/DevOps infrastructure rather than starting from scratch. Many labs already have telemetry pipelines (Prometheus, Grafana, ELK stack, etc.). The question is whether this builds ON those or replaces them.\n- \"Universal API wrappers\" suggests a new layer on top of existing systems, which could be efficient if it's thin, but wasteful if it duplicates functionality.\n- The proposal doesn't mention integration with existing infrastructure — it just says \"integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines.\" This is vague and could mean either good (leveraging what exists) or bad (building parallel systems).\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound: compliance should be a platform-level concern, not an individual researcher burden. The middleware approach is the right architectural pattern from a resource-efficiency standpoint — build once, serve many. This protects scientific researchers from being bogged down in compliance chores, preserving their capacity for actual research (balancing ambition vs. funding constraints).\n\nHowever, there are concerns:\n- \"Universal\" and \"mandatory for all production-ready models\" is very broad scope. Article 12 applies to HIGH-RISK systems specifically. Making this mandatory for ALL production-ready models might over-scope the project beyond regulatory necessity.\n- The proposal is somewhat vague on implementation specifics (what's the tamper-proof database? what existing infrastructure do we leverage?).\n- No mention of phasing or prioritization — does this all go in at once?\n\nBut fundamentally, from my vantage point: YES, this is a good use of resources. Centralizing compliance logging into a platform layer is more efficient than distributing the burden across research teams. It frees up scientific talent for actual science while ensuring regulatory compliance is handled systematically. The resource investment is justified IF the lab has or will have high-risk systems in its portfolio (which, given we're an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, it likely does).\n\nThe main reservation: the scope says \"all production-ready models\" which might be broader than what Article 12 requires. But as a platform investment, building it broadly and applying it selectively to high-risk systems is actually more efficient than scoping narrowly (you build once, can extend later).\n\nI'll approve this. It's a good resource-allocation decision: one centralized infrastructure investment vs. N distributed ad-hoc implementations. It frees scientific resources for actual research while ensuring compliance. Promise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's solid but the vagueness on implementation and the slightly over-broad scope (\"all production-ready models\" vs. \"high-risk systems\") keep it from being a perfect 100.\n\nLet me settle on 75.\n",
