ion boundary, systems with pre/post-processing that matters for compliance (e.g., input data checks against reference databases).\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The core value proposition — \"add a single decorator to your functions\" — is compelling for prototyping speed. If it works, it dramatically reduces the boilerplate each engineer needs to write.\n- However, the upfront cost of building this middleware is significant. It's essentially building an internal platform team's worth of infrastructure before any product work can happen.\n- Risk: if the middleware becomes a bottleneck or has bugs, every high-risk project is blocked waiting for the platform team to fix it. This creates a single point of failure that could slow down prototyping rather than speed it up.\n- For early prototypes where you're rapidly iterating on model architecture, having to route all telemetry through a centralized immutable database adds latency and complexity. You want your prototype loop to be fast.\n- The \"single decorator\" abstraction is seductive but may not hold in practice. Complex systems with multiple inference steps, conditional logic, fallback models, etc., will need more than a simple decorator pattern.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- A shared library means all projects get the same logging behavior — good for consistency and compliance audits.\n- But it also means one bug in the middleware affects all projects simultaneously.\n- The schema rigidity could be both a strength (consistency) and weakness (inability to capture project-specific events that Article 12(2)(a)-(c) might require).\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe idea has genuine merit — the decorator/wrapper pattern for reducing logging boilerplate is sound software engineering, and having a consistent schema helps with compliance. However, from a technical viability standpoint, the scope as described (\"across all high-risk projects,\" \"intercepts model calls\") is under-specified in ways that suggest it may