ment documentation.\n2. Article 12(1) specifically requires \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" - the proposal focuses more on technical documentation generation rather than the event logging mechanism itself. Though there's overlap.\n3. Article 12(2)(a)-(c) requires logs for identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations per Article 26(5). The proposal doesn't clearly address these specific logging requirements - it's more about documentation generation from experiment metadata.\n4. For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), there are specific minimum logging requirements in Article 12(3). This proposal doesn't mention those.\n5. \"Upon every model checkpoint\" - this could generate enormous volumes of documentation. Is that practical? Could it create compliance noise rather than clarity? But from a compliance standpoint, having more granular records is generally safer.\n6. The proposal says it \"ensures documentation stays current without slowing down the iterative R&D prototyping speed.\" This is good for operational feasibility, but I need to ensure the generated documentation meets the regulatory standard of being \"clear and comprehensive\" as Recital 71 states. Automated generation risks producing technically accurate but not necessarily \"comprehensible\" or \"clear\" documentation.\n7. The proposal doesn't mention access controls, retention periods, or audit trails for the documentation itself - all important for compliance.\n\n**From my Compliance Officer vantage point:**\n\nThis is a strong, well-targeted proposal that addresses a core obligation under Recital 71 (up-to-date technical documentation) and supports Article 12's traceability requirements. The use of existing infrastructure (MLflow/W&B) makes it feasible and cost-effective. The automatic trigger on checkpoints ensures the \"kept up to date\" requirement is met structurally rather than relying on human diligence.\n\nHowever, it has gaps - it