apabilities, AND limitations are not purely mechanical extractions from MLflow/W&B. Limitations especially require human judgment. Hyperparameters can be auto-extracted, yes.\n- The trigger model (model registry update) is reasonable for catching new models, but doesn't address the *operational* logging that Article 12(2)-(3) demands — start/end times of use, reference database checks, input data matching, verification personnel identification.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- From a prototyping standpoint, wiring MLflow/W&B to a documentation template via a CI trigger is a well-scoped sprint project. Maybe 1-2 sprints for an MVP. That's fast and achievable.\n- But it addresses a peripheral part of Article 12 (documentation currency) rather than the core requirement (runtime logging infrastructure). The actual Article 12(3) requirements — recording start/end times, reference database references, input data matches, verification personnel IDs — require building instrumentation into the *serving/inference* layer, not the training pipeline.\n- The proposal as written would give a false sense of compliance. It handles documentation metadata but doesn't implement the logging capabilities that Article 12 actually mandates.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe engineering approach is sound and technically viable for what it does. But from my vantage point, I'm concerned about:\n1. It misidentifies the core technical challenge of Article 12 (runtime logging vs. documentation metadata).\n2. The \"without adding manual overhead\" claim is overpromising — limitations sections won't auto-populate meaningfully.\n3. As a prototyping target, it's low-hanging fruit that doesn't address the hard part (inference-time event recording infrastructure).\n\nHowever, it IS technically viable as a component of a larger solution. It could be a useful piece. But as a standalone proposal for \"how to respond to Article 12,\" it's misaligned with what actually needs building.\n\nI think this is borderline. The technical approach works, the tooling choice is reasonable (MLflow/W&B are standard), and the CI/CD trigger pattern is clean. But it addresses maybe 20-30% of what Article 12 technically demands, and that's the easy part. From my prototyping vantage point, I'd want to see a proposal that tackles the inference-time logging architecture.\n\nI'll lean toward reject because:\n- It misidentifies the core technical challenge\n- The \"auto-populate limitations\" claim is not technically achievable in any meaningful way\n- It doesn't address the actual Article 12(1)-(3) runtime logging obligations\n- As a prototyping commitment, it would consume resources on a peripheral problem while the hard infrastructure (event logging at inference time) remains unaddressed\n\nPromise score: Maybe 40. The technical approach is sound for what it does, and it's a reasonable engineering pattern. But it solves the wrong problem from Article 12's perspective, and the limitations claim is technically unrealistic.\n",
