ng/telemetry tools in the lab rather than building from scratch. However, the proposal doesn't mention reusing anything - it says \"we will develop a middleware layer,\" suggesting new development.\n- The immutable database requirement is specific to audit needs and may not align with existing storage infrastructure.\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Correctly identifies that Article 12(1) requires technical capability for automatic logging, not just policy\n- Standardization reduces per-project compliance cost\n- \"By default\" framing means new projects inherit compliance without extra effort\n- Addresses the cross-cutting nature of the requirement\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- The scope is ambitious: a single library working across ALL high-risk AI models in different R&D streams is a big engineering commitment. Different model types (NLP, computer vision, tabular data) have very different telemetry needs.\n- \"Immutable database\" is expensive to operate over long lifetimes and may conflict with practical operational needs (e.g., debugging, data corrections for non-compliance-related issues).\n- The proposal doesn't address the specific minimum logging requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), which require recording of input data that matched searches and natural person identification. A generic middleware may not handle these domain-specific requirements well.\n- No mention of integration with existing lab infrastructure or phasing strategy.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a fundamentally sound approach - building shared compliance infrastructure is more resource-efficient than per-project solutions. However, the proposal as stated is somewhat over-scoped (\"all high-risk AI projects,\" \"across different models\") without acknowledging the heterogeneity of model types and the specific domain requirements in Article 12(3). The immutable database adds cost. But the core idea - a shared library so compliance isn't reinvented per project - aligns well with resource efficiency.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's the right architectural approach for a lab with multiple high-risk AI projects. The main concern is scope and execution risk, but the strategic direction is correct. I'd approve it but note that in practice it would need careful phasing and shouldn't be treated as a one-shot build.\n\nPromise score: From my resource-efficiency vantage point, this is genuinely valuable - a shared compliance layer prevents N projects from each building logging infrastructure. But the \"one library for all\" ambition introduces execution risk and potential over-engineering. I'd put it around 72-78. Let me settle on 74. It's clearly worth pursuing but not without reservation about scope management.\n",
