earch problem (true model interpretability).\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a research initiative, not an engineering deliverable. From my vantage point, I'm concerned about speed and quality of prototyping. A \"research project\" focused on \"novel probing mechanisms\" has no defined timeline, no clear prototype milestones, and high risk of producing nothing actionable within any reasonable timeframe.\n- The lab cannot commit scarce implementation resources to an open-ended research project when the actual regulatory requirement can be met with well-understood engineering techniques (structured logging, event capture, audit trails).\n- There's a risk of gold-plating: building an elaborate interpretability layer that no regulator actually requires, while the basic compliance logging (timestamps, inputs, outputs, reference DBs) remains unaddressed.\n\n**Does it clear my bar?**\n- From a technical viability standpoint: The core premise (recording internal decision-paths in human-comprehensible form) is not currently viable at production scale for most modern AI architectures. This is research-grade work with uncertain outcomes.\n- From a speed/quality prototyping standpoint: This doesn't give me a clear path to a working prototype that satisfies Article 12. It's an open-ended research bet. The actual compliance requirements (Art 12(1), 12(2), 12(3)) are well-defined and achievable with standard logging infrastructure.\n- The proposal doesn't address the specific technical requirements of Article 12 in a concrete way. It talks about \"interpretable logs\" and \"internal decision-paths\" which go far beyond what the regulation mandates (which is about recording events, inputs, outputs, timestamps, reference databases).\n\nMy assessment: This is an intellectually interesting idea but technically premature and misaligned with the actual regulatory requirement from a prototyping/viability standpoint. The regulation asks for event logging (achievable), not 