e templates during every model build\" — this is technically very doable. Template engines (Jinja2, etc.) combined with structured metadata extraction are well-trodden paths.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The core technical components (metadata extraction from VCS + experiment trackers, CI/CD integration, template rendering) are all mature technologies. A prototype could be built relatively quickly — weeks, not months, for a basic version.\n- The main engineering challenge is the mapping layer: how to structure the extraction so that it reliably populates the right fields in compliance templates. This requires understanding what the regulators actually want vs. what the tools naturally expose.\n- However, this is primarily an integration/configuration problem rather than a novel algorithmic challenge. It's very buildable with current tooling.\n- The \"every model build\" trigger means it needs to be robust — but CI/CD pipelines already have mechanisms for handling failures gracefully.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- The proposal somewhat overstates what can be fully automated. Recital 71 asks for documentation on \"capabilities and limitations\" and the \"risk-management system\" — these involve professional judgment that isn't purely derivable from hyperparameters and test scores. A pipeline can extract the *data*, but the *narrative interpretation* still needs human input.\n- However, even as a partial automation (extracting all the structured data and pre-filling templates), this is a high-value engineering project that would speed up prototyping cycles by removing documentation overhead.\n- The \"mapping R&D activity to Recital 71 requirements\" part requires some domain knowledge of what the regulation asks for, but that's a bounded specification problem, not an open-ended research challenge.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom a technical viability standpoint, this is very buildable with existing technology stacks. It's primarily an integration project connecting tools we likely already have to a documentation output. The prototype could be demonstrated quickly (the \"pull from VCS + experiment tracker → render template\" pipeline). The quality risk is moderate — the main uncertainty is how well automated extraction captures everything the regulation needs, but even partial automation has clear value.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar for approval. It's technically feasible with known tools, the prototyping path is clear and fast, and it directly reduces friction in our development cycle. The promise score should reflect that while I see it as very buildable, there are some gaps (narrative sections, risk-management documentation) that limit it from being a 100.\n\nI'd rate this around 82-85. It's technically solid, the prototyping path is clear and fast, uses mature tech, but the \"full automation\" framing slightly overstates what's achievable without human judgment on certain compliance narrative elements.\n",
