        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1334\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12(1) mandates that high-risk systems 'technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system,' and a standardized internal framework is precisely the architectural response this requires: it enforces consistent, tamper-resistant logging across all projects rather than leaving each team to improvise, which is where compliance gaps and audit failures typically emerge. The centralized approach also gives us a single verifiable surface for demonstrating traceability (Recital 71) during regulator inspections or post-market monitoring requests under Article 72, directly reducing enforcement exposure. My reservations are that the proposal does not yet address log immutability, GDPR-aligned data retention tied to system lifetime, access-control requirements for logs containing personal data, or how it encodes the specific minimum fields in Article 12(3) (reference database, matched input data, verifier identities) as non-optional schema constraints—but these are scoping details that belong in implementation, not disqualifications of the core concept.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen proposal P1334 as a Compliance Officer for an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, specifically regarding Article 12 (Record-keeping).\n\nLet me analyze this proposal from my professional vantage point: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is \"Unified Logging Framework for High-Risk Systems\" - a standardized internal software library providing automated event logging and timestamping across all high-risk R&D projects. It replaces ad hoc recording with a centralized system, includes a middleware layer that automatically captures inputs/outputs/user interactions, removes manual coding burden from researchers, ensures consistent compliance, and minimizes human error.\n\nFrom a Compliance Officer's perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Article 12(1) explicitly requires high-risk AI systems to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" A unified framework directly addresses this requirement at an architectural level rather than per-project.\n- Consistency across projects is a major compliance concern. If each team implements logging differently, there's a risk that some projects miss critical logging requirements (e.g., Article 12(3)(a)-(d) for Annex III point 1(a) systems). A standardized library enforces minimum requirements uniformly.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" is a long-duration requirement. A centralized framework is more maintainable and sustainable than scattered per-project implementations, reducing the risk that logs are lost or corrupted over time.\n- Reduces human error - compliance failures from missed logs could lead to enforcement actions under the EU AI Act (fines up to 7% of global turnover).\n- Facilitates traceability which is explicitly called out in Recital 71 as essential.\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n- \"Middleware layer\" that captures inputs, outputs, and user interactions - this needs to be carefully scoped. Article 12(2) says logging should capture events \"relevant for\" specific purposes (identifying risks, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operation). A blanket capture of ALL inputs/outputs might raise data protection concerns under GDPR, especially if personal data is involved. But the proposal doesn't specify what happens with those logs or how long they're retained - retention periods matter for compliance.\n- The proposal mentions it \"replaces ad hoc recording\" - but does it address Article 12(3) specific requirements (reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons in verification)? A unified framework should encode these as mandatory fields.\n- \"Removes the burden of manual coding from individual researchers\" - good for compliance consistency, but I need to ensure the framework is configurable enough to handle diverse high-risk system types.\n- The proposal doesn't mention log immutability, access controls, or how logs are preserved during substantial modifications or post-market monitoring events. These are critical for regulatory audits.\n- No mention of data retention periods aligned with system lifetime requirements.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a fundamentally sound and important compliance initiative. It directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) - automatic logging over system lifetime. The centralized approach reduces the risk of non-compliant implementations. However, it's somewhat high-level and doesn't address all the specific sub-requirements (Article 12(3), data protection considerations, retention, immutability). But as a framework/infrastructure proposal, it's the right architectural direction.\n\nFrom my vantage point (compliance, institutional survival, reputation):\n- This is a HIGH-value compliance investment. Without systematic logging, we're exposed to enforcement risk across all high-risk projects.\n- A unified approach means one audit surface rather than N different implementations to verify.\n- It demonstrates proactive compliance posture which helps market reputation and trust with regulators.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high - maybe 82-85. It's clearly aligned with Article 12 requirements, it's the right architectural approach, but I have some reservations about the completeness of the specification (data protection, retention, immutability) and whether \"middleware\" is the right technical characterization for all types of high-risk systems. It's a strong proposal that addresses the core compliance need, though it would need more detailed scoping to be fully sufficient.\n\nLet me settle on 83 - it's clearly the right direction and addresses a critical compliance gap, but needs refinement in specific areas before implementation.\n",
