iddleware\" framing suggests non-invasive integration.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- It explicitly builds on \"our core ML frameworks\" - leveraging what we already have rather than creating parallel systems.\n- Decorators and middleware are standard, lightweight patterns that don't require rearchitecting our stack.\n- This is an incremental addition to existing infrastructure, not a replacement.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Is the scope too narrow? Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems requires specific data points (reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification). The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input hashes, and version identifiers\" but doesn't explicitly address the reference database recording or human verifier identification. However, it says \"at a minimum\" these are what the SDK captures - the full Article 12(3) requirements would need to be addressed.\n- Actually, re-reading: the proposal says it records \"timestamps, input hashes, and version identifiers during runtime.\" This covers the general logging in Art. 12(1)-(2). For the specific Annex III 1(a) requirements (reference database, matched input data, verifier identification), those would need additional functionality. But the SDK architecture could be extended for that.\n- The proposal is reasonably scoped - it's a library, not a platform rebuild.\n\n**Verdict from my vantage point:**\nThis is exactly the kind of infrastructure investment I'd want to make. It:\n1. Amortizes compliance cost across all projects (efficiency)\n2. Protects research time by making logging automatic (preserves scientific ambition within constraints)\n3. Builds on existing frameworks rather than creating new parallel systems (effective use of existing infrastructure)\n4. Has a bounded, well-defined scope (a set of decorators and middleware - not an open-ended project)\n5. Reduces per-project compliance overhead going forward\n\nThe main risk is execution quality - if the SDK is poorly designed or too rigid, it becomes overhead rather than enabling. But the architectural pattern described (decorators, middleware) is sound and standard.\n\nI'd approve this. It's a high-leverage infrastructure investment with clear ROI across all our high-risk AI work. The promise score should be high but not 100 because:\n- The scope as stated may not fully cover Article 12(3) specific requirements for biometric systems (Annex III 1(a))\n- There's execution risk in getting the SDK right without becoming a bottleneck\n- It requires dedicated engineering time that could otherwise go to research\n\nI'd say around 82-85. Strong approval from my vantage point, with minor reservations about completeness of scope and execution risk.\n",
