yed high-risk systems over their full lifetime.\n\n**Key compliance concerns from my vantage point:**\n\n1. **Strengths:**\n   - Standardization is critical for compliance consistency. Having a single library ensures all teams follow the same logging protocol, reducing the risk of inconsistent or missing records.\n   - \"Automatic\" recording aligns with Article 12(1)'s requirement.\n   - Centralized secure storage backend addresses data integrity and availability requirements.\n   - Reduces burden on individual research teams, making it more likely they'll actually implement logging consistently.\n   - The API hooks and decorators approach is practical for R&D environments where developers want minimal disruption.\n\n2. **Concerns/Gaps:**\n   - The proposal says \"all high-risk AI prototypes\" — but Article 12 applies to the lifetime of the system, not just the prototype phase. I need to ensure this extends through deployment and post-market monitoring phases.\n   - No explicit mention of Article 12(3) specific requirements for biometric identification systems (Annex III 1(a)). The reference database capture is mentioned, but the verification person identification isn't clearly addressed.\n   - \"Without slowing down iterative prototyping\" — this is a tension. Compliance logging should not be an afterthought added only at deployment. If it's designed primarily for prototypes, there may be gaps when transitioning to production.\n   - No mention of log retention periods, access controls, or audit trails for the logs themselves (meta-compliance).\n   - The proposal doesn't address what happens if a researcher bypasses the middleware or uses it incompletely — no enforcement mechanism mentioned.\n   - \"Centralized, secure storage backend\" is vague from a compliance perspective — where is data stored? GDPR implications? Retention policies?\n\n3. **Institutional survival angle:**\n   - A standardized approach reduces legal risk significantly. If every system logs consistently, we're much better positioned for regulatory audits.\n   - Market reputation: demonstrating proactive, systematic compliance infrastructure signals to partners and regulators that the institution takes AI governance seriously.\n   - However, if this middleware has a bug or gap that causes a specific logging failure across ALL systems simultaneously (single point of failure), that's a significant institutional risk.\n\n4. **My assessment:**\n   This is fundamentally aligned with what Article 12 requires. It's the kind of infrastructure investment that a compliance officer would strongly support because it systematizes a regulatory obligation rather than relying on individual team diligence. The main concerns are: (a) scope limited to \"prototypes\" vs full lifetime, (b) no explicit coverage of Annex III 1(a) specific requirements, (c) single point of failure risk, (d) lack of enforcement mechanism.\n\n   However, as a proposal for implementation resource commitment, this is the RIGHT direction. It's not a perfect specification, but it identifies the correct architectural solution. The gaps can be addressed in detailed design. The core value proposition — standardization, automation, consistency — is exactly what compliance requires.\n\n   I would approve this because:\n   - It directly implements Article 12(1) and most of 12(2)\n   - Standardization reduces compliance risk across the institution\n   - It's resource-efficient (one library serves all teams vs. each team building their own logging)\n   - The gaps are addressable in implementation without changing the fundamental architecture\n\n   But my promise score should reflect that there are meaningful gaps to close before this is fully compliant-ready. I'd put it around 72-78. It's a strong foundation with identifiable compliance gaps that need addressing but don't invalidate the approach.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 75. The direction is correct and strongly aligned with my regulatory mandate, but the proposal as stated has scope limitations (prototypes vs lifetime) and specific requirement gaps (Annex III 1(a), verification person ID, log integrity/retention) that would need to be addressed in implementation. From a pure \"should we commit resources to this\" perspective, yes — because building custom logging per-team is worse for compliance than a standardized approach with known gaps we can fix.\n",
