uld judge it on its own merits for what it claims to do.\n- \"Integrated directly into the user interface\" — from a compliance perspective, UI integration is good because it ensures the logging happens at point-of-use rather than being a separate system that could be bypassed. This reduces the risk of gaps in the audit trail.\n- The proposal says it's for \"high-risk biometric AI systems\" which aligns with Annex III 1(a) scope.\n- Secure digital signatures go beyond the minimum requirement (which just says \"identification\") but this is good from a compliance robustness standpoint — it prevents disputes about who actually performed the verification, which matters in regulatory audits and potential litigation.\n- From institutional survival perspective: having a clean, auditable record of human oversight is critical if there's any incident or regulatory inquiry. This protects the institution.\n- From market reputation: demonstrable compliance with specific sub-requirements builds trust with regulators and enterprise clients who will demand proof of oversight.\n\nThis is a well-scoped, directly relevant compliance measure that addresses a specific letter-of-the-law requirement. It's not over-engineered (it's focused on one specific obligation), it's technically sound (digital signatures + access logs are standard, proven technology), and it maps cleanly to the regulatory text.\n\nFrom my vantage point:\n- Regulatory compliance: Directly satisfies 12(3)(d). Strong alignment.\n- Institutional survival: Protects against regulatory non-compliance findings specifically on human oversight traceability. If a regulator audits and can't identify who verified results, that's a clear violation with potential penalties.\n- Market reputation: Demonstrates thorough compliance implementation to clients and regulators.\n\nThis is a solid proposal. I'd approve it. The promise score should reflect strong confidence — this is exactly the kind of targeted, specific compliance measure 