ferent formats. You'd need standardization of log schemas first, which is a prerequisite dependency. But that's manageable.\n- ELK is heavy for a small team but very well-understood. Setup time is predictable (days to weeks for basic deployment).\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal conflates prototyping-phase utility with regulatory compliance requirements. From my perspective, the prototyping benefit (faster failure detection) is legitimate and separable from the full Article 12 compliance implementation. The dashboard as a dev tool for catching issues during prototyping is viable and useful.\n- \"Flags anomalies based on Article 12 criteria\" - this requires understanding what those criteria are technically detectable. Some of them (like identifying situations presenting risk under Art 79(1)) might be hard to automate without domain-specific logic. But basic logging of start/end times, reference databases used, input data matches (per Art 12(3) for biometric systems) are straightforward log fields.\n- The \"across all active high-risk systems\" is ambitious if the lab has many heterogeneous systems. But even a single-system deployment would be viable as a first prototype.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is technically very feasible. ELK is a standard choice, the scope is well-defined enough to start prototyping immediately, and it directly accelerates the development iteration cycle by making log analysis faster. The main risk is scope creep trying to cover \"all active high-risk systems\" simultaneously rather than starting with one. But as a prototype, building the pipeline for one system and extending later is standard practice.\n\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping), this is a solid, low-risk proposal that delivers immediate prototyping value. I'd approve it. The promise score: ELK dashboards are very well-trodden territory technically, the use case is clear, and it directly serves prototyping velocity. I'd say around 72-78. It's not 90+ because \"across all active high-risk systems\" in a single dashboard adds integration complexity that could slow initial prototyping if log schemas aren't standardized yet. But it's clearly viable and useful.\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 74.\n",
