es existing infrastructure (the institution presumably already has data storage/archiving systems).\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Automatically pipe these logs to a secure institutional archive\" — what about the cost of that archive? Storage for full input data over the lifetime of all high-risk systems could be substantial. But this is an operational cost, not a capital one, and it's necessary regardless of approach.\n- The proposal says \"total regulatory adherence\" which is a strong claim. Article 12(3) requires specific things for Annex III point 1(a) systems (recording of period of use, reference database, input data matched, identification of natural persons in verification). A generic middleware might not capture all project-specific elements. But the proposal frames it as a \"minimum\" layer that could be extended per-project.\n- Integration into \"core ML framework\" — this requires engineering resources to build and maintain. But compared to asking every project team to implement logging independently, it's far more efficient.\n- Risk: if the core ML framework changes or new frameworks are adopted, the middleware needs updating. But again, centralized maintenance is better than distributed.\n\n**Assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a very well-aligned proposal with resource-allocation efficiency. It's a build-once-deploy-many approach to compliance infrastructure. It leverages existing pipelines rather than creating parallel systems. It frees researcher time (the scarcest resource) for actual science. The main risk is the initial engineering investment, but that's amortized across all high-risk projects and compared to the alternative (each project team handling its own logging or non-compliance penalties), it's clearly superior.\n\nThe \"transparent middleware\" framing is smart — it doesn't disrupt existing R&D workflows, which preserves scientific ambition while adding compliance. This directly addresses my concern about balancing scientific ambition 