umentation.\" This is about generating the *documentation* that Recital 71 calls for (the comprehensive technical file), not about implementing the automatic logging capability required by Article 12(1). These are related but distinct obligations.\n\nStill, from my vantage point:\n- It uses existing R&D infrastructure effectively (Git, MLflow already deployed)\n- It reduces administrative overhead on scientists — directly improving resource allocation efficiency\n- The cost to implement is moderate (a software pipeline) but the ongoing savings in scientist time are significant\n- It's a practical, well-scoped project with clear deliverables\n- It doesn't require new funding sources or major infrastructure investment\n\nThe main concern: it addresses documentation generation but not the core Article 12 logging requirement. However, Recital 71 explicitly calls for this documentation to be \"kept up to date\" and in a \"clear and comprehensive form,\" so automating its generation is genuinely valuable. It's complementary to (not a substitute for) the actual logging infrastructure, but it's still a legitimate efficiency improvement.\n\nFrom my perspective as Lab Director:\n- This is a good use of existing infrastructure\n- It frees up scientist time (expensive resource) for actual research\n- Implementation cost is moderate and manageable\n- The ROI in terms of reduced administrative burden is clear\n- It's well-scoped and achievable without major funding\n\nI'd lean toward approving this. It's not the most critical item (the actual logging infrastructure per Article 12(1) would be more urgent), but it's a good efficiency play that uses what we already have. The promise score should reflect that it's valuable but not the single highest priority — maybe around 70-75.\n\nLet me settle on my assessment:\n- Rationale: It effectively leverages existing Git/MLflow infrastructure to automate a recurring documentation burden, freeing scientist FTE hours for research while maint