ses harm, the first thing regulators (and plaintiffs) will ask for is the technical file and logs showing compliance was maintained over time. Without this protocol, we're exposed.\n\n**Weaknesses/Considerations:**\n- The proposal focuses primarily on Recital 71 rather than Article 12 itself. Recitals are interpretive guidance, not directly enforceable obligations (though they inform interpretation of the binding articles). However, the practical content aligns with what Article 12 requires operationally.\n- \"Quarterly\" audits — is this sufficient or appropriate? For a fast-moving R&D institution, quarterly might be too infrequent if systems are deployed and evolving rapidly. But it's a reasonable starting cadence.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address the logging capabilities required by Article 12(1) (automatic recording of events) — it focuses on documentation currency rather than the technical implementation of logs. However, \"verifying the link between logs and technical specifications\" does implicitly engage with this.\n- It's a governance process, not a technical specification. As a compliance officer, I need both the governance layer AND the technical implementation. But as a proposal to pursue, establishing the governance framework is necessary even if it doesn't cover everything.\n\n**Assessment from Compliance Officer perspective:**\nThis is squarely in my domain. The EU AI Act makes record-keeping and documentation maintenance an explicit obligation for high-risk systems. Failing to maintain current technical documentation throughout the lifetime of a system would be a direct regulatory violation. Having a formal, auditable protocol for doing this is not just good practice — it's effectively mandatory for demonstrating compliance. The \"evidentiary audit trail\" framing shows understanding that in enforcement or litigation, the burden will be on us to prove we complied.\n\nThis proposal protects institutional survival (by providing defense again