        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2317 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Dynamic Living Documentation Protocol\" — a system for real-time, version-controlled technical documentation that evolves automatically alongside model training/validation processes. It integrates automated doc-generation tools into existing Git workflows to link code commits to risk-management assessments. It ensures intellectual merit is preserved through traceable history of scientific decisions.\n\nLet me assess this from my domain: technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Version-controlled documentation linked to Git workflows is well-understood engineering practice. Tools like Sphinx, MkDocs, Doxygen, or custom scripts can already generate docs from code commits. This is technically feasible today.\n- \"Real-time\" doc generation alongside training/validation processes is more nuanced. Training pipelines are long-running; hooking into them for doc updates is doable but adds complexity to the pipeline. However, it's not a novel technical challenge — CI/CD systems already trigger documentation builds on commits.\n- Linking code commits to risk-management assessments: this requires defining what \"risk-management assessment\" means in a machine-readable form and creating a mapping schema. This is feasible but the schema design is where complexity lives. It's not a stretch, but it's non-trivial work.\n- The \"evolves automatically alongside\" part implies some NLP or structured output from training/validation logs to generate prose documentation. This is harder. Auto-generating meaningful technical documentation (not just API docs) from training runs is an unsolved problem in general, though for specific structured outputs (hyperparameters, loss curves, validation metrics), it's straightforward.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The proposal frames this as a protocol/system to build. From a prototyping speed perspective, the core mechanism (Git-integrated doc generation) is a well-trodden path. You could prototype the basic version in days to weeks.\n- However, \"real-time\" and \"evolves automatically alongside training and validation processes\" adds significant scope. Training runs can take hours/days; \"real-time\" here likely means \"at each checkpoint\" rather than literally real-time. Still, integrating with diverse training pipelines (PyTorch, JAX, etc.) adds friction to prototyping speed.\n- The risk-management assessment linkage is where this gets less clear technically. What's the data model? Who writes the assessments? Are they structured or free-text? If free-text, auto-linking is hard. If structured (e.g., tagged with commit hashes), it's straightforward but requires process discipline.\n- For prototyping quality: the basic version (commit → doc page) would be high quality and immediately useful. The \"living\" aspect that truly auto-generates meaningful narrative from training data is where quality drops without significant ML/NLP work.\n\n**Relevance to Article 12:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events (logs). This proposal is more about documentation than logging per se, but Recital 71 explicitly mentions \"keeping records and the availability of technical documentation\" as part of the record-keeping obligation. So it's adjacent to the requirement.\n- The regulation says documentation should include \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used\" — this proposal targets exactly that.\n- However, Article 12 is specifically about LOGGING (automatic recording of events), while this proposal is more about TECHNICAL DOCUMENTATION (Article 11/Annex IV territory). There's overlap but the core ask here is documentation infrastructure, not logging infrastructure.\n\n**My Assessment:**\nFrom a technical viability standpoint, the core idea (Git-integrated version-controlled docs) is sound and well-understood. The harder parts (auto-generating meaningful narrative from training runs, linking to risk assessments) are feasible but require careful design. It's not a moonshot — it's an engineering project with clear scope if you define boundaries.\n\nFrom a speed/quality prototyping standpoint: the MVP (commit-linked doc pages with auto-generated metadata from CI artifacts) is very achievable quickly. The full \"living documentation that evolves automatically\" vision has longer tail risk but doesn't block getting value early.\n\nThe phrase \"intellectual merit is preserved through a clear, traceable, and permanent history of every key scientific decision\" is somewhat grandiose and vague in terms of what it actually delivers technically. What does the system do differently from `git log` + a well-structured README per experiment? The value-add over standard MLOps documentation practices (MLflow, Weights & Biases, DVC) needs to be clearer.\n\nOverall: It's a reasonable engineering project, technically viable in its core form, but somewhat vague in scope and the \"automatically evolves\" part introduces complexity that could slow prototyping if not bounded. I'd lean toward approve with moderate promise — it's feasible and useful, but the framing is broader than what's immediately buildable, and there's overlap with existing MLOps tools that might make the marginal value question important.\n\nLet me settle on a decision: The core technical approach (Git-integrated doc generation) is viable and I could prototype it quickly. The \"automatic evolution alongside training\" part is where I'd push back on scope. But as stated, it's not technically infeasible — just needs scoping. From my vantage point of \"can we build this well and fast,\" the answer is: yes for the core, with caveats on the full vision.\n\nI'll approve it but give a moderate promise score reflecting that