pective:**\n1. The proposal mentions \"input data, reference databases, and verification timestamps\" but doesn't explicitly mention recording \"the period of each use (start date/time and end date/time)\" per 12(3)(a). However, this seems like a minor omission that could be addressed in implementation — the framework's architecture would presumably capture session boundaries.\n\n2. The proposal mentions logging for Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically? Actually, it says \"all high-risk models\" which is broader. Article 12(3)'s minimum requirements apply specifically to Annex III 1(a) systems (biometrics). For other high-risk systems under 12(2), the requirements are more principle-based. The proposal seems to cover both by being comprehensive, but I should note whether it correctly distinguishes the tiered obligations.\n\n3. \"High-fidelity datasets necessary for deep failure analysis and scientific rigor\" — this is a research benefit dressed in compliance language. From my vantage point, I don't care about scientific rigor per se; I care that the logging meets regulatory requirements. However, if the framework produces data useful for both purposes, that's efficient resource use.\n\n4. The proposal doesn't explicitly address: log retention periods, access controls (who can view/modify logs), integrity of logs (tamper-evidence), or how logs feed into post-market monitoring (Article 72) and the risk reporting in Article 79(1). These are important compliance considerations but might be implementation details rather than architectural decisions.\n\n5. \"Standardized software layer\" and \"unified API\" — from a compliance standpoint, standardization is good because it reduces variance across systems, makes audits more predictable, and creates a single point of failure (which can be both a strength for compliance monitoring and a risk). \n\n6. The proposal commits implementation resources to building this infrastructure. From my vantage point, this is a necessar