mous public and regulatory scrutiny. Having demonstrable compliance infrastructure protects against enforcement actions, fines, and reputational damage.\n\n**Concerns:**\n1. The proposal says \"input data sources\" but Article 12(3)(b) specifically requires \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system.\" This is a specific technical requirement about which database was used for matching, not just where data came from. However, this could be a simplification in the proposal text rather than a fundamental error in design.\n2. The scope is narrow — only covers Annex III 1(a). But that's appropriate as a specialized module; other systems would need their own compliance tools.\n3. It says \"exact match timestamps\" but Article 12(3)(a) requires \"start date and time AND end date and time of each use.\" This is slightly different — it's about the period of use, not just when a match occurred. However, this could be interpreted as part of the logging requirement.\n4. The proposal focuses on biometric identification specifically (Annex III 1(a)), which covers \"biometric categorisation\" and \"remote biometric identification.\" Actually, let me recheck: Annex III point 1 is about \"Biometrics\" — 1(a) is remote biometric identification of natural persons; 1(b) is biometric categorisation. So the proposal correctly identifies this as the most stringent area.\n5. From a compliance standpoint, this is genuinely important. The EU will be watching biometric systems very closely, and having demonstrable Article 12(3) compliance is critical for market access in that sector.\n\n**Compliance Officer assessment:**\n- This directly addresses a legal obligation (Article 12(3)) with specific minimum requirements. Non-compliance here would mean the system cannot legally operate as a high-risk AI system in the EU.\n- The institutional survival angle: if we deploy biometric identification systems without proper Article 12(3) logging, we face enforcement 