nStrengths:\n1. Directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) — automatic recording of events over lifetime\n2. Standardization across projects reduces risk of non-compliant gaps — if one team forgets to implement logging properly, the framework ensures baseline compliance\n3. Immutability of logs is critical for regulatory audit trail and traceability (Recital 71 mentions \"comprehensible information\" and \"traceability\")\n4. Consistent formatting helps with post-market monitoring documentation and demonstrates systematic compliance to regulators\n5. Reduces institutional risk — a single point of failure in compliance vs. N different implementations across projects\n6. The middleware approach means researchers don't need to be compliance experts — reduces human error\n7. \"Over the lifetime of the system\" is addressed by the framework being persistent infrastructure\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n1. The proposal says it will \"track session durations and database references\" but doesn't explicitly mention all Article 12(3) requirements (input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification). This might be a scope gap.\n2. \"Immutable\" logs — good for integrity but need to consider what happens when the system is modified; the framework needs to handle versioning.\n3. The proposal doesn't explicitly mention retention periods or how logs are stored over the full lifetime (could be years/decades).\n4. It's a software engineering solution — as compliance officer, I'd want assurance it handles edge cases and that there's governance around who can modify the logging rules within the framework.\n\nHowever, from my vantage point: This is a fundamentally sound approach to ensuring institutional compliance with Article 12. The standardization reduces the risk of individual projects having gaps in their logging. The immutability aspect supports audit readiness. The middleware approach is practical for an R&D institution where researchers are not 