uations presenting risk or substantial modification\n   - Art 12(2)(b): facilitating post-market monitoring\n   - Art 12(2)(c): monitoring operation per Article 26(5)\n   - Art 12(3)(c): input data for which search led to a match\n   - Art 12(3)(d): identification of natural persons involved in verification (Art 14(5))\n   \n   The proposal seems narrow, focusing mainly on timestamps and reference database IDs. It doesn't explicitly mention checking for all the other required fields (input data matches, identification of verifying persons, etc.). However, it does say \"all mandatory fields specified in Article 12\" broadly, so perhaps the specific examples are just illustrative.\n\n2. The proposal focuses on the \"validation phase\" - but Article 12 requires logging over the ENTIRE LIFETIME of the system (Art 12(1)). A pre-deployment gate is useful but doesn't address ongoing compliance monitoring. However, as a validation tool, it's checking that the SYSTEM will produce proper logs going forward, not just validating historical logs. That's actually reasonable - you validate the logging capability before deployment.\n\n3. It says \"scanning script\" - this seems like a relatively lightweight implementation. For a robust compliance function, I'd want more than a single scanning script. But as an initial tool, it could be a good starting point.\n\n4. From institutional survival perspective: having automated pre-deployment compliance gates is essential. If we deploy a high-risk AI system that doesn't properly log events, we face:\n   - Fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices; up to €15M or 3% for other violations including Art 12)\n   - Reputational damage with regulators and clients\n   - Potential orders to withdraw systems from market\n\n5. From regulatory compliance perspective: This directly supports our obligation under Article 12. Having evidence that we validated logging capabilities pre-deployment is a strong compliance posture. It 