        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2641 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Immutable Data Lineage Pipeline\" — a system to track and link specific versions of reference databases to individual model inferences in real time, integrating a version-control layer for datasets that assigns a unique hash to every input match, storing these hashes within event logs. It claims this satisfies Annex III record-keeping mandates regarding database references and input data matches.\n\nLet me assess from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The core idea is sound: versioning reference databases and linking them to specific inference events via content hashes. This is a well-understood pattern in software engineering (content-addressable storage, git-like versioning for datasets).\n- \"Immutable\" data lineage is technically straightforward — you hash the database snapshot at query time and store that hash alongside the log entry. No exotic technology required.\n- \"Real-time\" integration: This adds some complexity. You need to ensure the hash of the reference DB is captured atomically with the inference event. If the DB is large, computing a full content hash per query could be expensive. However, if you version the DB (e.g., assign a version ID or hash at each update), then each query just records which version was active — that's O(1) lookup, very feasible.\n- Storing hashes in event logs: trivial. The log schema already needs to capture events; adding a database_version_hash field is straightforward.\n- \"Immutable\" aspect: This means once logged, the record can't be altered. You could use append-only storage or write-once semantics. Technically viable but adds some operational considerations (storage growth, retention policies).\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a well