ogging were left to individual projects, it could consume significant researcher/engineer time that would otherwise go into actual R&D. A shared framework frees up that capacity for scientific work.\n\nArguments against / concerns:\n- **Build cost and complexity**: Creating a \"unified\" middleware that works across \"different AI architectures\" is non-trivial. The lab might have heterogeneous systems (traditional ML, LLMs, multimodal models, edge deployments). A truly universal interceptor could be expensive to build and maintain. There's risk of scope creep or the framework becoming a bottleneck.\n- **\"Immutable ledger\"** — this adds infrastructure cost (blockchain? append-only database?) that may not be strictly required by Article 12. The Act requires \"automatic recording of events\" but doesn't mandate immutability per se. This could be over-engineering from a resource-efficiency standpoint, adding complexity without proportional regulatory benefit.\n- **Maintenance burden**: A shared middleware becomes critical infrastructure. If it breaks or needs updating for new model architectures, all projects are affected. This concentrates risk and creates an ongoing maintenance cost that must be budgeted.\n- **\"Guaranteeing regulatory compliance\"** is a strong claim. No single logging layer can guarantee full Article 12(3) compliance (which requires specific data elements like reference database identification, input data for matches, verification person IDs). The framework might handle timestamps and hashes but the domain-specific fields would still need per-project configuration.\n- **Opportunity cost**: The engineering resources to build this could alternatively be allocated to other priorities. However, given that Article 12 applies to ALL high-risk systems in the lab, the recurring per-project cost likely exceeds the one-time build cost.\n\n**Net assessment from my vantage point:**\nThe core idea — a shared logging middleware to avoid duplicative compliance 