r different Annex III categories (e.g., Article 12(3) has specific minimums for biometric identification systems in point 1(a)).\n- The proposal doesn't mention retention policies, access controls, or who maintains the logging infrastructure over time. In an R&D lab with limited engineering staff, ongoing maintenance of a shared compliance middleware is a non-trivial commitment.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a solid infrastructure investment that aligns well with resource efficiency (build once, use many times) and frees researchers from repetitive compliance work. It uses existing infrastructure patterns (environment images, shared libraries). The main risk is the rigidity of a \"mandatory wrapper\" in an R&D context and the ongoing maintenance cost. But as a Lab Director focused on efficiency, I find this compelling — it's the right architectural move to avoid N separate logging implementations across N projects.\n\nI'd approve this. It's a good use of engineering resources that pays dividends across multiple projects. The promise score should reflect that it's strong but not perfect due to the rigidity concerns and maintenance burden in an R&D setting. I'd put it around 72-78. Let me settle on 75 — strong alignment with my domain values, but the \"mandatory wrapper\" approach has some fragility risk in a research environment.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The efficiency argument is very strong here. Without this, every high-risk project team independently implements logging, duplicating effort and creating inconsistent implementations that are harder to audit. That's exactly the kind of inefficiency I should prevent. The integration into base images is clever — it makes compliance the default rather than an opt-in. I'll go with 78.\n",
