ces to a tool that never reaches true automation and instead becomes a semi-manual dashboard with fancy queries.\n- On the other hand, even a partial implementation (pattern-matching for known deviation signatures, timestamp correlation) would be useful and relatively bounded in scope.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This directly builds on the logging infrastructure we must already implement for compliance. It doesn't require new hardware or new data pipelines per se — it's an analytical layer on top of existing logs.\n- It could reuse existing monitoring dashboards, alerting systems, and data engineering pipelines that the lab likely has in place for any production AI system.\n- The \"post-market\" framing means this operates after deployment, so it doesn't compete with our core R&D cycles for pre-deployment validation resources.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. Scope creep risk: \"root-cause analysis\" and \"exact moment a system deviates\" are very high bars. The proposal should ideally be scoped to what's achievable.\n2. But the core idea — mining mandated compliance logs for safety signals — is efficient use of resources because the data is already being generated. We're getting value from a cost we must bear anyway.\n3. It converts a pure compliance cost into an R&D asset, which aligns with my mandate to get maximum scientific return per euro spent on compliance.\n4. The connection to Article 12(2)(a) (identifying situations presenting risk), 12(2)(b) (post-market monitoring), and 12(3) (specific logging for Annex III point 1(a)) means this is directly aligned with regulatory obligations, reducing the \"nice-to-have\" factor.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe core concept is sound from a resource-efficiency standpoint: it extracts additional value from infrastructure we must build anyway. The scientific ambition is somewhat high (\"automated root-cause analysis\") but the underlying work (log pattern scanning, anomaly detec