sal from a compliance perspective. It directly implements the core requirement of Article 12(1) and supports Articles 12(2)(a)-(c). The immutable, decoupled logging is exactly what a compliance officer would want to see — it ensures logs can't be altered after the fact, which is critical for audit and regulatory inspection.\n\nThe main gap I'd note is that it should explicitly cover the Article 12(3) minimums for specific Annex III categories, but the proposal's language (\"specific data points required by Article 12\") suggests this is intended. The modular design also allows for project-specific additions.\n\nDecision: Approve. This is squarely in my domain and addresses a core regulatory obligation. It reduces compliance risk institution-wide.\n\nPromise score: I'd give this a high score. It's directly implementing a mandatory requirement (Article 12(1) uses \"shall\"), it's standardized (reducing per-project risk), it has tamper resistance, and it scales across projects. The main uncertainty is whether the implementation will be thorough enough to cover all edge cases in Article 12(3) for specific Annex III systems, but the framework approach addresses this by design. I'd say around 85-90. Let me settle on 87 — it's very strong but there are implementation details (specific data points per Annex III category, retention periods, access controls for regulators vs. tamper prevention) that need to be nailed down during development.\n",
