        "reasoning_content": "Let me evaluate proposal P1104 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal: \"Integrated Compliance Documentation Templates\" — living technical documentation templates in version control (Git), using Markdown-based templates that require researchers to update specific compliance fields during every major code commit or model iteration. The goal is to weave documentation into the natural development lifecycle to minimize administrative paperwork time.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point:\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- Embedding documentation templates in version control (Git) is technically straightforward and well-understood. Markdown files in a repo are trivially implementable.\n- However, \"mandatory\" fields enforced during code commits introduces friction into the development workflow. This requires either pre-commit hooks, CI/CD pipeline checks, or PR review gates — all of which add complexity to the build system.\n- The challenge: AI model training iterations (fine-tuning runs, hyperparameter sweeps, data pipeline changes) don't always map cleanly onto \"code commits.\" A single model iteration might involve hundreds of small parameter tweaks, multiple data processing steps, and inference-time configurations that don't live in a Git repo at all.\n- Tracking \"algorithms, data sources, and risk assessments\" through Markdown templates is feasible for static documentation but becomes unwieldy for dynamic systems where the algorithm changes with each training run.\n- The idea of \"living\" documentation tied to commits has merit — it's essentially what good README/documentation practices already do, just enforced more rigorously.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- This is where I have the biggest concern. Requiring researchers to fill out compliance fields during every major code commit or model iteration will 