        "reasoning_content": "The proposal P1079 is about conducting a \"High-Risk System Classification Audit\" — essentially determining which of the lab's AI systems actually fall under the high-risk categories in Annex III of the EU AI Act, so that logging resources (and other compliance efforts) are only allocated where legally required.\n\nLet me assess this from my vantage point as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\n**My professional lens:**\n- Technical viability: Does this proposal describe something technically sound and implementable?\n- Speed/quality of prototyping: Does this help or hinder the ability to prototype quickly and well?\n\n**Analysis of P1079:**\n\nThis is fundamentally a scoping/governance activity, not an engineering implementation. It's about classification — determining which systems are in scope for Article 12 obligations before you start building logging infrastructure.\n\nFrom my perspective as a development engineer:\n- This is actually very valuable from a prototyping standpoint. If I don't know whether my system is high-risk, I either (a) over-engineer by adding full Article 12 compliance logging to every system regardless of need, wasting engineering time and complicating prototypes, or (b) under-engineer and then have to retrofit expensive logging later when classification says I needed it. Both are bad for prototyping speed and quality.\n- The \"cross-functional panel\" approach with legal experts is appropriate — classification is a legal question first, technical second. But the output (a \"mandatory classification registry\") gives me as an engineer a clear signal: \"this system needs logging, this one doesn't.\" That's a concrete input I can act on.\n- The risk here is process bloat: convening panels, producing registries, making things \"mandatory\" — these are governance overheads that could slow down prototyping if they become bureaucratic gatekeepers. However, the scope is limited to an audit/review exercise, not an ongoing approval mechanism. It's a one-time (or periodic) classification step.\n- The technical viability is straightforward: you look at your systems, compare them against Annex III criteria, and document the mapping. There's no novel engineering challenge here. It's feasible.\n- For speed/quality of prototyping: this actually HELPS. By defining scope early, engineers can prototype knowing whether logging infrastructure needs to be built in from day one or not. Retrofitting logging into a system after it's been deployed is far more expensive and disruptive than designing it in. So getting classification right upfront supports better architectural decisions during development.\n\nHowever, I should note: this isn't really an engineering proposal per se — it's a governance/legal scoping activity. From my pure \"technical viability and prototyping speed\" lens, it's somewhat peripheral. But it has a clear downstream impact on how I design systems (do I build in logging infrastructure or not?), so it matters to me.\n\nThe \"mandatory classification registry\" language is a bit concerning from an engineering agility standpoint — \"mandatory\" suggests a gate that could block prototyping. But if it's just a one-time audit that produces a reference document, it shouldn't impede ongoing work.\n\nI think this clears my bar at a moderate level. It's not a technical implementation proposal I'd champion, but it's a necessary precondition that affects how I architect systems. It's feasible (no technical barrier), and it supports prototyping quality by preventing both over- and under-engineering of compliance features. But it's also somewhat outside my direct domain — it's more of a legal/governance activity with engineering consequences.\n\nDecision: approve — it's a reasonable, low-cost scoping step that directly affects how I design logging architecture into systems. It prevents wasted prototyping effort on non-applicable