re cost.\n\n**My decision:**\nThe core concept — leveraging existing VCS and experiment tracking to auto-generate living technical documentation — is strongly aligned with my mandate of efficient resource use and maximizing existing infrastructure. It reduces the marginal cost of compliance documentation from linear (each new model requires manual doc work) to near-zero after initial pipeline setup. \n\nHowever, I have reservations about:\n1. \"Real time\" being over-specified (wastes resources)\n2. Scope unclear — does this cover ALL R&D models or just deployed high-risk systems?\n3. The word \"automatically... regulatory-ready\" oversimplifies the human-in-the-loop requirement\n\nBut these are implementation details that can be refined during execution. The core architecture is sound and efficient. I'll approve it but note the scope concerns in my rationale, and give a high-but-not-perfect promise score reflecting that it's a strong direction with some scoping work needed.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. From a strict resource-allocation standpoint: this proposal commits engineering resources to build an integration pipeline. The question is whether the ROI justifies it. Given that we're developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act (as stated in my role description), compliance documentation IS required, and doing it manually at scale would be enormously expensive. The initial investment pays back quickly once we have multiple model versions. I'm comfortable approving this.\n\nPromise score: I'd say 82. It's a strong, efficient use of existing infrastructure. Deductions for: (a) \"real time\" over-specification suggesting potential resource waste, (b) unclear scope boundary between all R&D artifacts vs. deployed high-risk systems only, (c) the \"automatically regulatory-ready\" framing risks underestimating human review effort needed.\n",
