ecessity\n- Addresses a long-term strategic issue but not an immediate compliance gap\n\nI'll lean toward reject or low-approve. From my strict compliance vantage point, I need systems that LOG and are ACCESSIBLE for the purposes stated in Article 12(2). Cryptographic obfuscation of logs could be seen as undermining the very purpose of traceability. The regulation says \"Having comprehensible information... is essential\" (Recital 71). If ZKPs make the log incomprehensible to the person who needs to use it for monitoring, does it comply?\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal is an R&D initiative - it's exploring whether these methods CAN work while still meeting requirements. It says they'll \"validate these methods against the specific requirements of Annex III.\" So it's not proposing to deploy non-compliant logging; it's researching whether compliant AND privacy-preserving logging is possible. That's more defensible.\n\nBut my job as Compliance Officer is: do we have the resources and do we need this? We need working logs NOW. This research won't produce that immediately. It's a 2-3 year R&D project at best before any deployment-ready solution exists. And even then, the specific requirements of Article 12(3) - especially naming natural persons (12(3)(d)) - are very hard to reconcile with ZKPs.\n\nI'll reject this. It doesn't directly serve my compliance mandate for current and near-term systems, it addresses a longer-term optimization question, and the specific statutory requirements create significant hurdles that make the research outcome uncertain. The \"publishable contribution\" angle also suggests it's not primarily driven by compliance necessity. My resources are better spent ensuring we have straightforward compliant logging in place.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 25-30. It addresses a real tension, but from my immediate compliance vantage point, it's speculative and doesn't ensure we meet current obligations. The specific requirements of Articl