hinner investment. This is good use of existing infrastructure.\n\n3. **Funding constraints**: By automating documentation, the marginal cost of adding another model version to compliance tracking approaches zero (it's automatic). Without this, each new experimental iteration requires additional manual documentation effort, which scales linearly with R&D output. For a lab operating under funding constraints, this non-linear scaling is critical — it means you can maintain compliance without proportionally increasing administrative overhead as you iterate more rapidly.\n\n4. **Balance between scientific ambition and funding**: A lab that wants to push ambitious research needs to iterate quickly on models. If each iteration requires manual documentation updates to stay compliant, researchers will either slow down (reducing scientific output) or skip documentation (creating compliance risk). This system removes that tension — you can iterate as fast as you want, and the records follow automatically.\n\nConcerns:\n1. **Scope creep / over-engineering**: The proposal mentions \"auto-generates compliance summaries for every model version.\" In an R&D lab with many experimental models (most of which won't become deployed high-risk systems), generating full compliance documentation for every single experiment might be wasteful. You'd want to scope this: maybe the automated capture is always-on (cheap, just logging), but the \"compliance summary\" generation should only trigger when a model is designated as heading toward deployment. However, the proposal says it bridges \"experimental code and formal records\" which suggests it's designed for the R&D phase — so perhaps it captures lightweight metadata during experimentation that can be expanded into full documentation later. This is actually reasonable.\n\n2. **\"Custom reporting layer\"**: Building custom software always carries risk of scope creep and maintenance burden. But if it's thin (just templating existing MLflow data into a format aligned with Recital 71 requirements), this is manageable. The key question is whether this requires dedicated engineering time or can be absorbed by existing infrastructure teams.\n\n3. **Real-time synchronization**: \"Keeping documentation synchronized with the actual system evolution in real-time\" — this is ambitious but if MLflow is already capturing experiment metadata, the reporting layer just needs to format it. Real-time isn't necessarily required for compliance (the regulation says records should be kept up to date), so even near-real-time or batch processing would suffice. This shouldn't be a blocker.\n\n4. **Does it actually cover what Article 12 requires?** The proposal focuses on training hyperparameters, dataset versions, validation results — which maps well to Recital 71's requirements about \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes.\" However, Article 12(1) specifically requires \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" over the system lifetime, and Article 12(2) focuses on runtime logging for traceability. This proposal seems more focused on the technical documentation aspect (Recital 71 / Article 11 territory) rather than the runtime event logging required by Article 12(1)-(2). But Recital 71 explicitly mentions \"keeping records and the availability of technical documentation\" as part of the record-keeping requirement, so it's within scope. It doesn't cover the full Article 12 runtime logging picture, but that might be a separate system. As a component addressing the training/documentation side, it's relevant.\n\n**Overall assessment**: From my resource-allocation and infrastructure-efficiency perspective, this is a strong proposal. It leverages existing tooling (MLflow), reduces per-iteration compliance costs dramatically, frees researcher time for scientific work, and scales well as R&D output increases. The investment is moderate (a reporting layer on top of existing MLflow) and the ROI in terms of saved re