nstraints.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:** This is a key point in my favor. The proposal explicitly says it will \"integrate our existing experiment tracking tools\" — meaning we already have the data collection layer. We don't need to build new logging infrastructure from scratch; we just need a templating/reporting layer on top. This is a low-incremental-cost approach that leverages what's already there. The \"templating engine\" component sounds relatively straightforward compared to building new ML systems or new data pipelines.\n\n**Concerns/risks:**\n- \"Real-time during the validation phase\" — is this actually necessary, or could periodic/batch reporting suffice? Real-time adds complexity and resource overhead (compute for continuous processing). But Recital 71 says documentation should be kept \"up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime,\" so some degree of automation in updating makes sense.\n- The scope: \"extracts metadata from training runs\" — this addresses part of what Recital 71 requires (algorithms, data, training processes) but may not cover everything (risk-management system documentation, capabilities/limitations descriptions). So it's a partial solution, which is fine for an initial step.\n- Maintenance cost: templating engines need updating as the regulation's implementation guidance evolves. But this is a manageable ongoing cost vs. the continuous burden of manual reporting.\n- The proposal seems reasonably scoped — not trying to solve everything at once, just automating the documentation generation from existing data.\n\n**From my vantage point specifically:**\n- This directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency (researcher hours redirected to research)\n- It leverages existing infrastructure (experiment tracking tools already in place)\n- The incremental cost is moderate (templating engine integration, not a new system build)\n- It frees capacity for scientific work without requiring additional funding head