terpretability research.\n- The framing \"transforms a legal requirement into an intellectual contribution\" is appealing but also somewhat of a rationalization for diverting resources from core compliance work into a tangential research direction.\n- From a funding constraint perspective: the lab needs to budget for compliance (building logging systems, maintaining them). Layering on a dedicated research project that requires \"advanced causal inference techniques\" adds cost without directly serving the regulatory obligation.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- If the lab is building Article 12-compliant logging anyway, the logs will exist. The question is whether the additional work of developing interpretability methods from them justifies dedicated resource allocation.\n- This could be a byproduct of compliance work - but the proposal frames it as a \"research project\" with its own identity, suggesting dedicated staffing and resources.\n- There's a risk this becomes a nice-to-have that perpetually gets deprioritized when compliance deadlines hit, or worse, one that competes for the same engineering/ML staff needed to build actual logging infrastructure.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal has intellectual merit but from my vantage point as Lab Director focused on resource efficiency and effective use of infrastructure:\n- It doesn't directly serve compliance - it's a downstream research activity that assumes compliance infrastructure exists.\n- It requires dedicated resources (researchers in causal inference, temporal analysis) that could be applied to the actual compliance work.\n- The \"log-based interpretability\" angle is interesting but the lab can note that logs are structured for compliance and may not be designed for causal inference (they record events, not necessarily the causal structure needed).\n- It's a research project, meaning open-ended timeline and uncertain outcomes - bad fit for scarce resources that need to deliver conc