efine what metadata gets captured, build the templates, integrate with existing pipelines. This is a one-time infrastructure investment.\n\n**Relevance to Article 12:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events (logs) - this proposal addresses the documentation side more than the logging side per se, but Recital 71 explicitly links technical documentation to traceability and compliance verification.\n- The \"technical documentation\" requirement in Recital 71 is about keeping records up-to-date throughout the system's lifetime - this proposal directly addresses that.\n- For Annex III 1(a) systems (biometrics), points (a)-(d) require specific data recording - the metadata-driven approach could support generating those records automatically.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Synchronous updating\" alongside code commits might be over-constraining for rapid prototyping where you're iterating quickly on model architectures before committing to a final design. You might want some flexibility in when docs get finalized vs. draft.\n- The scope is broad - it's an \"internal workflow\" revision, which could have organizational resistance and implementation overhead that affects speed.\n- But the core technical idea (auto-generate compliance metadata from pipeline outputs) is very implementable with current tooling.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a technically sound and well-motivated proposal. The documentation-as-code pattern is proven, the CI/CD integration is natural for an engineering organization, and it directly addresses a real pain point (end-of-project doc crunch). From a prototyping speed perspective, automating the compliance documentation generation removes a manual bottleneck. The initial setup cost is moderate but well-understood. I see no fundamental technical blockers.\n\nThe one reservation: \"synchronous updating alongside code commits\" might be slightly rigid for early prototyping phases where you're still exploring architectures. But even tha