already use (PyTorch, TensorFlow, HuggingFace, etc.)\n- The \"immutable ledger\" might be overkill - could a simple append-only database or even timestamped log files in S3 meet the Article 12 requirements? The regulation says \"technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs)\" - it doesn't mandate immutability per se. Though audit trails benefit from immutability, building a full immutable ledger system might be scope creep.\n- The SDK approach means we centralize compliance logic rather than scattering it across projects. This is good for consistency and reduces the chance of non-compliant implementations.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Secure, immutable ledger\" - this sounds like it could balloon in complexity. Article 12 requires logging capabilities, not a blockchain or cryptographic hash chain. A well-designed append-only log with access controls likely suffices. The proposal might be over-specifying the solution.\n- However, if we're developing AI systems that will be deployed by clients (as an R&D institution), having robust, tamper-evident logs is genuinely valuable for client trust and regulatory audits. So the \"immutable\" aspect has some justification.\n- The SDK needs to handle multiple model types, multiple deployment environments. That's real engineering complexity.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a strong proposal from a resource-efficiency standpoint. It's infrastructure that multiplies across all projects. The main risk is scope creep in the \"immutable ledger\" component and whether we can build it within our constraints without diverting too much from scientific work. But fundamentally, this is the right kind of investment - shared infrastructure that reduces per-project compliance overhead.\n\nI'd lean toward approving with a moderate-to-high promise score. It's clearly aligned with efficient resource use (build once, deploy many), protects research velocity, and uses a practical technical approach (Python wrapper). The main caveat is that \"secure, immutable ledger\" needs to be scoped carefully to not become an over-engineered solution when Article 12's actual requirements might be more modest.\n\nI'll approve this with a promise score around 78-82. It's clearly the right direction and good use of resources, but I want to flag that the \"immutable ledger\" component needs careful scoping to avoid becoming an over-engineered sink for engineering hours.\n",
