burning funding without producing science. Decoupling protects that.\n\nConcerns/risks:\n1. **Upfront investment**: Building a shared library and centralized immutable database is non-trivial engineering work. It requires dedicated resources before any model benefits from it. In a constrained budget environment, this is a significant upfront cost with deferred payoff.\n2. **\"Total traceability\" claim**: The proposal says \"total traceability\" but Article 12(3) has specific minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics). A generic middleware might need domain-specific extensions to capture things like \"reference database against which input data has been checked\" or \"identification of natural persons involved in verification.\" So it may not be as plug-and-play as suggested.\n3. **Intercepting API calls**: This assumes a clean API boundary exists across all lab systems. In practice, R&D code can be messy — direct tensor operations, batch scripts, notebook explorations. The middleware might miss edge cases or require significant refactoring of existing pipelines to route through the interception layer. That's hidden resource cost.\n4. **Single point of failure / bottleneck**: A centralized database for all logging could become a performance bottleneck during heavy training runs or evaluation periods. This could slow down actual research if not properly architected.\n5. **\"Immutable\" requirement**: True immutability (e.g., append-only, cryptographic hashing) adds complexity and storage cost. Over the \"lifetime of the system,\" data volume can be substantial.\n\n**Net assessment from resource-allocation perspective:**\nThis is a well-conceived proposal that addresses the core tension I care about: how to meet regulatory obligations without grinding R&D productivity to a halt. The decoupling principle is sound, and building on existing internal framework is efficient use of infrastructure. However, the \"total traceability\" framing oversells