        "reasoning_content": "The proposal P2090 is about \"Regulatory Overhead Resource Budgeting\" — setting aside a specific percentage of compute and labor budget for traceability/compliance requirements in high-risk AI projects, as a mandatory line item in the funding model.\n\nLet me evaluate this from my vantage point: Development Engineer at an R&D institution, domain of concern is technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nKey considerations:\n1. Is this a technical proposal? It's primarily a financial/resource management proposal. It doesn't specify any technical architecture for logging, no implementation details for how logs are generated, stored, retrieved, or maintained over the system lifetime. It's an administrative/budgetary measure.\n\n2. Does it advance technical viability? Not directly. It acknowledges that compliance work will consume resources, which is true, but setting aside a \"specific percentage\" doesn't solve any technical problem. The actual logging infrastructure (what events to capture, how to timestamp them, how to store them over the lifetime of the system, how to handle the reference database tracking for Annex III point 1(a) systems, etc.) requires specific engineering decisions that this proposal doesn't touch.\n\n3. Speed/quality of prototyping: A fixed percentage budget is blunt. Some high-risk AI systems (e.g., a simple classification model with limited deployment) might need very little logging infrastructure, while others (e.g., a biometric verification system under Annex III 1(a)) need substantial engineering for tracking reference databases, input data matches, verifier identities, etc. A flat percentage doesn't map well to actual technical requirements. It could also slow prototyping if the budget is ring-fenced and can't be flexed when a project's logging needs are smaller than expected (or larger).\n\n4. Is there any merit? There IS some validity in the observation that compliance work gets squeezed out of project budgets when it's not explicitly accounted for. In practice, at R&D institutions, if you don't budget for something, it doesn't get done. So having a line item is better than having no allocation. However, this is a resource management/policy decision, not a technical solution. It doesn't tell me HOW to build the logging system, what architecture to use, how to ensure logs are technically feasible over the \"lifetime of the system\" (which could be years), or how to make it work with our existing ML infrastructure.\n\n5. From my engineering vantage point: This proposal is essentially a budget memo dressed as an R&D initiative. It commits resources in an abstract way without specifying any technical approach. The \"specific percentage\" is undefined — what percentage? How is it determined per project? This lacks the specificity needed for me to assess whether it would actually deliver working logging infrastructure.\n\n6. Speed/quality of prototyping impact: Ring-fencing a fixed percentage could actually HURT prototyping speed if the allocation is too large for simple systems, or create bottlenecks if the \"compliance budget\" team is separate from the core engineering team and has different priorities/timelines. It also creates a false binary — as if all compliance work fits neatly into one bucket, when in practice logging architecture decisions are deeply intertwined with the system design itself (you can't bolt on logging after the fact; you need to instrument during development).\n\nMy assessment: This is an administrative/financial measure that has some indirect relevance but doesn't constitute a technically viable solution to Article 12. It doesn't specify what to build, how to build it, or what architecture to use. From my vantage point focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality, this proposal doesn't move the needle on actual engineering outcomes. It's a necessary but extremely insufficient condition. I'd lean toward rejecting because it commits implementation resources (the lab's scarce budget) to an administrative process rather than a technical solution, and the fixed-percentage approach could actually impede prototyping flexibility.\n\nHowever, I should note there IS some value — acknowledging compliance costs upfront is better than discovering them mid-project. But as a \"proposal\" that would consume implementation resources, it's more of a policy recommendation than an actionable engineering plan. It doesn't tell me what to build or how.\n\nI'll reject it with a moderate-low promise score. Maybe 20-25? It acknowledges a real problem (compliance work getting starved) but the solution is too blunt and non-technical to be useful from my vantage point, and it could actually slow prototyping by creating rigid budget boundaries.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise around 22.\n",
