fic Annex III 1(a) requirements.\n- \"Develop a shared internal library\" — this requires upfront engineering investment. If the lab only has one or two high-risk systems, is this overkill? But as an R&D institution that *develops* AI systems subject to the Act, it's reasonable to expect multiple projects will need this.\n- The phrase \"absolute regulatory compliance throughout the prototype lifetime\" is aspirational but potentially misleading — middleware can't guarantee compliance by itself; the schema needs to be validated against specific requirements per system type.\n- Risk: over-standardization could create a bottleneck if the library becomes a single point of failure or requires all projects to conform to one rigid interface before they can iterate quickly on research questions.\n- The \"standardized schema for all event logs\" might not fit all use cases — different high-risk categories may need different granularities of logging.\n\n**Resource allocation assessment:**\nThis is a well-targeted infrastructure investment. For an R&D institution developing multiple AI systems, the upfront cost of building one middleware layer is almost certainly less than the cumulative cost of each project team independently implementing compliant logging. It's the kind of \"use existing infrastructure effectively\" play that makes sense — you build the compliance plumbing once and route all projects through it.\n\nThe main risk is scope creep or over-engineering (trying to make it truly \"universal\" when different system types need different things), but the core concept is sound from an efficiency standpoint.\n\nI'd rate this as a strong proposal. It's exactly the kind of cross-cutting infrastructure investment that preserves research time while meeting regulatory requirements efficiently. The main caveat is ensuring the schema is flexible enough for different risk categories and that it doesn't become a bottleneck. But fundamentally, this is good resource allocation.\n\n