ping:**\n- This is explicitly a research project (\"The team will investigate...\"). It's not proposing a prototype or a concrete engineering solution. From my vantage point focused on prototyping speed and quality, this is exploratory R&D that may take years before yielding anything deployable.\n- MPC systems are notoriously difficult to implement at scale with acceptable latency. Getting a working prototype of an MPC-based logging pipeline would be a significant engineering effort.\n- Differential privacy for structured logs: the noise calibration for useful log entries (timestamps, database references, match results) while preserving enough signal to actually perform traceability is non-trivial and may not yield practical results for all Article 12(3) fields.\n- \"We will publish our findings\" - this is academic output, not a deliverable system component.\n\n**Does it address the regulation?**\n- It partially touches on how logs are stored/shared (privacy of verification personnel in 12(3)(d)), but the core requirement is that the system MUST technically allow automatic recording. The proposal doesn't propose a logging architecture - it proposes obfuscating/transforming the logged data with cryptographic techniques. That's an orthogonal concern to whether you have a working, performant log pipeline.\n- For most high-risk AI systems, standard access controls + encryption at rest/transit + retention policies handle the GDPR side without needing DP or MPC on the logs themselves.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a development engineering / prototyping perspective, this is a research project with unclear path to a deployable artifact. The technical approaches (DP, MPC) are either overkill for the actual regulatory need (standard access controls suffice for most cases) or technically very challenging to implement at production quality and speed. It doesn't propose a concrete logging architecture that I could prototype. It's intellectually interesting but from my seat - focused on building working systems quickly and well - this doesn't give me a clear path to a shippable component. The \"conflict\" it addresses is largely solvable with standard security engineering (RBAC, encryption, retention policies) rather than exotic cryptographic protocols.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: for certain Annex III(1)(a) systems where verification involves sensitive biometric or identity data, there might be a genuine case where logs need more than standard access controls. But even then, the proposal is too vague and research-oriented to commit implementation resources to.\n\nI'll reject this. My promise score should reflect that while I see some intellectual merit, from my engineering/prototyping vantage point it's very low priority. Maybe 15-20. It's not zero because the problem space (privacy in logs) is real, but the proposed approach and framing don't give me a clear path to a working prototype, and for most cases standard security engineering suffices.\n\nI'll go with 18.\n",
