        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1386\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core architecture—version-controlled docs generated from the codebase and CI pipeline, with data-lineage metadata capturing training sources and validation outcomes—is a well-trodden engineering pattern (Git + CI hooks + doc-gen) that we can prototype within sprint cycles; it would meaningfully reduce rework from stale documentation during iterative prototyping. However, the proposal overstates the degree of 'automatic' synchronization achievable (semantic mapping between arbitrary commits and compliance-relevant changes still requires disciplined tagging or NLP heuristics), and it addresses Recital 71's documentation requirement while largely sidestepping Article 12(1)'s runtime logging obligation for deployed systems, which is a distinct engineering artifact.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P1386 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n\"Living Technical Documentation System. A dynamic repository that tracks algorithm changes, training data sources, and validation results in real-time throughout the development cycle. This replaces static PDF reports. We will implement a version-controlled documentation tool linked directly to our codebase to automatically update technical files during R&D iterations. It will synchronize commits with compliance logs. This satisfies Recital 71's requirement for up-to-date documentation while preserving institutional knowledge and professional reputation.\"\n\nFrom my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on:\n- Technical viability of solutions\n- Speed/quality of prototyping\n\nLet me assess:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A version-controlled documentation tool linked to the codebase is technically feasible. Tools like MkDocs, Sphinx with Git integration, or custom CI/CD pipelines that generate docs from code annotations are well-established patterns.\n- \"Synchronizes commits with compliance logs\" - this is where it gets interesting and potentially problematic. Automatically updating compliance documentation on every commit is doable in principle (CI hooks, webhooks), but the mapping between a git commit and meaningful compliance-relevant changes (e.g., \"this commit changed the training data source\") requires semantic understanding or very disciplined tagging conventions. Not trivially solvable with off-the-shelf tools.\n- \"Tracks algorithm changes, training data sources, and validation results in real-time\" - tracking algorithm changes via git is straightforward. Tracking training data sources requires structured metadata (data lineage tools like WhyAI/Why Logs, or custom schemas). Validation results can be piped from CI test runs. \"Real-time\" is a stretch but near-real-time via CI pipelines is feasible.\n- The proposal conflates two things: technical documentation (Recital 71) and logging (Article 12). Article 12(1) requires the system to technically allow automatic recording of events over its lifetime - this is about runtime logs, not development documentation. The proposal seems to address Recital 71's documentation requirement but doesn't clearly address Article 12's logging requirements for deployed systems. However, it does mention \"synchronize commits with compliance logs\" which hints at bridging the gap.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a significant infrastructure investment. Building or configuring a living documentation system that auto-generates from code and CI pipelines is a real engineering project - potentially weeks to months depending on scope.\n- However, it directly serves prototyping quality: if documentation stays in sync with code, engineers don't waste time reading stale docs, onboarding is faster, and regression risk from undocumented changes decreases.\n- \"Replaces static PDF reports\" - this is actually a good engineering instinct. Static documents that go stale are worse than no documentation for R&D velocity.\n- The concern: if the tool requires developers to manually tag commits or maintain metadata schemas to generate compliance docs, it adds friction to the prototyping loop. If it's truly automatic (parsing code diffs, running tests, capturing data lineage), it could be net-positive for speed. But \"truly automatic\" is an overstatement of what current tools can do reliably.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\n- The core idea is sound and technically feasible with existing tooling patterns (Git + CI/CD + documentation generation).\n- It addresses Recital 71 well but only tangentially addresses Article 12's runtime logging requirement.\n- For prototyping speed: the initial build cost is real, but long-term it reduces rework and confusion. The risk is that \"real-time\" and \"automatically update\" set expectations that are harder to meet in practice than they sound.\n- The proposal is vague on implementation specifics (what tool? what schema? who maintains the mapping between commits and compliance events?). As a development engineer, I'd want more specificity before committing resources, but the direction is right.\n- It's not a wild idea - it's a reasonable engineering solution to a real regulatory requirement. The feasibility is moderate-to-good with current tooling, though \"real-time\" is aspirational.\n\nI think this clears my bar as technically viable (it's not moonshot territory) and would positively impact prototyping quality over time. It's not perfect - the Article 12 runtime logging aspect is underaddressed, and the \"automatic\" claims are somewhat optimistic - but the core architecture (version-controlled docs linked to codebase, CI-synced compliance artifacts) is a standard engineering pattern that we could prototype quickly and iterate on.\n\nDecision: approve\nPromise: I'd say around 72-78. It's solid directionally, technically feasible with existing patterns, and directly improves prototyping workflow quality. But it's not a slam-dunk because the \"real-time automatic\" framing is optimistic, the Article 12 runtime logging gap is notable, and the implementation cost to get it right (vs. a good-enough v1) could be significant. I'll go with 74.\n",
