ientifically.\n- It's a budgeting framework — a financial accounting structure.\n- The \"intellectual merit\" of the proposal as a research contribution is essentially zero.\n- Its broader scientific impact is negligible — it's an internal administrative practice.\n\nOn the other hand: if the lab's research output suffers because compliance costs silently drain project budgets, then this could indirectly protect the capacity to do frontier research. But that's a very weak connection, and the proposal itself doesn't advance knowledge in any way. It's a reasonable operational/administrative idea, but from MY specific vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), it has minimal relevance.\n\nI think I should reject this from my vantage point. It's not that the idea is bad — it might be very sensible from an operations or finance perspective — but from the lens of \"does this advance the knowledge frontier, enhance intellectual merit of research, or have broader scientific impact?\" the answer is essentially no. It's a budgeting mechanism.\n\nPromise score: I'd put this quite low from my vantage point. Maybe 10-15. It's not harmful, but it doesn't do anything for the knowledge frontier. It's a reasonable administrative practice that belongs in a different function's domain (finance/operations). I'll give it around 12.\n\nDecision: reject.\n",
