 requirements of Article 12\"). From a pure research standpoint, I'd want the data model to be driven by what makes experiments scientifically reproducible and interpretable first, with EU compliance being one mapping layer. If the schema is designed around regulatory checkboxes rather than scientific needs, it might capture the wrong things or miss important contextual metadata (e.g., why certain hyperparameters were chosen, ablation context, related hypotheses).\n- \"Removes the manual burden of record-keeping from the researcher\" — this is operationally nice but I need to be careful: if the automation is too opaque, researchers might stop thinking carefully about what they're doing. The act of documenting can itself force rigor and reflection. However, capturing telemetry automatically doesn't eliminate the need for human-written interpretive notes.\n- \"Without interrupting the creative research process\" — this is a good aspiration. Research processes are iterative and messy; forcing structured logging at every step could add friction if not done well. But if it's truly non-intrusive hooks, that's fine.\n- The scope seems to cover training specifically (\"core model training scripts\"). What about inference-time behavior, data preprocessing pipelines, or the broader research workflow (literature review, hypothesis formulation)? Article 12 covers the full lifetime of the system. But from a pure \"knowledge frontier\" angle, capturing the training process is where most of the scientific content lives for ML research.\n- \"Immutable logs\" — scientifically, this is good (prevents post-hoc modification of results). But in practice, researchers iterate: they might run 50 variants of a hyperparameter search. Does \"every experiment run\" include all failed runs? That would be great for science but could create massive data management challenges.\n\nOverall assessment from researcher's vantage:\nThis is a solid proposal that has genuine scientific merit beyond mere compliance. Provenance and reproducibility infrastructure is something the research community broadly needs, and doing it well (standardized, automated, immutable) elevates the lab's outputs. The main concern is whether the design will be science-first or compliance-first in its data model. But as a proposal for implementation resources to build, it clearly advances the knowledge frontier by making the lab's work more reproducible and transparent, which has broader scientific impact (other labs could adopt similar patterns). It's not cutting-edge novel research itself, but it's essential infrastructure that enables better science.\n\nI'd lean toward approving this from the researcher's vantage because reproducibility and provenance are core to intellectual merit of research. The promise score: it's valuable but not a frontier breakthrough — it's foundational infrastructure. I'd say around 72-78. It clearly serves the researcher's domain (reproducibility, scientific integrity) without being a moonshot. Let me settle on 75.\n",
