tent, or lack the structured metadata needed for regulatory documentation. This could require significant investment in developer culture change (requiring structured commits) before the automation actually produces useful output.\n- Real-time auto-population of \"EU technical dossiers\" is ambitious. The dossiers need to contain specific information per Article 12 and Recital 71: general characteristics, capabilities, limitations, algorithms, data, training/testing/validation processes, risk-management system documentation. Much of this isn't trivially extractable from commit messages.\n- However, the core idea — linking VCS metadata to compliance docs — is a reasonable engineering project with clear ROI if done right. It's not a moonshot; it's a systems integration project.\n- The cost is moderate: it requires engineering time (probably 2-4 senior engineers for initial build) and some process changes, but it leverages existing tools rather than requiring new infrastructure purchases.\n\n**Assessment from my vantage point:** This is a strong proposal on resource-allocation efficiency grounds. It converts an ongoing, recurring administrative cost into a one-time engineering investment that then pays back continuously. It uses existing R&D infrastructure (VCS, data versioning, CI/CD logs) rather than requiring new lab space or equipment. It directly addresses the tension between rapid scientific iteration and regulatory documentation requirements — which is exactly the balance my domain cares about.\n\nThe main risk is that \"scraping commit messages\" underestimates the effort needed to make structured metadata available, but this is a solvable engineering problem, not a fundamental flaw in the concept. The proposal is scoped reasonably — it's an integration/automation project, not a research program.\n\nI'd approve this. It's a clear efficiency gain with bounded cost that leverages existing infrastructure. Promise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's very well-aligned