dization**: The \"modular software library\" approach means it can be adopted incrementally by different research groups with varying architectures, reducing friction and adoption barriers.\n\nConcerns from my perspective:\n1. **Centralized ledger risk**: An immutable, centralized ledger could become a single point of failure or bottleneck. If the lab's infrastructure team is small (as many R&D labs are), maintaining this shared service becomes an ongoing resource commitment. Who maintains it? This is an ongoing operational cost that needs to be factored in.\n2. **Scope creep risk**: Building a \"unified framework\" across \"various AI architectures\" could balloon in scope. Different high-risk systems under Annex III have different logging requirements (Article 12(3) specifies minimums for biometric systems). A truly unified solution might need significant customization per use case, eroding the efficiency gains.\n3. **Upfront investment vs. ongoing benefit**: The initial development cost by the infrastructure team is non-trivial. If the lab only has a few high-risk systems currently, the ROI might not justify the upfront build compared to a simpler approach (e.g., a set of guidelines and a basic template). However, if the pipeline of high-risk projects is growing, the framework pays for itself faster.\n4. **Scientific ambition balance**: By making it part of the \"standard project template,\" there's a risk that compliance logging becomes an afterthought bolted onto research rather than something designed into the system. But conversely, having it in the template means researchers don't have to think about it at all, which actually frees up their time for scientific work. This is a net positive for scientific ambition.\n5. **Effectiveness**: Does it actually meet Article 12 requirements? It mentions timestamps (Article 12(3)(a) - period of use), input references (could cover 12(3)(b) and (c)), but doesn't explicitly mention recording of the reference database, identification of natural persons in verification (12(3)(d)). The \"modular\" aspect might address this through configuration, but it's worth noting.\n\nOverall assessment from my resource-allocation perspective:\nThis is a strong infrastructure investment that directly addresses the waste I'm concerned about. The key value is amortization: one build serves N projects instead of N builds for N projects. The integration into the standard template reduces adoption friction to near-zero (it's already there when you start a new project). This is exactly how effective use of R&D infrastructure should work — shared services that remove compliance overhead from individual research groups so they can focus on science.\n\nThe main risk is scope and maintenance burden, but these are manageable if the framework is kept modular (as stated) and scoped appropriately. The upfront cost is justified by the ongoing savings in duplicated engineering effort across multiple projects.\n\nI would approve this. It's a high-leverage infrastructure investment that aligns perfectly with my mandate of resource-allocation efficiency and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nPromise score: I'd give this around 82-85. It's very well-aligned with my concerns, the resource-efficiency argument is strong, but there are some implementation risks (scope management, maintenance burden on a possibly small infra team, ensuring it actually meets all Article 12 sub-requirements across different Annex III categories) that keep it from being a perfect 90+.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
