or compliance.\n- Capturing \"technical lineage\" and \"internal state transitions\" goes beyond minimum requirements, providing a stronger audit trail.\n- The API layer approach suggests it could be modular and adaptable to different system types (high-risk categories).\n- Having hyperparameters recorded alongside logs supports the technical documentation requirement in Recital 71.\n\n**Concerns/risks from compliance perspective:**\n- The proposal frames this primarily as converting compliance into a \"rich dataset for analyzing emergent model behaviors.\" This framing could be problematic - if the primary motivation is scientific analysis rather than regulatory compliance, there's a risk that the logging infrastructure gets deprioritized or designed in ways that serve research needs rather than audit requirements. However, the proposal does say it integrates with \"mandatory EU logs,\" so the compliance baseline appears to be met.\n- The scope is broad: \"deep telemetry for scientific analysis\" + \"technical lineage of every model decision\" + \"internal state transitions and hyperparameters.\" This is a significant engineering commitment. As Compliance Officer, I care that this gets done correctly and on time, not necessarily that it's maximally rich. But over-engineering isn't a compliance risk per se - under-engineering is.\n- The proposal says it will be \"integrated into our primary training pipelines.\" This is good for Article 12(1) (lifetime logging), but I need to consider: does this cover deployment/inference logs as well? Article 12(3)(a) specifically requires recording start/end times of each use. If the toolkit only captures training pipeline events, it might not fully satisfy inference-time logging requirements. However, \"every model decision\" and \"internal state transitions\" could encompass inference.\n- From a compliance perspective, having a unified system that handles both mandatory logs AND additional telemetry is actually good - it creates