on control, experiment tracking). The main risk from my vantage point is the \"real-time\" mandate potentially creating friction in the research workflow if not carefully scoped to distinguish between exploratory R&D work and systems actually being developed for high-risk deployment. However, as a lab director focused on resource efficiency, I'd note that:\n- It doesn't require new major infrastructure investments\n- It formalizes practices likely already partially in place\n- The regulatory requirement is non-negotiable for high-risk systems\n- The main cost is process discipline and some workflow adjustment\n\nThis clears my bar. It's efficient use of existing tools, addresses a hard regulatory requirement, and the \"real-time\" concern can be managed through scoping (only fully deployed/production candidates need the most granular logging; exploratory work can be logged at a coarser granularity). I'd approve but note that implementation should build on existing experiment-tracking infrastructure rather than creating new systems.\n\nPromise score: This is a strong, necessary proposal from an efficiency standpoint because it leverages what we have. It's not a stretch or speculative — it's compliance work done efficiently. The \"real-time\" language is slightly concerning for R&D workflow but manageable. I'd put this around 78-82. It's clearly needed and implementable within existing constraints, but the implementation details (especially \"real-time\") will need careful scoping to avoid creating unnecessary overhead on the research team.\n\nI'll go with 80.\n",
