e wheel each time\n- CON: Building this framework itself is a significant upfront investment before any project benefits\n- CON: A \"unified\" framework risks becoming a bottleneck if it's not flexible enough for diverse model architectures (LLMs, CV models, tabular classifiers all have very different execution paths)\n- CON: \"Compliance by default\" in the pipeline could add latency or complexity to prototyping cycles — researchers may work around it if it's annoying\n- PRO: Consistency means less debugging of logging issues later, which does improve quality\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level. It describes a library but doesn't address how it handles diverse model types (the Annex III systems are very different from each other)\n- \"Modular plugins\" is a good pattern but the actual engineering challenge is defining the plugin interface well enough to be useful without being constraining\n- The proposal conflates two things: (1) building the logging infrastructure, and (2) integrating it into the dev pipeline. The integration part could actually HURT prototyping speed if done poorly\n- However, the core idea — a shared library with a consistent API for the specific Article 12 data points — is technically sound and would reduce repetitive work\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a dev engineer's perspective, this is a solid, technically viable proposal. The scope is well-defined by the regulation (timestamps, DB references, input matches, verification personnel). A shared library with plugins is a standard pattern. The main risk is that it becomes either too rigid (hurting prototyping) or too generic (not capturing what's actually needed per system type). But as a starting point for a logging framework, it's technically sound and would save significant engineering time across multiple projects.\n\nI'd approve this — it's technically feasible, the scope is clear, the architecture pattern is sound, and it addresses a real repetitive-engineering problem. 