e 14(5) (human oversight) AND Article 12(3)(d). Failure here could mean enforcement action.\n- This is relatively low-cost (\"lightweight web interface\") but high-value for compliance defense.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:**\n- Having a robust audit trail demonstrates to customers, regulators, and the market that we take traceability seriously.\n- In a sector where trust in AI systems (especially biometric) is fragile, being able to show full human accountability chains is a competitive differentiator.\n\n**Potential gaps I should note but shouldn't reject for:**\n- The proposal doesn't mention data retention periods — the Act requires keeping records throughout the system's lifetime (Recital 71). But this could be handled in implementation details.\n- It doesn't mention GDPR implications of storing identity data linked to specific verification actions — but that's an adjacent compliance issue, not a reason to reject this proposal.\n- \"Immutable\" needs to be technically defined (append-only database? WORM storage? cryptographic hash chain?) — again, implementation detail.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a well-targeted, legally grounded proposal that addresses a specific and enforceable requirement of the AI Act. It's proportionate in scope, uses existing infrastructure (SSO), creates defensible evidence, and directly supports our ability to demonstrate compliance if challenged by regulators or in litigation. As Compliance Officer, I would strongly support this.\n\nMy promise score: This is a clear, well-scoped compliance requirement with direct legal basis. It's not speculative — it's implementing an explicit obligation. From my vantage point (regulatory compliance and institutional survival), this is high priority. I'd rate it around 82-85. It's not a 100 because:\n- It only covers one sub-element of Article 12(3)\n- \"Immutable\" needs technical specification to be truly defensible\n- There are adjacent considerations (GDPR, retention periods) that n