rious risk. Automating this reduces that risk significantly.\n\n**Market reputation:** Being able to demonstrate to regulators and customers that our documentation is systematically generated from actual experimental data (with dataset hashes proving provenance) is a strong market signal of compliance maturity.\n\n**My decision framework:**\n- Does it advance regulatory compliance? Yes, meaningfully — it addresses the technical documentation requirement which is inextricably linked to record-keeping obligations.\n- Does it protect institutional survival? Yes — by reducing the risk that we fail to produce required documentation on time or with sufficient accuracy.\n- Does it support market reputation? Moderately — demonstrates systematic compliance posture.\n\n**Counterarguments:**\n- It doesn't directly implement Article 12(1)'s logging requirement (the system must \"technically allow\" auto-recording). But that's a different engineering task. This proposal is complementary, not contradictory.\n- Risk that automation creates a false sense of completeness — the pipeline only captures what's in experiment tracking tools. If something isn't tracked there, it won't be in the documentation. But this is an implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw in the proposal.\n\nOverall: This is a strong compliance enabler. It doesn't directly implement Article 12(1)'s logging mandate, but it addresses the closely related technical documentation requirement that Recital 71 explicitly ties to traceability and post-market monitoring. From my vantage point, this reduces institutional risk significantly by making compliance documentation scalable with R&D iteration speed.\n\nI'll approve this. Promise score: It's very useful for compliance but I'd note it addresses documentation generation rather than the core logging requirement of Article 12 itself. Still, it's a clear win. I'd put it around 82-85. The fact that it's specifically tied to Recital 71 (which supports Article 12) and p