otyped quickly - maybe 2-4 weeks for a functional prototype using existing tooling (ELK stack, Prometheus-style alerting).\n- However, the \"AI-driven auditing\" component that detects \"substantial modification\" is where prototyping gets slow. You need to define what constitutes substantial modification operationally, which depends on the specific high-risk system being built. This is domain-dependent and hard to generalize.\n- The proposal as stated is somewhat vague on technical architecture - \"scans system logs for indicators\" could mean regex matching (easy) or ML-based anomaly detection (harder, needs training data).\n- The value proposition is real: Article 12(2)(b) specifically calls for logging that facilitates post-market monitoring. Having a tool that automates the analysis of those logs has clear utility.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Predefined safety and performance thresholds\" - who defines these? For novel AI systems, you don't know what anomalies look like until you've seen them operate. Predefined thresholds might give false confidence or miss genuinely new risk patterns.\n- The proposal frames this as protecting \"market reputation\" and avoiding \"public regulatory breaches or liability issues\" - this is a business/compliance framing rather than an engineering one. From my perspective, I need to know: what's the technical architecture? What log format? What's the detection model? How do we handle false positives without alert fatigue?\n- The proposal says \"the lab will develop a separate monitoring agent\" - this implies building new infrastructure. Given scarce resources, is there enough differentiation from existing open-source or commercial log-monitoring solutions to justify in-house development?\n- For prototyping speed: the MVP (collect logs + threshold alerts) is fast. The \"AI-driven\" intelligent detection layer is where it gets slow and uncertain.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe basic concept is sound and maps reason