stment that frees up expensive scientific labor for actual R&D work.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: The proposal explicitly leverages tools we likely already have in our stack — Git (version control), MLflow (experiment tracking), code comments (inherent to development). It doesn't propose buying new systems or hiring new staff. It proposes a thin integration layer that connects existing data sources into compliance-ready documentation. This is very well aligned with \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\"\n\nPotential concerns:\n- \"Custom documentation generator\" implies some development work — but this is modest compared to building entire logging infrastructure from scratch.\n- The proposal focuses on technical documentation (algorithms, training processes) rather than the full Article 12 scope (which also includes automatic event logging). However, it addresses a meaningful and resource-intensive portion of compliance.\n- It's not a complete solution — we'd still need the logging capabilities for events during operation (Article 12(1)-(3)). But as one component of our broader compliance strategy, it's well-scoped.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a high-value proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses resource efficiency by automating a repetitive administrative task using existing infrastructure. The initial investment is modest relative to the ongoing cost savings (scientist hours freed up). It doesn't require new funding beyond development time for the custom generator component. It aligns perfectly with making our R&D operation more efficient without compromising scientific output.\n\nI would approve this. Promise score: This is very strong from my perspective. I'd put it around 82-85. It's not a 100 because \"custom documentation generator\" still represents some development risk and the scope (technical documentation specifically) doesn't cover all of Article 12's logging requirements. But from resource-efficiency and infrastructure-leverage angles, it's excellent work.\n",
