ions.\n- The proposal says it \"eliminates the manual burden\" - in practice, it would significantly reduce but likely not eliminate it entirely, because some regulatory documentation requires narrative explanation, risk assessments, and contextual information that isn't captured in structured experiment logs.\n\n**Quality concerns:**\n- Auto-generated documentation from MLflow/VCS data is good for the factual/quantitative parts (hyperparameters, metrics, dataset versions) but less so for qualitative descriptions of algorithms, limitations, or risk management processes.\n- The \"standardized regulatory templates\" need to be carefully designed to match what Recital 71 actually requires. There's a risk of building automation around an incomplete understanding of the documentation requirements.\n- Version control integration for model code is doable but can get messy with monorepos, multi-model pipelines, etc.\n\n**From my specific vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):**\nThis is a highly feasible engineering project with clear, well-understood components. The core automation (pulling structured data from MLflow/VCS → rendering into templates) is technically low-risk and fast to prototype. The main risk is scope creep: trying to automate the entire documentation when only parts are truly automatable from structured sources. But as a prototyping target, it's excellent - you can get a working MVP quickly that handles the 60-70% of documentation content that IS derivable from experiment trackers and VCS.\n\nI would approve this. It's technically sound, fast to prototype, addresses a real pain point (keeping documentation in sync with model iterations), and the core engineering challenges are well-understood. The promise score should be high - maybe 78-82. Not a perfect 100 because:\n- The \"eliminates manual burden\" framing is slightly overpromising; some sections will still need human narrative input\n- The proposal doesn't address the Art