ng compliant while another isn't, reducing audit risk and rework costs.\n5. The scope is well-bounded: it's a wrapper around inference engines capturing specific data points (timestamps, input-output pairs, reference database versions) rather than an open-ended platform build.\n\nCons/Concerns:\n1. \"Unified\" across \"diverse AI architectures\" could be ambitious. Different model types (LLMs, computer vision, time-series, etc.) may have very different logging needs. A truly unified solution might require significant engineering to handle edge cases. However, the proposal seems scoped to inference engine wrappers, which is reasonable.\n2. Development cost: Building and maintaining this wrapper requires dedicated engineering resources. But compared to the alternative (every project team building their own), it's clearly more efficient at scale.\n3. Risk of becoming a bottleneck: If all new prototypes depend on this module being in the base image, any bugs or updates could block research. However, this is manageable with good versioning and testing.\n4. The proposal mentions \"all new prototypes\" but what about existing ones? Migration cost isn't addressed. But from a resource-allocation standpoint, applying to new projects first is a sensible phasing approach.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a well-scoped infrastructure investment that directly addresses Article 12 requirements while improving resource efficiency across the lab. It leverages existing infrastructure (internal ML libraries, inference engines, base images) rather than requiring net-new platforms. The one-time development cost is amortized across all future projects. It reduces researcher friction and ensures compliance is \"baked in\" rather than bolted on per-project. From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is clearly superior to the alternative of ad-hoc logging per project.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score: This is strong from my vantage point. It's efficient, leverages existing infrastructure, and reduces ongoing compliance overhead. I'd give it around 82-85. It's not a 95+ because \"unified across diverse AI architectures\" carries some execution risk in terms of scope creep, and the development itself consumes resources upfront. But the ROI is clear and well-aligned with my domain concerns.\n\nLet me settle on 83 - strong approval, good resource efficiency, but some execution uncertainty around truly unifying across all architecture types.\n",
