ectly**: The regulation requires technical documentation to be \"kept up to date\" throughout the lifetime (Recital 71). A dynamic pipeline inherently solves the staleness problem that plagues static documentation approaches. This is not a nice-to-have; it's a structural compliance requirement.\n4. **Scalability across projects**: Once built, this infrastructure can serve all high-risk AI systems in the lab rather than requiring per-project documentation efforts. Good amortization of the build cost.\n\n**Concerns from my perspective:**\n1. **Upfront build cost**: Even though it leverages existing tools (Git, MLflow), building a \"documentation generator tailored to the EU AI Act requirements\" is non-trivial engineering work. It requires understanding both the regulatory text and the lab's specific system architectures. There's a real risk of scope creep here—what does \"tailored to the EU AI Act requirements\" mean in practice? Article 12, Annex IV technical documentation, risk management documentation, etc., all need to be covered.\n2. **Complexity risk**: The proposal describes something ambitious (\"real-time audit trail from data ingestion through to deployment\"). In a lab with funding constraints, \"real-time\" and \"through to deployment\" could balloon into an enormous project. A phased approach might be more prudent, but the proposal as stated doesn't mention phasing.\n3. **Ongoing maintenance**: A live documentation pipeline requires ongoing maintenance, updates when regulations change, adaptation to new model architectures. This is a recurring cost that must be budgeted.\n4. **Does it address Article 12's logging requirement (paragraphs 1-3)?** The proposal focuses more on the \"technical documentation\" aspect of Recital 71 rather than the automatic event logging (logs) required by Article 12(1)-(3). It mentions \"audit trail\" but doesn't specifically address the structured event logging that Article 12 mandates. This is a partial solution—it addresses