        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1043\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Latent-space snapshots and full attention-map archiving per inference generate data volumes (potentially gigabytes to terabytes per model run) that are economically and architecturally infeasible as a 'standard logging pipeline' component, especially under lifetime retention obligations. Article 12's concrete requirements—structured event logs capturing start/end timestamps, reference-DB identifiers, matched input records, and verifier identity (Art. 12(3))—are cleanly met by conventional structured-event infrastructure; bolting on a research-grade tensor-state capture layer introduces open-ended model-coupling complexity, unpredictable storage costs, and multi-quarter prototyping timelines with no commensurate regulatory requirement.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 15\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal \"P1043\" suggests:\n- Developing logs that capture internal reasoning paths (latent space snapshots, attention-map archiving)\n- Implementing these as integrated components of the standard high-risk system logging pipeline\n- Framing this as both compliance AND a scientific contribution\n\nLet me assess from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n1. **Latent space snapshots**: This is extremely expensive in terms of storage and computation. For models with large hidden dimensions, capturing full latent states at every layer for every inference is prohibitive. The data volume would be enormous - potentially gigabytes per inference step depending on model architecture. This makes \"integrated components of our standard high-risk system logging pipeline\" practically unworkable in most deployment scenarios.\n\n2. **Attention-map archiving**: Similarly, attention maps for large transformer models (especially with long context windows) generate massive data volumes. For a model with 100+ layers and sequences of thousands of tokens, you're looking at terabytes of attention data per day of operation. This is not a realistic logging component for production systems.\n\n3. **Integration into \"standard high-risk system logging pipeline\"**: The EU AI Act's Article 12 requires recording of events relevant to traceability - specific things like start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, person identification (for Annex III point 1(a) systems). It does NOT require internal model state capture. Building an entirely new infrastructure for latent space and attention map storage is massively over-engineered relative to what the regulation actually mandates.\n\n4. **Prototyping speed**: The scope here is enormous. Developing a pipeline that captures, stores, indexes, and makes queryable latent states and attention maps across all layers of potentially multiple models would be a multi-year infrastructure project. This severely impacts our ability to prototype quickly or deliver working compliance solutions on timelines.\n\n5. **Relevance to actual Article 12 requirements**: The regulation asks for logs that enable: (a) identifying risk situations, (b) facilitating post-market monitoring, (c) monitoring operation. While internal states *could* theoretically help with these, the regulation specifically enumerates what's needed (especially in paragraph 3). There's a much simpler path to compliance - structured event logging with relevant metadata. The proposal conflates \"comprehensible information on how systems perform\" from Recital 71 with \"capture every internal tensor state.\"\n\n6. **Practical issues**: \n   - Storage costs at scale would be prohibitive\n   - Data retention obligations (lifetime of system) make this even worse\n   - GDPR implications for storing such detailed operational data\n   - Model architecture changes would break the logging pipeline (different models have different latent dimensions, different numbers of layers)\n   - Debugging/maintenance complexity increases enormously\n\n7. **The \"scientific contribution\" framing**: While appealing, from a prototyping/engineering standpoint this introduces research uncertainty into what should be an engineering task with clear requirements. Research initiatives have unpredictable timelines and outcomes. My function needs reliable, deliverable solutions on schedule.\n\n**What Article 12 actually requires technically:**\n- Automatic recording of events (logs) - straightforward event logging\n- For Annex III 1(a) systems: start/end times, reference DB, input data for matches, person identification - all standard structured data\n- The \"comprehensible information\" in Recital 71 refers to technical documentation, not necessarily internal model state capture\n\n**Verdict:** This is technically over-scoped, has prohibitive cost/storage implications, introduces research uncertainty into an engineering compliance task, and doesn't align with what the regulation actually requires. From a prototyping speed perspective, it would tie up engineering resources for months/years on infrastructure that may not be necessary for compliance and whose \"scientific contribution\" angle is speculative. I should reject this.\n\nPromise score: This has some interesting ideas (internal state capture could aid interpretability research), but from my specific vantage point of technical viability and prototyping speed, the cost-to-benefit ratio is terrible, the scope is unbounded, and it doesn't map cleanly to what Article 12 actually requires. I'd put this at maybe 15-20 - there's a kernel of something useful (structured internal diagnostics for debugging), but as proposed it's not viable as a compliance logging solution within reasonable engineering timelines.\n",
