uickly (weeks to months). But getting it to a state where it reliably flags things that \"meet Article 12(2) risk criteria\" requires domain expertise in what those criteria mean operationally, plus enough labeled data.\n- The proposal says \"R&D project\" which suggests exploration rather than committed implementation, but the prompt says approving means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue it. So this would be a full build commitment.\n\n**Critical concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. The core technical challenge - defining what constitutes a \"risk meeting Article 12(2) criteria\" in log-space patterns - is actually more of a domain/legal question than a purely engineering one. Without clear signal definitions, the ML model has no well-defined target. This is a major viability risk.\n2. Training data problem: You need examples of actual Article 79(1) risks or substantial modifications occurring in your logs to train/supervise the detector. These are rare by design. Unsupervised anomaly detection might work but will have poor precision.\n3. The \"real-time\" requirement adds engineering complexity (streaming pipeline, low-latency inference) that multiplies prototyping time.\n4. However, the core architecture (log ingestion → feature extraction → anomaly scoring → alerting) is well-trodden. A prototype could be built in a reasonable timeframe.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe idea has merit as a direction - moving from passive logging to active monitoring is genuinely useful and aligns with Article 12(2)'s purpose. The core engineering (log analysis pipeline + anomaly detection) is technically feasible to prototype. However, the proposal as stated conflates several hard problems: defining regulatory risk patterns in log space, training data scarcity for rare events, real-time streaming infrastructure, and integration with compliance workflows. \n\nFrom a prototyping speed/quality standpoint, I can see a viable narrow prototype (e.g., \"scan logs for N sp