        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2153\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A formal, automated log-lifecycle policy is operationally necessary to keep Article 12(1) compliance sustainable over the system lifetime; tiered (hot-to-cold) storage preserves regulatory retrievability while containing cost, and a clearly defined deletion schedule aligned to legal retention limits prevents both premature destruction of evidence and uncontrolled data bloat that would itself create GDPR exposure. The principal compliance risk is the automated deletion component: it must be engineered with mandatory overrides for ongoing regulatory inquiries, litigation holds, and post-market safety investigations (Arts. 72–73), and 'lifetime' under Article 12(1) must be explicitly mapped to the system's declared end-of-life so that no log is purged before its statutory period expires.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 78\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P2153 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n**Proposal P2153: Log Lifecycle and Resource Management Policy**\n\nThe proposal addresses managing the volume and retention of mandatory logs through:\n- A formal policy + automated system\n- Tiered storage strategy (hot → cold migration)\n- Automated deletion schedule for data past legal retention limits\n- Balancing compliance with budget constraints\n\nLet me assess this from a Compliance Officer's perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Article 12(1) requires logs \"over the lifetime of the system.\" This is critical. The proposal acknowledges that logs must persist, and tiered storage (cold storage) keeps them accessible while reducing cost. This is sound.\n- A formal policy is appropriate for demonstrating compliance to regulators. Having a documented governance framework around log management shows organizational diligence.\n- Automated deletion \"past legal retention limits\" — this is actually important because retaining data beyond what's legally required creates its own GDPR/data protection issues. So automated deletion aligned with legal requirements is prudent.\n- The tiered approach (hot → cold) ensures logs remain retrievable for regulatory inspection, audit, or incident investigation while managing cost.\n\n**Concerns/Risks:**\n- \"Automated deletion schedule\" — this is the most dangerous part from a compliance standpoint. If the automated system deletes logs that are still needed (e.g., during an ongoing regulatory inquiry, litigation hold, or if the retention period calculation is wrong), that would be catastrophic. Article 12(1) says \"over the lifetime of the system.\" If we auto-delete before end-of-life, we're non-compliant. The proposal needs to be very careful here: deletion should only occur AFTER the legal retention limit AND after confirming no outstanding regulatory/litigation holds.\n- \"Low-cost cold storage\" — need to ensure that cold storage still allows reasonable retrieval times for regulatory inspections. If a regulator requests logs and it takes 48 hours to retrieve from cold storage, that could be a problem in urgent post-market safety investigations (Article 72/73).\n- The proposal says \"past its legal retention limit\" — but what IS the legal retention limit? Article 12(1) says \"over the lifetime of the system.\" For high-risk AI systems under Annex III point 1(a), there are specific requirements. The proposal needs to clearly define what constitutes end-of-lifetime and ensure no premature deletion.\n- Budget protection is a legitimate institutional concern, but the framing \"without allowing logging overhead to cannibalize the budget for our primary scientific research\" could be seen as potentially deprioritizing compliance. From a pure compliance standpoint, I need to make sure this doesn't lead to under-investment in logging capabilities.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable and necessary operational proposal. Every organization handling high-risk AI systems will face the log volume problem. A tiered storage approach with proper retention management is standard practice. The formal policy component shows governance maturity. However, the automated deletion piece needs very careful implementation safeguards (litigation holds, regulatory hold overrides, clear definition of \"lifetime\"). \n\nAs a Compliance Officer, I would approve this but with the understanding that it must be implemented with appropriate safeguards. The proposal as stated is directionally correct and addresses a real operational challenge. It's not a compliance innovation per se, but it's necessary infrastructure for maintaining compliance over time without blowing up costs (which threatens institutional survival).\n\nThe risk of NOT having such a policy is actually higher: unmanaged log growth could either lead to (a) budget crisis threatening the institution, or (b) ad-hoc deletion decisions that might violate retention requirements. A formal, automated approach with proper safeguards is the compliance-sound path.\n\nI'll approve this with a moderately high promise score — it's important and well-conceived but has implementation risks around the deletion component that need careful handling.\n",
