anual record-keeping tasks that typically slow down the transition from prototype to production.\" As a development engineer, I see this directly: every time we move a model forward, documenting what was done manually eats engineering hours. Automating this removes friction.\n- Building on MLflow (which we likely already use or are using) means low integration cost — no new infrastructure to stand up, just an exporter layer. This is a \"thin\" addition to existing tooling.\n- The structured output can also feed into other pipeline stages, making prototyping iterations faster because compliance documentation doesn't become a blocking step.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Custom exporter that maps technical metrics directly to EU AI Act requirements\" — the mapping layer needs careful legal input. If the regulation's requirement categories shift or are interpreted differently, the exporter needs updating. But this is a maintenance concern, not a viability blocker. The core engineering work (extract → transform → emit) is solid.\n- It doesn't fully cover Article 12(1)'s \"automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system\" — that's more about runtime event logging (start/end times, input data, reference databases for biometric systems). This proposal is more about development-phase documentation (hyperparameters, dataset versions, validation results). So it addresses part of the compliance picture but not all. However, from a prototyping speed perspective, the dev-phase documentation automation is genuinely high-value and technically sound.\n- The scope seems reasonable — not overreaching, not under-scoped for what it claims to do.\n\n**Decision:** This is technically very viable (builds on existing tooling, clear scope, standard engineering work), directly accelerates prototyping by removing manual documentation overhead, and the integration pattern (MLflow → exporter → structured report) is a well-understood architecture. I'd approve this.\n\n**Promise score