l documentation kept up to date, and automatic recording by means of logs. The proposal supports the \"automatic recording\" aspect well.\n\n**My professional assessment as Compliance Officer:**\n\nPositives:\n- Directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) — automatic logging without researcher burden\n- Reduces risk of non-compliance due to developer oversight (the #1 failure mode in practice is that developers forget or fail to implement logging properly)\n- \"Unified\" and \"standardized\" approach ensures consistency across projects, which is critical for auditing\n- Removes the \"tedious manual burden\" — this is a key compliance risk because when logging is optional/manual, it gets skipped under time pressure\n- Technical consistency aids regulatory audits — regulators can see uniform log formats across systems\n\nConcerns:\n- The proposal as described captures \"start/end times and input metadata by default\" which covers Article 12(3)(a) partially but doesn't explicitly address the reference database (12(3)(b)), match data (12(3)(c)), or natural person identification (12(3)(d)) for Annex III 1(a) systems\n- The proposal is an enabling infrastructure — it's necessary but not sufficient. You still need to ensure the middleware captures all required event types, maintains logs over the system lifetime, and that logs are accessible for regulatory inspection\n- \"Lightweight wrapper\" language raises a question: can it scale to capture all the depth of information needed? For instance, \"input metadata\" is vague — does it include the actual input data or just metadata about it?\n\nHowever, from a compliance standpoint, this is a fundamentally sound and important investment. The single biggest risk in Article 12 compliance is that individual project teams implement logging inconsistently or incompletely. A unified middleware approach:\n- Ensures baseline compliance (start/end times) by default\n- Creates an audit trail showing the institution takes re