e proposal focuses more on operational logging than full technical documentation, but the \"audit-ready repository\" language suggests awareness of broader compliance needs.\n\nNow from my Compliance Officer perspective:\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses Article 12(1)'s automatic recording requirement\n- Standardization ensures institutional consistency — critical for an R&D institution with multiple projects\n- Immutable storage protects against tampering (audit integrity)\n- Reduces researcher burden, which is important because researchers won't voluntarily implement compliance measures consistently\n- \"Audit-ready\" language shows awareness of market surveillance and regulatory inspection needs\n- Replaces \"fragmented ad-hoc logging\" — this is exactly the kind of gap that gets flagged in regulatory audits\n- The shared API approach is practical for an R&D institution with many different projects/models\n\nConcerns:\n- The proposal says \"permanent storage\" — Article 12 requires logs over \"the lifetime of the system,\" which is a defined period. Permanent could be broader than required, but it's not harmful from compliance standpoint (having more data isn't a violation).\n- It doesn't explicitly mention the specific logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (Article 12(3)). However, as a framework, it should accommodate those.\n- \"Minimizes individual researcher burden\" — while well-intentioned, I need to ensure this doesn't lead to under-documentation of risk events. The framework needs to be designed with the specific event categories in mind, not just generic metadata capture.\n- The proposal is a foundational infrastructure investment, which is exactly what compliance requires before you can demonstrate systematic compliance across multiple AI systems.\n\nFrom institutional survival perspective: Without standardized logging, each project would develop its own approach, creating inconsistency that regulators would flag during market surveillance. This framework reduces that risk significantly.\n\nFrom market reputation perspective: Having a unified, auditable logging infrastructure is a strong signal to customers and partners that the institution takes compliance seriously. It's a competitive differentiator in enterprise AI markets where buyers increasingly require regulatory compliance evidence.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a high-value proposal from my vantage point. It addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) directly, provides the infrastructure needed for traceability under 12(2), and can be extended to meet 12(3). The standardization aspect is critical for an institution with multiple projects. The main gap is that it's somewhat generic and doesn't explicitly enumerate the specific event categories required by different articles (79, 72, 26(5)) or the Annex III specific requirements. But as a framework/foundation layer, it's the right architectural decision.\n\nI would approve this. It's fundamentally what Article 12 requires us to build. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-88. It's not a perfect proposal because it lacks specificity on which events to capture for each regulatory trigger and doesn't explicitly address Annex III point 1(a) requirements, but the architectural direction is exactly right and the implementation approach (API wrapper, immutable storage) is sound.\n\nI'll give it an 85 — strong approval with the understanding that detailed specification work will be needed to ensure all sub-requirements of Article 12 are mapped to specific capture fields in the framework.\n",
