. \"Plug-and-play\" lowers the barrier for new projects to start compliant, reducing onboarding time and resource burn at project inception.\n\n**Cons/Concerns:**\n1. Upfront development cost: Building a standardized middleware library requires dedicated engineering resources before it delivers value. This is a fixed cost that must be absorbed from the lab's budget. If the lab has few high-risk projects currently, the ROI might be slow.\n2. \"Integrated into all internal AI pipelines\" — this could be a significant integration effort if existing pipelines are heterogeneous. The cost of retrofitting or rebuilding interfaces across diverse infrastructure could be substantial.\n3. Maintenance burden: A unified framework becomes a critical shared dependency. If it breaks, all projects are affected. This creates a single point of failure and requires ongoing maintenance resources.\n4. It may not address the full scope of Article 12 — the regulation requires specific things for Annex III point 1(a) systems (recording periods, reference databases, input data matching, natural person identification). The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input references, and user identifiers\" which maps somewhat but isn't a complete mapping. However, the proposal is about the framework architecture, not the specific fields, so this might be acceptable as a design principle.\n5. Risk of over-engineering: If only 2-3 projects are truly high-risk, a full \"unified framework\" might be more infrastructure than needed. A lighter-weight solution per project might be more resource-efficient at small scale.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is strongly aligned with resource-allocation efficiency. The core value proposition — amortize compliance engineering across projects, reduce per-project overhead, leverage existing pipeline infrastructure through a middleware layer — is exactly the kind of structural investment that makes sense for an R&D lab managing multiple projects unde