fication/categorization). This limits applicability but also focuses resources.\n  - \"Automatically feed these identities into the Article 12 logs\" — this is good; it addresses the automatic recording requirement of Article 12(1).\n  - The proposal frames itself as satisfying a specific legal requirement, which is clear and auditable from a compliance standpoint.\n\nFrom an institutional survival perspective:\n- This is a relatively contained project. It's not asking for a full platform overhaul; it's a specialized module/interface.\n- However, committing resources to build this NOW means the lab assumes it will deploy Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric identification). If the lab's actual product pipeline doesn't include such systems, this is premature resource commitment. But I should judge on merits — if they ARE developing such systems, this is necessary.\n- The proposal is reasonable in scope but somewhat narrow. It only addresses Article 12(3)(d) and partially (b) through the logging feed. It doesn't address (a), (b), or (c) of paragraph 3 comprehensively.\n\nFrom a market reputation perspective:\n- Having a clear, auditable verification portal demonstrates regulatory seriousness to clients and regulators.\n- The MFA aspect adds trust signals.\n- However, if the implementation is seen as checkbox compliance rather than integrated into the broader system, it could look superficial.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a reasonable, targeted proposal that addresses a specific legal requirement. It's not overreaching in scope, and it's clearly tied to regulatory text. The main concerns are: (1) whether \"forcing confirmation\" is proportionate or misreads the law; (2) MFA may be scope creep beyond what Article 12 strictly demands; (3) it only covers one sub-requirement of paragraph 3, which could create a false sense of completeness.\n\nI think this clears a reasonable bar for approval from a compliance perspective — it's directly responsive to a legal obligation, app