        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate proposal P2381 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution whose domain is \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\"\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n\"Automated Compliance Documentation Pipeline\" - A tool that automatically generates technical documentation by aggregating metadata from training runs and operational system logs. It bridges the gap between raw logs and human-readable compliance reports. Integrates dataset versioning and hyperparameter tracking with a reporting engine that maps these directly to regulatory requirements. Triggers updates upon every major model version change. Ensures documentation remains current without manual overhead to researchers.\n\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability, speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- This is fundamentally an ETL/reporting pipeline: collect metadata from training runs (MLflow, Weights & Biases, etc.), collect operational logs, map them to regulatory fields, generate reports.\n- The components are well-understood engineering problems: data aggregation, version tracking (dataset versioning via DVC, LakeFS, or similar; hyperparameter tracking via existing MLOps tools), template-based report generation, event-triggered updates.\n- The \"mapping to regulatory requirements\" part is where it gets tricky — you need a maintained mapping layer between technical metadata fields and Article 12 / Annex III requirements. This is doable but requires legal/compliance input to define the schema.\n- Triggering on \"major model version change\" is straightforward with CI/CD hooks or event-driven architecture.\n- The overall architecture is technically sound and buildable with existing tooling. It's not a research problem — it's an engineering integration problem.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This could be prototyped relatively quickly because the building blocks exist