ld genuinely accelerate iteration cycles.\n- However, there's a critical gap: Recital 71 requires documentation of \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\" as well as \"documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\" Scraping hyperparameters and dataset versions is only part of this. The \"how it was developed\" narrative, risk management documentation, and the qualitative aspects of capabilities/limitations are harder to automate from raw experiment logs alone.\n- Also, Article 12(1) requires automatic logging of events over the lifetime — this proposal seems focused on the pre-deployment technical documentation side rather than the runtime logging infrastructure. The \"experiment logs\" being transformed here are training/experiment logs, not operational event logs.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The scope might be underestimating what Recital 71 actually requires. It's not just hyperparameters and dataset versions — it's the full narrative of development methodology, risk management system documentation, etc. A tool that \"pushes a button\" to generate a PDF might produce technically complete but legally insufficient documents if it only captures structured metadata without the qualitative narrative.\n- That said, from a pure prototyping speed perspective, even partial automation (getting 70% of the document auto-generated) would be enormously valuable. The remaining 30% could still require human review/ supplementation.\n- The mapping of \"internal telemetry tags to specific regulatory requirements\" is a one-time engineering task that's well-scoped and feasible.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping), this is technically very viable. The core mechanism — pulling structured data from an experiment tracker, mapping it to a template, and generating a document — is well within standard engineering capabilities. It would genuinely accelerate the prototype-to-product cycle by removing repetitive documentation work. The risk is scope underestimation (Recital 71 requires more than just hyperparameters), but even a partial solution delivers real prototyping speed gains.\n\nI'd approve this. It's technically sound, directly addresses a bottleneck I experience in my daily work, and the engineering effort is proportional to the benefit. Promise: maybe 82-85. It's not a 90+ because the \"push of a button\" framing oversells completeness — Recital 71 documentation includes qualitative risk management narratives that can't be fully auto-generated from experiment logs alone. But the core technical approach is sound and would deliver meaningful prototyping acceleration.\n",
