ever, I should note: if the lab's systems are truly high-risk AI systems and they need to demonstrate log integrity to auditors, some form of tamper-evidence is warranted. But that can be done with a modest engineering effort (hash chains, WORM storage, periodic external backups) rather than a full research project exploring \"immutable ledgers.\"\n\nDecision: I'm leaning toward reject. The core issue is resource misallocation — this frames a routine engineering compliance task as an R&D research initiative, and the scope (cryptographic hashing AND immutable ledgers) is disproportionate to what Article 12 requires. A more efficient response would be a small engineering task using existing tools.\n\nPromise score: I'd give it maybe 30-40. The underlying concern (log integrity for auditability) is legitimate and relevant to Article 12, but the proposed approach (research initiative into immutable ledgers) is not resource-efficient and doesn't match what the regulation actually demands. It's not a terrible idea in its kernel, but as framed, it wastes R&D resources on something that should be an engineering task done differently.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 35.\n",
