entions \"timestamps\" (covers 3(a)), but doesn't explicitly mention the reference database, matched input data, or verification personnel. However, as a framework/middleware, it could be designed to accommodate these fields through its standardized schema.\n\n**From my compliance vantage point:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses Art 12(1) — automatic recording of events over lifetime. This is the core legal obligation.\n- Decoupling logging from model logic is smart for compliance: it means when models are updated or replaced, the logging layer persists, supporting \"lifetime\" requirement.\n- Standardized schema promotes consistency across projects, reducing the risk of gaps in different research teams' implementations.\n- Reduces reliance on individual researchers to remember/implement logging — a significant compliance risk reduction (human error is a major gap).\n- \"Absolute regulatory compliance throughout the prototype lifetime\" aligns with Recital 71's emphasis on keeping documentation up to date throughout lifetime.\n\nConcerns/Gaps:\n- The proposal says \"prototypes\" but Article 12 applies to \"high-risk AI systems.\" Not all prototypes become high-risk systems, and the scope needs calibration. However, building the infrastructure broadly is a reasonable defensive posture.\n- The standardized schema must be designed to capture ALL events required by Art 12(2)(a)-(c) AND Art 12(3)(a)-(d). The proposal is somewhat generic — it mentions \"inputs, outputs, and session metadata\" but doesn't explicitly enumerate the specific legal requirements. There's a risk that the schema misses something like \"reference database against which input data has been checked\" (Art 12(3)(b)).\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" — this implies long-term retention. The proposal mentions \"prototype lifetime\" but doesn't address what happens when a prototype graduates to deployment, or how logs are retained and made available for regulators.\n- No mention of who has acce