ted is still valuable - it's addressing the \"technical documentation\" part which IS required (Recital 71 mentions both). And Article 12(3) says \"logging capabilities shall provide, at a minimum...\" for Annex III 1(a), but Articles 12(1)-(2) apply to all high-risk systems.\n\n**My assessment from the prototyping speed/quality angle:**\n- This is technically very viable and well-scoped for what it addresses (documentation generation).\n- It directly serves my interest in not slowing prototyping with manual documentation burden.\n- The implementation is straightforward: template + data source + CI trigger.\n- Main concern: scope creep. If this balloons into trying to also handle runtime logging, it becomes a much harder problem. But as stated, it's focused and achievable.\n- It doesn't fully satisfy Article 12 on its own (misses the runtime logging requirement), but it addresses a real and necessary component of compliance that would otherwise slow down engineering teams.\n\nI think this is approve-worthy from my vantage point. It's technically sound, well-scoped, directly improves prototyping speed by automating a manual burden, and the implementation path is clear. The promise score should reflect that while it's valuable, it only addresses part of Article 12 (the documentation side, not the runtime logging side), so I can't give it a perfect score from a \"does this solve our compliance problem\" perspective - but from \"will this help my team prototype faster and with higher quality,\" it scores well.\n\nI'll approve with a promise around 78-82. It's clearly viable, addresses a real pain point for prototyping speed, the tech is mature (MLflow + CI/CD templating), but it only partially addresses Article 12 (documentation yes, runtime logging no). From pure prototyping speed perspective it's strong; from full compliance perspective it's incomplete but still valuable.\n\nLet me settle on 80.\n",
