g but vague. The actual regulation specifies what must be logged. For Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric verification), the data being logged is specific and bounded. Privacy concerns are more about access control and retention policies than about the logging mechanism itself.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Assessment:**\n- This proposal commits a \"small team\" to an R&D/experimentation project. From my vantage point, this is concerning because:\n  1. The regulation has clear requirements that can be met with standard engineering approaches (structured logging, append-only logs, hash-chained records). We don't need a research initiative - we need implementation.\n  2. A \"research initiative\" implies uncertainty and exploration, which slows down prototyping. My function is about speed and quality of delivering working prototypes. Committing resources to experiment with formats when off-the-shelf solutions exist delays the actual delivery.\n  3. The framing as \"transforms a regulatory requirement into a technical innovation\" sounds good for institutional prestige but doesn't address what I need: a clear, implementable logging architecture that meets Article 12's requirements within acceptable performance budgets.\n  4. The scope is too broad (\"high-throughput AI systems\") - we need to focus on the specific Annex III categories we're building for, each with different logging needs per Article 12(3).\n\n**Counter-arguments (steel-manning):**\n- If our systems genuinely have extreme throughput requirements (e.g., real-time biometric verification at scale), then the performance overhead of continuous logging could be a genuine technical challenge worth investigating.\n- Privacy-preserving aspects might matter if we're processing sensitive biometric data where even log records need protection.\n- Having some R&D headroom for optimization is reasonable.\n\n**My decision:**\nThe proposal as framed is too much \"research\" and not enough \"engineering.\" The technica