er se.\n\n**From my vantage point specifically:**\n- This is a high-efficiency play: low marginal cost (scripts), leverages existing infrastructure (MLflow), frees scarce resource (scientist time) for research.\n- It directly addresses the \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" concern — we already have MLflow, we just need to add an output format.\n- The resource commitment is modest: a few engineer-hours to build and maintain scripts, versus ongoing scientist hours spent on manual documentation.\n- Risk: if the templates don't capture all required elements or if the automated extraction misses context, you could end up with non-compliant documentation that still requires human correction — partially negating the efficiency gain. But even partial automation saves significant time.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It's cost-effective, leverages existing infrastructure, and directly addresses a recurring resource drain (scientist time on compliance paperwork). The implementation cost is low relative to the benefit. I'd approve this with good confidence.\n\nPromise score: I'd rate this around 78-82. It's a clear efficiency win, but there are some implementation risks around completeness of automated documentation and the need for human validation that prevent me from going higher. It's not a perfect solution (you still need someone to review), but it's a strong net positive for resource allocation.\n\nLet me settle on 80 — it's a clear \"yes\" from my perspective, well-aligned with my mandate, but not so revolutionary or risk-free that I'd give it 90+.\n",
