        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director focused on resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal P2667 is about an \"Immutable Provenance Ledger for High-Risk Systems\" - a WORM (Write-Once-Read-Many) storage architecture to archive logs and reference database snapshots with tamper-proof guarantees, API key access control, integrated into cloud deployment pipeline.\n\nLet me assess this from my domain of concern:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- A dedicated WORM storage backend is a significant infrastructure investment. Is it necessary? Article 12 requires \"technical allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" It doesn't specifically mandate immutability or tamper-proofing per se - it requires logs be recorded and available for traceability, compliance verification, and post-market monitoring.\n- However, from a practical standpoint, if you're building a logging system anyway, adding immutability features (like append-only constraints, cryptographic hashing of entries) is relatively low marginal cost compared to building the entire WORM storage architecture as a separate dedicated backend.\n- The proposal frames this as a new \"secure, write-once storage architecture\" - that sounds like a significant new infrastructure build rather than leveraging existing logging and storage capabilities with some added immutability guarantees.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- This is a compliance/infrastructure project, not a scientific one. It doesn't advance our R&D mission directly.\n- The question is: does the regulation actually require this level of engineering? Article 12(1) says \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" The key requirement is that logs are recorded and available. Recital 71