tion option rather than a new system to build, this becomes much cheaper. The proposal says \"implement a WORM storage backend integrated into our cloud deployment pipeline\" - if this means configuring existing cloud services with immutability policies, that's reasonable and low-cost. If it means building custom WORM infrastructure, that's overkill.\n\nThe language is ambiguous but leans toward a new implementation (\"designed to archive,\" \"implement a WORM storage backend\"). \n\nFrom my vantage point:\n- The need is legitimate (compliance requires logging)\n- The solution as described risks being over-engineered relative to what the regulation demands\n- It may not leverage existing infrastructure optimally\n- The resource commitment for a dedicated architecture is significant when simpler approaches could meet the regulatory bar\n- But it's not frivolous - there IS a real need, and some form of tamper-resistance is prudent\n\nI'd lean toward reject or low-approve. The proposal conflates \"logs must be recorded\" with \"we need a new dedicated WORM storage architecture.\" A more efficient approach would be to configure existing logging with append-only semantics and access controls. Building a bespoke \"provenance ledger\" system is more ambition than the regulation demands, and it competes for engineering resources that could go toward actual R&D or simpler compliance measures.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal isn't insane - it's addressing a real requirement in a defensible way. But from a resource-efficiency standpoint, it's building a new system when configuration changes to existing systems would likely suffice. The \"high level of technical assurance needed to satisfy EU auditors\" is somewhat speculative - the regulation doesn't explicitly require WORM or cryptographic immutability.\n\nI'll reject this as over-engineered for the regulatory requirement, consuming scarce engineering resources that could be more efficiently deployed. But I'll give it a moderate promise score because the underlying need is real and part of what's proposed (access control, logging integration) is useful even if the full WORM architecture isn't justified.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, with a promise around 35-45. The core insight (logs need integrity protection) is correct, but the solution over-commits resources relative to what Article 12 actually requires and relative to simpler alternatives that leverage existing infrastructure.\n",
