uirements.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Immutable ledger\" — this could be expensive infrastructure-wise. Blockchain or similar immutable storage can be costly. But it might just mean append-only logging with cryptographic hashing, which is more modest. The term is somewhat vague but the intent (auditability) is clear.\n- Scope: \"across all high-risk prototypes\" — this is ambitious but appropriate since we need compliance for all high-risk systems. However, implementing a middleware layer in ALL pipelines upfront could be a large resource commitment before we know which projects will actually qualify as high-risk under the Act. There's a risk of over-investing if only some prototypes end up being deployed as high-risk systems.\n- \"Raw input data\" capture — storage costs could be significant, especially for training data. This has ongoing infrastructure and cost implications.\n- The proposal is an architectural commitment — once you build this middleware layer into pipelines, it's hard to remove or modify. That's a resource lock-in consideration.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is actually a well-conceived approach for resource efficiency. Rather than each research group building its own ad-hoc logging (which would be wasteful and inconsistent), a single modular framework leverages existing pipeline infrastructure and distributes the compliance cost across all projects. The middleware approach means researchers don't need to individually implement logging — it's automated at the infrastructure level. This protects scientific ambition by keeping prototyping cycles fast.\n\nThe main risk is over-building (implementing for \"all\" high-risk prototypes before we know which ones will actually be deployed), but a modular library should allow incremental adoption. The storage cost of raw input data is a consideration but manageable.\n\nI think this clears the bar from my perspective. It's resource-efficient, leverages existing infrastructure, protects re