urces to a broad research project on standardized logging protocols, while potentially neglecting the core compliance work of ensuring YOUR high-risk AI systems actually meet Article 12's requirements in practice, is a risk.\n- However, if done right, having a well-defined internal logging standard (even if also open-sourced) directly supports compliance with Article 12 and makes audit readiness much easier.\n\n**Market Reputation:**\n- Being seen as the entity that defined the global logging standard for AI compliance could be extremely valuable reputationally. It positions the lab as a thought leader.\n- But it also creates expectations - if your standard becomes de facto industry practice, any gap in YOUR implementation is more visible.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal has genuine compliance value but is over-scoped and misframed for what Article 12 actually requires. The core compliance need is: ensure our high-risk AI systems technically allow automatic logging of the specific events listed in 12(2) and 12(3). A standardized internal schema that maps to those requirements would be highly valuable. But framing this as a \"research project dedicated to creating a standardized, interoperable protocol across diverse architectures\" with an open-source component is much larger than what compliance demands.\n\nThat said, the idea of having a clear, documented schema for logging that directly maps to Article 12's requirements IS something I'd support. The question is whether this proposal as written - with its broad scope and research framing - clears my bar.\n\nI think this partially aligns with compliance needs but overreaches in scope and adds unnecessary risk (becoming the \"global standard\" bearer). The core value for compliance would be: define the logging schema that meets Article 12(2) and 12(3), implement it across our systems, document it as part of technical documentation. That's different from building an open-source API for \"diverse high-risk AI architectures.\"\n\nI'm leaning toward reject with a moderate promise score. It has elements I'd want (structured logging schema aligned to Article 12), but the scope and framing don't match what compliance actually requires, and it commits scarce resources to something beyond our regulatory obligation. The \"open-source\" and \"global standards\" aspects add reputational upside but also risk without addressing the immediate compliance need directly.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"The lab will develop an open-source API and schema that allows different systems to export telemetry in a unified, machine-readable format.\" If this is primarily for OUR systems (our diverse high-risk AI architectures), then it's directly relevant to Article 12(1) compliance - ensuring all our systems can log consistently. The \"open-source\" aspect is an add-on. And the mapping to 12(2)(a),(b),(c) and potentially 12(3) would make this very valuable for audit readiness.\n\nBut the language \"across diverse high-risk AI architectures\" suggests it's meant to be broader than just our systems - it's an industry standard project. And \"positions the institution as a primary scientific authority in defining the global standards\" confirms the ambition is beyond internal compliance.\n\nI'll reject it because: (1) the scope exceeds what Article 12 requires of us, (2) it commits resources to a research/standards project rather than ensuring our systems comply, (3) the \"global standard\" positioning creates additional regulatory exposure, and (4) while I'd absolutely want a well-defined internal logging schema mapped to Article 12, that's a different (smaller) project from what this proposes.\n\nPromise score: Maybe 35-40. There's genuine value in the underlying idea for compliance, but as framed it doesn't clear my bar for resource commitment.\n",
