d; this is about surfacing and formatting it rather than creating new data pipelines from scratch.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Is the scope realistic? Recital 71 asks for comprehensive documentation including risk-management systems, capabilities, limitations, etc. Some of that (like risk assessments) may not be easily auto-generated from training run metadata alone. The proposal might overstate what \"auto-generate\" can cover.\n- However, even partial automation of the data-heavy components (algorithms, datasets, validation metrics) would free significant researcher time for the judgment-intensive parts.\n- There's a question of whether this is truly a small project or a larger integration effort. But the framing as \"hooks into existing software\" suggests it's scoped appropriately.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis proposal scores very well from my vantage point. It:\n1. Uses existing infrastructure (experiment tracking) rather than building new systems\n2. Directly addresses researcher time burden (resource efficiency)\n3. Protects scientific output while meeting compliance\n4. Is reasonably scoped to what can be automated from available metadata\n5. Has clear, bounded deliverables\n\nThe main risk is scope creep or underestimating the integration complexity, but the core concept is sound and well-aligned with resource constraints. I'd approve this with a high promise score. Maybe not a perfect 100 because there are some questions about how much of Recital 71's documentation can truly be \"auto-generated\" vs. requiring human judgment (risk management descriptions, system limitations narratives), but the core value proposition is strong.\n\nI'll give it around 82-85. It's clearly worth pursuing from a resource-efficiency standpoint, and the existing-infrastructure angle makes it low-risk in terms of capital commitment. The slight deduction is for uncertainty about how much of the full Recital 71 documentation scope can be truly automated vs. requiring manual input.