iews of all active R&D streams\" which suggests ongoing overhead even for stable projects.\n- The cross-functional committee model tends to produce slow decisions and political compromises rather than crisp technical guidance.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my engineering vantage point, the core insight (know your compliance tier early so you build the right logging) is valid and useful. But the mechanism proposed (monthly cross-functional board reviewing ALL active R&D streams) is heavyweight governance that would slow down prototyping cycles. The overhead of a standing committee meeting monthly across all functions is significant and doesn't scale with the actual number of projects or their complexity.\n\nA lighter-weight approach — like a one-time classification at project initiation, embedded in the existing project charter process, with re-classification triggers on scope change — would achieve the same technical benefit without the ongoing governance overhead. The proposal as written adds a recurring meeting cadence and a committee structure that introduces latency into my prototyping workflow.\n\nI'm leaning toward reject. The underlying need is real (know your logging requirements early), but the proposed mechanism is misaligned with fast prototyping. It's a governance solution to what could be a simpler process step. The monthly review of ALL streams is particularly problematic — most R&D projects won't change classification month to month, so you're burning engineering time attending or waiting on these reviews.\n\nPromise score: The idea has some value (maybe 30-40 range) but as proposed it's the wrong mechanism for my domain. I'll say around 25. It addresses a real problem but in a way that would slow me down rather than help me build faster and better prototypes.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 25.\n",
