fication).\n- Reducing per-project engineering overhead means the lab can actually achieve compliance across ALL high-risk projects rather than just some — this is an institutional survival concern. If you have 50 high-risk projects and only 10 have proper logging, you're non-compliant on 40.\n- \"Over the lifetime\" aspect: a centralized framework is more likely to persist and be maintained over time compared to ad-hoc per-project solutions that get abandoned.\n\n**Potential concerns/gaps:**\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamping, input tracking, and user identification\" but doesn't explicitly mention recording of risk situations (12(2)(a)), post-market monitoring events (12(2)(b)), or operational monitoring (12(2)(c)). However, a well-designed framework would presumably capture these as configurable event types.\n- It says \"high-risk AI projects\" — good scope alignment.\n- The proposal is about building infrastructure, which requires significant engineering resources upfront but pays dividends in ongoing compliance.\n- From market reputation: having a robust, auditable logging system demonstrates regulatory seriousness to clients and partners. If a regulator comes auditing, being able to produce complete, consistent logs across all projects is far better than piecemeal documentation.\n\n**Risk of NOT doing this:**\n- Without centralized logging, each project team interprets \"automatic recording\" differently → compliance gaps.\n- If a serious incident occurs (Art 79), the lab needs to demonstrate what happened. Fragmented logs = inability to trace = regulatory sanction + reputational damage.\n- The lifetime requirement means logs must be maintained and retrievable for years — a centralized system handles this better than distributed ones that get deleted when projects end.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is squarely on-target for Article 12 compliance. It addresses the core requirement (automatic logging over lifetime), maps to specific sub-requirements in para 3, r