1 requires (documentation of \"training, testing and validation processes\").\n- Reducing manual burden helps ensure compliance actually happens consistently rather than being a periodic pain point.\n\nConcerns/limitations:\n- The proposal focuses on \"technical documentation\" (Recital 71 territory) but Article 12(1)-(3) is specifically about *logging capabilities* — the system must technically allow for automatic recording of events over its lifetime. This is an engineering/architectural requirement in the AI system itself, not just a documentation pipeline. The proposal doesn't clearly address the logging infrastructure required by Art. 12(1).\n- \"Transforms existing R&D metadata\" assumes metadata exists and is sufficiently granular. In practice, researchers may not capture all necessary data points (e.g., reference database identifiers for biometric matching under Art. 12(3)(b), identification of natural persons in verification under Art. 12(3)(d)).\n- The proposal says \"standardized reports\" — but compliance documentation must be specific to each system, its intended purpose, and the risk profile. A one-size-fits-all template may not meet the \"appropriate to the intended purpose\" standard of Art. 12(2).\n- \"Preserves scientific productivity\" framing is a researcher-centric justification; from compliance standpoint, the value is in ensuring completeness, accuracy, and audit-readiness.\n- The pipeline generates documentation FROM existing tools — but if those tools don't capture what Article 12 requires (especially the specific logging requirements for high-risk systems), the pipeline will generate incomplete documentation. It's a downstream solution to an upstream problem.\n\nHowever, as a compliance officer:\n- This is genuinely useful infrastructure. The EU AI Act's technical documentation requirements are substantial and must be maintained throughout the system's lifetime. Having automated generation from source-of-truth data (VC, experiment trackers)