're 3 months from delivery and realize our logging architecture doesn't support Article 12\" scenario that would blow up budgets.\n- It's a process change, not a capital expenditure — very well suited to tight funding constraints.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Leverages existing project management milestones (which already exist for grant reporting, internal reviews, etc.). Adding a compliance checkpoint to an existing milestone is far cheaper than creating new governance structures.\n- The Compliance Officer role presumably exists; quarterly review is a modest time commitment compared to ad-hoc end-of-project fire-drills.\n- Templates create institutional memory and reduce dependency on individual researchers' compliance knowledge.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Mandatory\" could be seen as bureaucratic overhead by research teams, potentially creating resistance or superficial compliance (filling templates without real understanding). But from a resource-efficiency standpoint, standardization is still better than nothing.\n- The workshop series has an upfront time cost that needs to be scheduled within existing workloads — no incremental budget line mentioned. This could mean it competes with active research for researcher hours in the short term.\n- However, the ROI (avoiding costly retrofits) clearly outweighs the short-term training investment.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-designed, low-cost process improvement that directly addresses the most expensive failure mode of Article 12 compliance: discovering at project completion that logging and documentation requirements weren't engineered in from the start. It uses existing infrastructure (milestones, compliance officer, project templates) and doesn't require new funding. The resource-efficiency argument is strong. From my vantage point, this is exactly the kind of \"do more with what we have\" approach that fits a constrained R&D lab.\n\nI would approve this. Promise score