        "reasoning_content": "The proposal P2127 is about creating a \"Prototyping Sandbox Policy\" that distinguishes between exploratory research and high-risk development, creating a tiered approach to Article 12 record-keeping obligations. The idea is that full logging requirements only kick in once a project reaches a certain TRL or risk profile, keeping early prototypes in a low-friction environment.\n\nLet me assess this from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with:\n- Resource-allocation efficiency\n- Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints\n- Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\n\nMy professional lens: I care about whether this proposal efficiently allocates our scarce implementation resources, whether it balances the lab's scientific ambitions against what we can actually fund and staff, and whether it makes good use of what we already have.\n\nAssessment:\n\n**Rationale:** The core insight is partially sound from a resource-allocation perspective — you don't want to commit full logging infrastructure (hardware, storage, personnel for maintenance) to projects that are still in the exploratory phase and may never reach high-risk deployment. That's genuinely efficient use of scarce resources. HOWEVER, there's a critical regulatory problem: Article 12 applies to \"high-risk AI systems\" as defined by the Act. The Act doesn't have TRL-based exemptions. A high-risk system is determined by its intended purpose and classification (Annex III), not by its development stage. You can't unilaterally decide that because a prototype is at TRL 3, it's exempt from Article 12 — if the system IS classified as high-risk by its intended purpose, the logging requirements apply. The \"tiered approach\" would be creating an internal policy that contradicts regulatory obligations.\n\nBut wait — let me think more carefully. If a project is in early prototyping and hasn't yet been deployed or marketed as a high-risk AI system, does Article 12 technically apply? The Act applies to providers of high-risk AI systems placed on the market or put into service. An internal prototype that isn't being marketed might not trigger the full obligations yet. However, if you're developing it WITH THE INTENT to make it high-risk (which is what this lab does), then as a provider, you'd need to have these capabilities ready by the time you place it on market or put it into service.\n\nThe resource-allocation angle: Building logging infrastructure only at a later stage means you might need to retrofit it into systems that weren't designed with it in mind — that's actually MORE expensive and less efficient than building it in from the architecture phase. Retrofitting is a classic resource waste.\n\nAlso, \"preventing premature logging constraints from stifling early-stage ideas\" — this sounds good but the actual cost of basic event logging (timestamps, input/output records) at the prototype stage is minimal compared to the full production system. The \"industrial bookkeeping\" framing overstates the burden. A simple log file isn't really what's killing innovation.\n\nThe classification system itself requires governance overhead — who classifies? When does a project cross the TRL threshold? What are the exact criteria? This creates an additional administrative layer that consumes management time and attention.\n\nFrom my vantage point: The proposal addresses a real tension (not over-constraining early research), but its solution is misaligned with regulatory reality and likely less resource-efficient than it appears. A simpler internal practice of \"we implement basic logging architecture from TRL 4 onward\" would achieve the same goal without creating a formal policy apparatus that implies regulatory non-compliance. The proposal as stated risks being read by regulators as an attempt to delay compliance, which creates legal risk that consumes even more resources in legal counsel time.\n\nI'd lean toward reject or low-approve. It's not harmful per se — having an internal policy about when you start implementing full logging is reasonable operational planning. But the framing as a \"tiered approach\" to Article 12 obligations is problematic, and the governance overhead of maintaining a classification system is a resource cost that may exceed the benefit at our scale.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says it creates a \"tiered approach to record-keeping.\" This could be interpreted as: we build minimal logging early (which costs little) and full Article 12-compliant logging later. That's actually reasonable operational planning. But the way it's framed — as preventing \"premature logging constraints\" and protecting from \"industrial bookkeeping\" — suggests it's trying to AVOID logging obligations entirely at early stages, which is a compliance risk.\n\nI'll reject it. The resource cost of creating a formal classification policy, the governance overhead, and the regulatory misalignment outweigh the modest benefit (which could be achieved by simply noting in project plans \"full logging implementation begins at TRL X\"). The proposal over-engineers a solution to a problem that's mostly perceived rather than real at our scale.\n\nPromise score: I'd give it around 25-30. There's a kernel of valid operational thinking (don't build full production infrastructure for prototypes), but the formal policy apparatus, regulatory misalignment, and governance overhead make it net-negative from my resource-allocation perspective.\n",
