        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1351\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A standing audit function that verifies log integrity and documentation currency on a quarterly cadence maps directly onto Article 12's 'throughout the lifetime' obligation (Recital 71) and is cheaper than post-hoc remediation, but mandating that lead engineers serve as permanent gatekeepers over *all* R&D projects — including work unlikely to land in Annex III high-risk territory — imposes a recurring drag on the scarcest resource we have and creates a single bottleneck in the deployment pipeline. The standardized rubric is a genuine efficiency win, yet scoping the board's reach to projects actually tracking toward high-risk classification (rather than 'all R&D') would preserve scientific throughput at negligible additional compliance risk.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 63\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate proposal P1351 from the perspective of a Lab Director at an R&D institution whose domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n**P1351: High-Risk AI Compliance Audit Board**\n- A permanent cross-functional committee (legal experts + lead engineers)\n- Vets ALL R&D projects against EU risk categories\n- Final gatekeeper before deployment\n- Mandatory quarterly audits of system logs and documentation\n- Standardized scoring rubric based on Article 12 requirements\n- Goal: institutionalize oversight, protect market reputation, identify regulatory gaps\n\nNow let me assess this from my vantage point (resource-allocation efficiency, scientific ambition vs. funding constraints, effective use of existing R&D infrastructure):\n\n**Arguments for:**\n- Institutionalizing compliance oversight is sensible — without some structured mechanism, Article 12 compliance could be ad hoc and expensive to remediate later.\n- Quarterly audits of logs and documentation align directly with what the regulation requires (keeping records up-to-date throughout lifetime).\n- A standardized scoring rubric reduces ambiguity in what \"compliant\" means, which saves downstream time.\n- Catching regulatory gaps before deployment is cheaper than post-market penalties or remediation.\n\n**Arguments against (from my specific vantage point):**\n- \"Permanent cross-functional committee comprising legal experts and lead engineers\" — this is a significant recurring resource commitment. Lead engineers are the scarcest resource in an R&D lab. Pulling them into quarterly audit cycles pulls them away from research and development work. This directly impacts scientific ambition and throughput.\n- \"Vet ALL R&D projects against EU risk categories\" — this is a broad mandate that could create bureaucratic friction on every project, even those unlikely to be high-risk. The overhead of routing everything through a committee gate adds latency to the R&D pipeline.\n- \"Final gatekeeper before any system moves toward deployment\" — single-point-of-failure bottleneck. If the board is backlogged or has scheduling conflicts, deployments stall. This is inefficient use of infrastructure.\n- The proposal is somewhat generic and institutional in nature. It's a governance structure rather than a specific technical implementation. From my resource perspective, it commits ongoing personnel time without specifying how lean it could be. Could this be handled by existing compliance processes with a lighter touch?\n- However, the quarterly cadence of log audits is reasonable and aligns with the \"kept up to date throughout the lifetime\" requirement in Recital 71.\n- The standardized rubric is a good efficiency measure — it prevents each project from reinventing its compliance documentation approach.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this proposal has real merit in terms of institutionalizing what Article 12 requires (ongoing record-keeping, traceability), but the \"permanent committee\" and \"vet ALL R&D projects\" framing is resource-heavy. The key tension: it's a recurring cost on lead engineers' time that could be better spent on development. However, without some structured mechanism, compliance will either be neglected (risking penalties) or handled ad hoc (expensive when issues arise).\n\nThe quarterly audit of logs and documentation specifically maps to Article 12(1)-(3) requirements and Recital 71's \"kept up to date\" language. The standardized rubric is an efficiency win. But the \"gatekeeper before ANY system moves toward deployment\" is overbroad from a resource perspective — not every R&D project will become a high-risk AI system, and routing all through this board wastes engineer time on non-applicable reviews.\n\nI think this clears my bar but with reservations about scope. It's a reasonable governance structure that, if implemented leanly (not literally pulling senior engineers off projects for full-time committee work), addresses real compliance needs efficiently. The quarterly cadence is proportionate. The rubric standardizes effort. I'll approve it but note the resource concern, giving it a moderate-to-good promise score — maybe 62-68 range. It's useful but not transformative, and its resource cost is non-trivial.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, with a promise of around 63. It addresses a real need (institutionalized record-keeping verification per Art 12), the quarterly cadence is proportionate to the \"throughout lifetime\" requirement, and the rubric adds efficiency. But it's a recurring cost center on scarce engineering talent, and the \"ALL R&D projects\" scope is broader than strictly necessary for Article 12 compliance (which only applies to high-risk systems). It would be more resource-efficient if scoped to projects actually heading toward high-risk deployment rather than all R&D.\n\nI'll go with 63.\n",
