        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2202\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This middleware directly operationalises Article 12(1)'s core legal obligation—automatic, lifetime logging—by removing reliance on individual engineers to remember or implement bespoke logging, thereby eliminating the single most probable source of a compliance gap that would trigger enforcement under Articles 99/79. However, as scoped it standardises start/end times and input identifiers (mapping to Art. 12(3)(a)) but does not explicitly capture the reference-database record (12(3)(b)), match-specific input data (12(3)(c)), or the natural-person verifier identification required under 12(3)(d)/Art. 14(5), nor does it address log immutability, retention beyond system lifetime, or regulator access—gaps that a notified body or market-surveillance authority would flag during audit.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 83\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2202 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer whose domain is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal: \"Standardized Telemetry Middleware\" — a universal API wrapper that automatically captures inference events and system metadata for all high-risk models, standardizing recording of start times, end times, input identifiers, implemented as a middleware library in core Python frameworks intercepting model endpoint calls, writing logs to a centralized database.\n\nLet me assess this against Article 12 requirements:\n\n**Article 12(1):** \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\"\n- The middleware directly addresses this by providing automatic recording. It's a technical implementation that ensures logs are captured without relying on individual engineers remembering to add logging code. This is strong from a compliance standpoint — it reduces human error and omissions.\n\n**Article 12(2):** Logging capabilities shall enable recording of events relevant for:\n- (a) identifying situations presenting risk or substantial modification\n- (b) facilitating post-market monitoring\n- (c) monitoring operation per Article 26(5)\n- The middleware captures inference events and system metadata, which would support these purposes. However, the proposal focuses on \"start times, end times, and input identifiers\" — this is somewhat narrow compared to what 12(2) requires (identifying risk situations, substantial modifications). It's a foundation but may not be sufficient alone.\n\n**Article 12(3):** For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), minimum requirements include:\n- (a) start/end date and time of each use ✓ (covered by \"start times, end times\")\n- (b) reference database against which input data has been checked ✗ (not explicitly mentioned)\n- (c) input data for which the