sessment:**\nThis is fundamentally a good compliance infrastructure investment. The biggest regulatory risk under Article 12 is that engineers build new high-risk prototypes without proper logging, creating non-compliant systems that would fail notified body audits or face market surveillance issues. A standardized middleware layer dramatically reduces this risk. It's a force multiplier for compliance.\n\nHowever, it's not a complete solution — it doesn't cover all of 12(3) minimums explicitly, and there are gaps (log immutability, retention, access for regulators). But as a foundational layer, it's highly aligned with the regulation's intent.\n\n**Market reputation:** Having robust, standardized logging infrastructure demonstrates regulatory maturity to partners, customers, and market surveillance authorities. It signals that the institution takes compliance seriously at an architectural level rather than as an afterthought.\n\n**Institutional survival:** Without adequate logging, a single incident where logs are missing or inconsistent could lead to enforcement action, fines (up to 7% of global turnover under Article 99), or withdrawal of CE marking. This middleware is preventive infrastructure that protects against catastrophic compliance failures.\n\nMy assessment: This is a strong proposal from a compliance perspective. It addresses the core requirement directly, reduces systematic risk of non-compliance, and provides a foundation for more detailed logging requirements. The gaps I noted (12(3)(b)-(d) specifics, retention, immutability) are implementation details that can be addressed as the middleware matures, but the architectural decision to have a centralized, automatic, standardized logging layer is exactly what Article 12 demands.\n\nI would approve this. It's not perfect — it doesn't cover every sub-requirement explicitly — but it's the right foundational investment and addresses the most critical compliance risk (inconsistent or missing logs across model