isting code/metadata is genuinely useful for iteration speed. Every time I change a hyperparameter or swap a dataset version, the doc updates automatically rather than me manually tracking it.\n- The \"real-time\" aspect is technically achievable with hooks into CI/CD (GitHub Actions, GitLab CI) that trigger document regeneration on commits.\n- However, there's a risk of scope creep: building a full documentation generation pipeline is itself a non-trivial engineering project that could consume weeks/months of my time - time I'd rather spend on the actual AI system prototyping. The ROI depends heavily on how much documentation volume we're dealing with.\n\n**Critical assessment:**\n- The proposal is technically viable in its core (metadata extraction + template population), but it oversells by implying it \"generates the required technical documentation\" as if that's a solved problem. It can't replace human-authored risk management narratives, testing rationales, or limitation descriptions.\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, having automated metadata tracking is genuinely valuable - it reduces the chance of documentation drift (the doc says v2.1 dataset but the code uses v3.0). This is a real quality problem in fast-moving R&D.\n- The \"minimizes administrative burden on scientists\" framing is correct and aligned with what I'd want as an engineer: I don't want to spend 4 hours per sprint writing documentation when a script could do it.\n- Risk: if the toolchain itself becomes complex to maintain, it adds its own technical debt. But this is manageable - the extraction logic is straightforward (parse config files, read git log, query MLflow/Weights & Biases).\n\n**My decision:**\nThe core idea has genuine technical merit and directly serves my function's concerns (speed of iteration by reducing manual doc updates, quality by preventing metadata drift). It's not a panacea - it won't generate the full Article 12 documentation autonomously - but as an enabler tha