o theoretical framework being developed. The intellectual merit as a research endeavor is very low. It's a well-posed engineering task but not a research question.\n\n**Broader scientific impact:** Would this advance the field of AI science? No. A logging compliance library doesn't change how we understand neural networks, doesn't improve model performance, doesn't open new avenues of inquiry. Its impact is purely regulatory/operational. The broader scientific community would gain nothing from knowing that a particular lab built a unified logging API for compliance purposes. There's no publishable methodology, no generalizable insight about AI systems themselves.\n\nOne could argue indirectly: standardized logging might enable meta-research (analyzing patterns across many runs), but the proposal as stated doesn't aim at that. It's explicitly framed around \"regulatory audits\" and \"legal adherence,\" not research analytics or scientific discovery.\n\nThe proposal also has a somewhat naive framing: \"removes the manual burden of compliance from researchers while ensuring total legal adherence.\" This conflates engineering implementation with regulatory understanding, and \"total legal adherence\" is an overclaim — logging is necessary but not sufficient for compliance. But more importantly from my vantage point, this is simply not a research proposal.\n\nMy decision: reject. This is an infrastructure/engineering task with negligible intellectual merit as a research contribution and no advancement of the knowledge frontier or broader scientific impact. It's a reasonable thing to build (and presumably should be built by engineering teams), but it doesn't clear my bar as a researcher evaluating proposals for their scientific value.\n\nPromise score: I'd put this very low from a research perspective. Maybe 5-10. It's not harmful, and good logging infrastructure is necessary for any lab to function (including doing research), but it contributes essentially nothing to the