nce**: The Act requires logging to be technically functional. Having a verification mechanism is a proactive compliance measure that demonstrates due diligence to regulators. It's not explicitly required by Article 12, but it supports the requirement that systems \"technically allow for\" automatic recording — you need to verify they do.\n- **Market reputation**: If a high-risk AI system fails and we can't produce logs showing what happened, our credibility in the EU market is destroyed. This framework protects against that scenario.\n\nThe proposal is well-scoped: it's specifically about verifying Article 12 logging capabilities, uses synthetic failure scenarios (good practice), runs on deployment cycles (appropriate frequency), and addresses both risk events and modifications. It's not overreaching — it doesn't propose building the entire compliance infrastructure, just the verification layer for one specific article requirement.\n\nOne concern: \"automated audit scripts\" is somewhat vague in terms of scope. But as a proposal to commit resources to pursue, this is reasonable. The synthetic failure scenarios approach is sound — you want to test that logs capture edge cases, not just happy paths.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a focused, practical compliance control that directly supports our Article 12 obligations. From my vantage point, I would approve this.\n\nPromise score: This is highly relevant to my domain. It's not the most glamorous initiative, but it's exactly the kind of operational assurance work that prevents regulatory failures. I'd say around 78-82. It's strong but I'm slightly reserving because \"automated audit scripts\" could be under-scoped (what about log integrity, retention periods, format requirements?) or over-promised (synthetic failure scenarios covering ALL Article 12(3) minimum requirements is non-trivial). But the core idea is sound and directly serves compliance. I'll go with 80.\n",
