and fixing it.\n6. Scope limited to Annex III high-risk systems — appropriate scoping, not overreaching into non-applicable territory.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n1. Resource commitment: Quarterly across all Annex III systems could be significant, especially if the institution has many such systems. But the proposal says \"scarce implementation resources\" are the constraint. However, from a compliance perspective, this is not optional — you need to verify your logs work. The cost of non-compliance (fines up to 7% of global turnover or €35M under Article 99) far exceeds the cost of quarterly testing.\n2. \"Identify a specific risk event\" — the red team would need to know what they're looking for, which somewhat limits the test's value. But this is a practical constraint; you can't expect a blind search through all logs without some hypothesis. The article itself frames traceability around identifying specific situations (Art 12(2)(a)).\n3. This is an internal process, not a direct regulatory requirement per se — Article 12 requires the technical capability to log, but doesn't explicitly mandate periodic stress testing. However, Recital 71's emphasis on \"comprehensible information\" and enabling traceability supports this as a best-practice verification mechanism. Under the conformity assessment regime (Article 43) and QMS requirements (Article 17), demonstrating that your logging actually works is part of proving compliance.\n4. The proposal mentions \"protects our market reputation by proactively identifying traceability gaps before they are discovered by EU regulators\" — this is a valid compliance concern but also somewhat defensive. Still, from institutional survival perspective, being the one who finds the problem vs. the regulator finding it is a massive difference in regulatory relationship and penalty severity.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\nThis proposal directly supports compliance with Article 12 by verifying that the technical logging requirements are