        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2786 from my vantage point as a Researcher concerned with the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Automated Provenance Engine\" — a middleware layer that automatically maps data lineages from raw input to final output for every high-risk system run, using a versioned data lake integrated with training scripts to generate Article 12 compliant logs without manual researcher intervention.\n\nLet me assess this strictly through the lens of:\n- Knowledge frontier (does it advance what we know about AI systems?)\n- Intellectual merit of research (is there genuine scientific contribution here?)\n- Broader scientific impact (does it enable or constrain future research?)\n\nKey considerations from my vantage point:\n\n**Positive aspects:**\n- A versioned data lake with automatic lineage tracking could genuinely be a research infrastructure advance. Understanding how training data flows through a system and evolves over time is scientifically valuable — it enables reproducibility studies, ablation analyses, and understanding of how model behavior drifts as underlying data shifts.\n- Automatic provenance capture at the infrastructure level could enable meta-science: studying how AI systems evolve, what training configurations produce what capabilities, etc. This is genuinely at the knowledge frontier of AI science.\n- If well-designed, this infrastructure would support research into interpretability and robustness by providing clean, structured data about what went in and what came out.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- The proposal is framed primarily as a compliance tool (\"Article 12 compliant logs\"). This framing risks reducing it to an administrative overhead layer rather than treating it as a research asset. If the engine is designed solely to satisfy regulatory checkboxes, it may not capture the scientifically interesting information about