ragraphs are duplicated), which suggests the proposal wasn't carefully developed or reviewed. This is a minor red flag about the quality of thinking behind it.\n\n**Specific concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. The mapping problem: Article 12(3) requires very specific fields for biometric systems (period of use, reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons). A generic \"experimental metadata\" API won't naturally capture these without significant domain-specific instrumentation. You'd need per-system-type plugins or adapters, not just a blanket versioning integration.\n2. The proposal conflates research experiment tracking with production system logging. Article 12 is about the high-risk AI SYSTEM in operation (logs over its lifetime), not about R&D experiments. A provenance engine for research projects doesn't automatically give you runtime event logging for deployed systems. These are different technical challenges.\n3. \"Total reproducibility for every published result\" — this is a stretch goal that goes well beyond what Article 12 requires, and it's unclear whether the proposed mechanism (versioning API) actually achieves this in practice across heterogeneous research environments.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe idea has a kernel of good engineering thinking — reusing existing versioning infrastructure to reduce compliance overhead. But as stated, it conflates R&D experiment tracking with production system event logging (which is what Article 12 actually mandates), and the \"automatic mapping\" hand-waves over the hardest part (the domain-specific instrumentation for specific Annex III categories). From a prototyping speed perspective, this is pitched as a lab-wide platform project rather than something you can prototype quickly. The duplication in the text also suggests it wasn't fully thought through.\n\nI'd lean toward rejecting this: the core technical claim (that versioning tools + an API = Article 12 compliance) is oversimp