 better than per-team solutions.\n\n**Arguments against or concerns:**\n1. **Single point of failure / bottleneck**: If one team maintains this, delays in that team could block all compliance work across the lab. But this is manageable with good SLAs.\n2. **Upfront cost**: There's a non-trivial upfront investment to build the shared service before any project can use it. This creates a temporary drag on scientific output while the infrastructure is being built. However, this is a one-time cost vs. recurring per-team costs.\n3. **Flexibility concern**: A \"unified\" framework might not handle every edge case of different AI architectures. But the API-driven approach suggests some flexibility in what events are recorded.\n4. **Opportunity cost of engineering time**: The infrastructure team's time spent on this is time not spent on other R&D infrastructure improvements. But compliance is mandatory — we must do it either way, so the question is centralized vs. distributed, and centralized is clearly more efficient.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This proposal directly addresses my core concerns about resource-allocation efficiency and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure. It's a cost-saving measure that also reduces regulatory risk through consistency. The integration into existing compute clusters (rather than new hardware) shows good understanding of infrastructure constraints. The upfront engineering investment is justified because it replaces N separate investments with one. This is squarely in my wheelhouse and I'd strongly support it.\n\nThe main concern is the upfront cost and potential bottleneck, but these are minor compared to the alternative of fragmented per-team compliance tooling eating into research budgets.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score. It's very well-aligned with my vantage point. Maybe 85-90. I'll say 87 — it's excellent from an efficiency standpoint, but I'd want to see more detail on the maintenance model and how the shared serv