ure rather than replacing or fundamentally restructuring it. A sidecar/proxy pattern is an additive change, not a rip-and-replace.\n- It leverages the fact that models are already accessed via APIs across clusters — so intercepting at the API layer is natural to the existing architecture.\n- The \"transparent interceptor\" approach means no model code changes needed — minimal disruption to existing workflows.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- Building a unified sidecar for ALL high-risk models is ambitious. Different models may have different I/O formats, different latency profiles, different data sensitivity levels. A truly \"unified\" solution might require significant customization that undermines the \"build once\" efficiency argument.\n- The secure vault storage for all logs across all systems represents ongoing infrastructure cost (storage, access control, retention management) — this is a recurring cost, not just an up-front one.\n- However, compared to alternatives (each team building their own logging), this is clearly more resource-efficient at scale.\n\n**Does it clear my bar?**\nFrom my perspective on resource efficiency and infrastructure use: Yes. This is the most efficient architecture pattern for meeting Article 12's requirements across a multi-model R&D lab. It minimizes per-model implementation cost, leverages existing cluster topology, preserves research velocity, and the up-front investment is amortized across all high-risk systems. The decoupling argument is strong — it prevents compliance from becoming a recurring tax on every project team.\n\nThe main risk is that \"unified\" might be aspirational rather than achievable without significant per-model adaptation work, but even so, the sidecar pattern is more efficient than embedded logging.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score — maybe 82-85. It's well-aligned with my concerns about efficiency and infrastructure use. The slight deduction is for the \"unified across all\" ambitio