on (implementing Article 12 in our own systems). The \"collaborate with other EU research institutions\" language signals multi-party coordination overhead. While an open standard could eventually reduce per-system costs, the path to adoption is uncertain and lengthy, and we cannot defer our own compliance on the hope that a global standard will materialize.\n\nI'll reject this but give it a moderate promise score — it's not a terrible idea (maybe 40-50), it has long-term value, but from my specific vantage point of efficient resource allocation toward meeting Article 12 obligations with our existing infrastructure and funding constraints, it doesn't clear the bar. It's more of a \"nice to have after we're compliant\" or better yet, something a consortium or standards body (like CEN/CENELEC) should own rather than an individual R&D lab with finite resources.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 42.\n",
