ng rigor required for each milestone\" is practical — it recognizes that not all phases require the same depth.\n\n**Cons/Concerns:**\n- A standing review board has its own resource cost (people time, meeting cadence). If the lab is small, this could be disproportionate overhead. However, compared to the cost of building unnecessary logging infrastructure across all projects, this is likely net positive.\n- Risk of the classification being too narrow — if we misclassify a project as non-high-risk and it actually is, we face compliance failure. But the proposal says \"audits project scopes against Annex III criteria\" which suggests a formal process to mitigate this.\n- The protocol itself needs maintenance and updating as regulations evolve or as our projects evolve in scope. This is a living document cost.\n\nOverall assessment: From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is one of the most clearly value-add proposals I could receive. It's essentially saying \"before we spend scarce engineering hours building logging infrastructure, let's confirm we actually need to.\" This is basic fiscal prudence applied to compliance. The review board adds governance rigor without being overly bureaucratic for a single protocol. The milestone-based approach to logging depth is sensible — you don't need full Annex III logging during early prototyping if the project hasn't yet crossed into high-risk territory.\n\nThe main risk is execution: if the review board becomes a bottleneck that slows down research, it could hurt scientific agility rather than protect it. But as described, it's positioned as a gate before resource allocation, not a recurring approval process for every experiment. That's reasonable.\n\nI'd rate this highly from my vantage point. It directly addresses the core tension I manage: how to meet regulatory obligations without bleeding resources on compliance infrastructure that isn't legally required. The \"before full-scale resource allocation\" framing