p my model behind the standard API interface, logging happens automatically. That's good. The alternative — having each researcher manually implement Article 12 logging — would be a massive speed killer and quality risk (inconsistent implementations).\n\nHowever, there's a risk: if the framework is too rigid or has high overhead, it could slow down prototyping. A well-designed middleware with minimal latency impact should be fine, but this needs to be validated in practice. Also, debugging issues in the logging layer itself can waste time.\n\n**Verdict:**\nThe proposal is technically viable as a foundational component. It addresses a real pain point (standardization across architectures) and aligns with prototyping speed goals by automating capture at boundaries. The main technical risk is coverage gaps for non-standard execution paths, and the \"input hashes\" detail may not fully satisfy Article 12(3)(c)'s requirement for actual input data recording. But as a \"baseline compliance\" framework that handles the common cases automatically, it's a sound engineering investment.\n\nI'd approve this — it's technically feasible, well-motivated from a development perspective, and directly serves the prototyping speed goal by removing per-project logging implementation work. The promise score should reflect high confidence but not perfection given the coverage concerns. I'd say around 78-82. Let me settle on 80.\n",
