ons and immutable audit log support traceability requirements\n- Addresses the \"clear and comprehensive form\" requirement from Recital 71\n- The electronic sign-off workflow creates a clear record chain\n- Lightweight design suggests feasible implementation within resource constraints\n\nConcerns:\n- It only addresses one specific sub-provision (12(3)(d)) - not the full scope of Article 12\n- \"Lightweight web portal\" - need to ensure it's robust enough for regulatory inspection; EU authorities may demand more than a simple approve/reject button\n- The proposal assumes our system falls under Annex III point 1(a) - if we have multiple high-risk systems, this is only partial compliance\n- \"Immutable audit log\" - good in principle, but the implementation details matter for regulatory acceptance (retention periods, format requirements, accessibility to regulators)\n- Article 72 post-market monitoring and Article 26(5) operation monitoring are referenced in 12(2)(b) and (c) but this proposal doesn't explicitly address those\n- The \"streamlined technical workflow\" framing is good for operational adoption but I need to ensure it meets the \"appropriate to the intended purpose\" standard in 12(2)\n\nFrom a compliance officer's perspective:\n- This directly supports a specific, verifiable regulatory requirement (12(3)(d))\n- SSO + timestamps + immutable log = good audit trail for regulatory inspections\n- Market reputation: having a clean human verification workflow demonstrates responsible AI governance to clients and regulators\n- Institutional survival: non-compliance with Article 12 carries fines; this reduces that risk for the specific provision it addresses\n- However, it's narrow in scope - only covers one aspect of record-keeping\n\nThe proposal is well-targeted at a specific compliance gap. It's practical, implementable, and directly maps to a regulatory text requirement. As a compliance officer, I'd want this to exist. The \"lightweight\" framing is actually