er to build it now or later.\n- \"Immutable storage\" adds operational complexity and cost (you can't just delete old data). Retention periods under the Act could be long.\n- The proposal is somewhat generic S3 architecture talk. It doesn't specify retention policies, access controls, or how it integrates with existing lab infrastructure. But those might be implementation details rather than deal-breakers at the screening stage.\n- From a resource allocation standpoint: building a centralized system vs. each project team handling their own logging — centralization is more efficient for maintenance and audit, but requires dedicated engineering resources to build and maintain.\n- The \"automated indexing\" and \"fast retrieval\" aspects add complexity and ongoing cost.\n\n**My assessment as Lab Director focused on resource efficiency:**\n\nThis is a reasonable infrastructure investment IF we are actually building high-risk AI systems that fall under Annex III (point 1(a) specifically). It's not speculative — it addresses a concrete regulatory requirement. The S3-based approach is the most cost-effective path to this capability. Asynchronous writing protects our research compute resources. Centralization avoids duplicative efforts across projects.\n\nHowever, I should note:\n- It's an infrastructure commitment that will consume engineering time (building) and ongoing operations budget (maintenance, storage costs).\n- The proposal doesn't address phasing — do we build the full system now or start with a minimal viable version?\n- \"Immutable\" storage has cost implications for long retention.\n\nOn balance: This is a necessary investment if we're pursuing high-risk AI systems. It's not scientific ambition — it's compliance infrastructure. The S3 architecture is appropriately lean (not over-engineered). It directly serves resource efficiency by centralizing, preventing project-level bloat, and keeping research systems fast. I'd approve this but note the cost/complexity considerations.\n\nPromise score: From a pure resource-efficiency standpoint, this is well-aligned. It's not flashy R&D but it's operationally necessary and the proposed architecture (S3, async) is appropriately lean. I'd put this around 72-78. It's solid infrastructure thinking, cost-effective approach, but it IS a real commitment of engineering resources that could be directed at actual research if we weren't building high-risk systems. Since we ARE (that's the premise), I'll go with about 75.\n\nLet me settle on my decision: Approve. The architecture is appropriately scoped, uses cost-effective technology, protects R&D throughput, and centralizes what would otherwise be duplicated across projects. It's not over-engineered.\n",
