d study is methodologically strong — it captures temporal dynamics that cross-sectional studies miss. This is genuinely better science than one-off evaluations.\n- The \"secure telemetry loop\" between deployment and R&D lab is a concrete, well-scoped mechanism for data collection.\n- Using findings to \"iteratively refine model safety constraints\" closes the research-to-practice loop, which adds intellectual coherence.\n- However, there are some concerns: What specific hypotheses would be tested? The proposal is somewhat vague on the scientific questions. It says \"analyze how high-risk systems drift or fail\" — this is broad. A strong research proposal would have more specific research questions and methodologies for analysis.\n- The claim that it's \"impossible to generate in isolated laboratory settings\" is somewhat overstated — some aspects of drift (distribution shift, concept drift) can be simulated in lab settings. But the full ecosystem of interactions with real users, real data pipelines, real operational conditions is indeed not replicable.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- If executed well, this could produce genuinely novel empirical evidence about AI system behavior that no one else has access to. The EU AI Act creates a unique regulatory environment where these logs are mandated and standardized — this is actually a rare opportunity for comparative studies across systems.\n- Could contribute to the broader field of AI reliability, safety, and robustness research.\n- However, there's a question of whether findings would be publishable/shareable given that they involve specific deployments (potentially with PII or proprietary data). The \"secure telemetry loop\" language suggests internal use rather than open science. This limits broader scientific impact somewhat.\n- If the lab is one of many developing high-risk AI systems under EU AI Act, the longitudinal data could be part of a larger collaborative effort, but the proposal as stated seems to be