iant documentation. But building such a tool is technically straightforward.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + prototyping speed/quality), this is a highly viable, well-scoped engineering project. It leverages existing infrastructure patterns (experiment tracking, templated report generation, event-triggered workflows). The technical risk is low. The prototyping speed benefit is real - automating documentation removes a manual bottleneck from the iteration cycle. \n\nThe main caveat I'd note: the \"every checkpoint\" trigger during rapid prototyping could generate noise, and the tool's coverage of Article 12 seems to focus on development-phase records rather than runtime event logging. But as stated, the technical solution is sound and would genuinely accelerate our work.\n\nI'd rate this fairly high on promise from my perspective. It's a practical, buildable solution that directly addresses a friction point in prototyping. I'd say around 82-85. It's not a 95+ because there are some scope ambiguities (does it truly cover the full Article 12 requirements or just development metadata?) and the \"every checkpoint\" trigger needs refinement for practical use during rapid iteration. But it's clearly above my bar for approval from a technical viability standpoint.\n\nDecision: approve\nPromise: 83\n",
