igh-risk AI pipelines.\" This assumes adoption. Compliance is about ensuring things actually happen, not just creating tools. But that's an implementation detail — the framework itself is a necessary prerequisite.\n- It doesn't explicitly mention retention periods or how logs are stored/protected (though those may be in other articles).\n- It focuses on the technical logging mechanism but doesn't address who reviews the logs, how they feed into post-market monitoring workflows, etc. However, Article 12 is specifically about the *technical capability* for automatic recording — the framework addresses exactly that.\n- The proposal says \"ensures technical compliance with Article 12\" — this is slightly overconfident because a library alone doesn't guarantee compliance (it must actually be used correctly), but as an infrastructure investment, it's clearly aligned.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a highly relevant and well-targeted proposal for my function. It directly addresses the core technical requirement of Article 12(1) — that systems \"technically allow for\" automatic logging. Standardization across projects reduces compliance risk (no gaps, no inconsistent formats). It's a scalable investment rather than repeated ad-hoc work per project. From an institutional survival standpoint, having a single auditable logging standard is far stronger than N different implementations.\n\nThe main limitation is that it's a tool/framework — it doesn't by itself guarantee compliance (adoption, correct configuration, retention policies, etc.). But as a foundational infrastructure piece, it's exactly what a compliance officer would want to see in place. It's not sufficient alone, but it's necessary and clearly aligned with the regulation's text.\n\nI'll approve this. Promise score: This is a strong alignment with my function's needs. It addresses the core technical requirement directly, reduces institutional risk through standardization, and supports audit readiness. I'd say around 8